insert into public.questions
  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation)
values
  ($q$ei-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Rising ocean temperatures cause corals to expel the algae that live in their tissues and supply most of their food. Without the algae, corals turn white and begin to starve. ______ if temperatures return to normal within a few weeks, the algae can recolonize the coral, and it may recover.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","However,","In other words,","Furthermore,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The last sentence introduces a hopeful exception that contrasts with the grim consequences just described. "However" signals this contrast. "Therefore" signals a result, "In other words" a restatement, and "Furthermore" an additional similar point.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In her early career, the photographer worked exclusively in black and white, arguing that color distracted viewers from form and light. ______ she began using color film in the 1970s, she largely abandoned black and white and never returned to it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Once","Although","Unless","Whereas"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence describes what happened after she began using color: she stopped using black and white. "Once" signals that one event happened and the other followed from that point on. "Although" would set up a contrast that the clauses don't support, "Unless" sets up a condition, and "Whereas" sets up a comparison.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The library's new policy allows patrons to borrow tools, musical instruments, and even fishing rods. ______ the most popular item in the collection, according to the head librarian, is a high-quality telescope that has a waiting list of more than two hundred people.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","Notably,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence highlights an especially interesting detail about the new collection. "Notably" signals that a remarkable point is being drawn out. There is no cause-and-effect, contrast, or similarity relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$If the river's water level drops below a certain point, the cooling system at the power plant cannot function. The plant's operators must therefore reduce its output during droughts; ______ they risk damaging the reactor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["similarly,","for instance,","otherwise,","meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The final clause describes what would happen if the operators did not reduce output. "Otherwise" signals this alternative, undesirable consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The heavy clay soil in the valley holds water well, which allows crops to survive long dry spells. ______ it drains poorly during wet years, and fields can remain waterlogged for weeks after a storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","At the same time,","For instance,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence presents a drawback of the same property (holding water) that is described as a benefit in the first. "At the same time" introduces a qualifying, contrasting consideration about the same soil. The other choices signal cause, example, or summary.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violinist rehearsed the concerto for months, but on the night of the premiere a string snapped during the first movement. ______ she borrowed the concertmaster's instrument and finished the performance without missing more than a few bars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Undeterred,","Consequently,","In contrast,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes the violinist continuing despite the mishap. "Undeterred" signals that she was not stopped by the setback. "Consequently" would suggest the snapped string caused her to finish, which is illogical; "Previously" refers to an earlier time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most people assume that the brain is fully developed by adolescence. ______ research using brain scans shows that the prefrontal cortex, which governs planning and impulse control, continues to mature until the mid-twenties.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Thus,","In addition,","In fact,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the common assumption presented in the first. "In fact" signals that what follows contradicts or corrects a previous statement. "Similarly" and "In addition" signal agreement, and "Thus" signals a result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The engineers first tested the new battery design in a laboratory at room temperature, where it held its charge for more than a thousand cycles. ______ they tested it in freezing conditions, where its capacity fell by nearly half after just a hundred cycles.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","In other words,","Subsequently,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes a later step in the testing process. "Subsequently" signals that this test came next in time. The other choices signal contrast, restatement, or similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city council approved funding for the new bike lanes in 2016. ______ construction stalled for three years while residents argued about whether the lanes should replace parking spaces or traffic lanes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","However,","Similarly,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes a delay that contrasts with the approval described in the first. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The fossil's teeth are flat and ridged, well suited to grinding tough plant material. ______ its jaw lacks the sharp, blade-like teeth that would have allowed it to slice through flesh. Paleontologists therefore conclude that the animal was almost certainly an herbivore.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","However,","Instead,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds a further piece of evidence that supports the same conclusion (that the animal ate plants). "Moreover" signals additional support. "However" would signal contrast, but the lack of slicing teeth is consistent with the flat teeth.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In the 1800s, many doctors believed that diseases such as cholera were caused by "bad air" rising from rotting matter. ______ they focused their efforts on eliminating foul smells rather than on purifying drinking water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","In contrast,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The doctors' focus on smells was a result of their belief in "bad air." "Consequently" signals this cause-and-effect relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The bridge was designed to sway slightly in strong winds, a feature intended to keep it from cracking under stress. On the day it opened, ______ thousands of pedestrians walking in step caused it to sway so much that it had to be closed for nearly two years of modifications.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["therefore,","in addition,","however,","for example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The opening-day events show the swaying feature backfiring, which contrasts with its intended purpose. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The author spent ten years researching the novel, traveling to archives in four countries and interviewing dozens of descendants of the people she wrote about. ______ the book that emerged is unusually rich in historical detail, from the price of bread in 1840s Dublin to the cut of a ship captain's coat.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","Unsurprisingly,","Meanwhile,","Regardless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Given the extensive research described in the first sentence, it is expected that the book would be rich in detail. "Unsurprisingly" signals that the outcome follows naturally from what came before.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Older adults who regularly learn new skills, such as playing an instrument or speaking a foreign language, tend to show slower cognitive decline than those who do not. ______ the researchers caution that their study shows only a correlation; it may be that people with healthier brains are simply more likely to take on new challenges.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Thus,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence qualifies the finding described in the first, warning against reading too much into it. "Even so" signals that what follows limits or complicates the preceding point. "Thus" signals a result, "For instance" an example, and "Likewise" a similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ice core contains layers that correspond to individual years, much like the rings of a tree. Each layer also preserves tiny bubbles of air from the time it formed. ______ by analyzing these bubbles, scientists can measure how much carbon dioxide was in the atmosphere hundreds of thousands of years ago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","On the other hand,","Similarly,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The final sentence describes what scientists can do because the layers preserve ancient air. "Thus" signals this consequence. The other choices signal contrast or similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The architect designed the library to use as little energy as possible. Its walls are made of thick rammed earth that stays cool in summer and warm in winter. ______ its roof is angled to capture rainwater, which is filtered and used to flush the building's toilets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","Additionally,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The final sentence presents another feature that serves the same goal (efficiency). "Additionally" signals the addition of a parallel point.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage leads businesses to hire fewer workers. ______ others point to studies of neighboring states with different minimum wages that found little or no difference in employment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Conversely,","Similarly,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence presents an opposing view to that in the first. "Conversely" signals contrasting positions. "Therefore" signals a result, "Similarly" agreement, and "In particular" a specific example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new varieties of rice produce far more grain than traditional varieties, but only when they receive large amounts of fertilizer and water. ______ for farmers who cannot afford these inputs, the traditional varieties often remain the better choice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final sentence draws a conclusion based on the condition described in the previous sentence. "Thus" signals this logical consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The team expected that the newly discovered planet, which orbits very close to its star, would have no atmosphere at all. ______ measurements from the space telescope revealed a thin layer of gas containing water vapor and carbon dioxide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Likewise,","Moreover,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The measurements revealed the opposite of what was expected. "Instead" signals that something replaced the expected outcome. "Accordingly" would signal a result consistent with expectations.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Honeybees communicate the location of food through a "waggle dance": the angle of the dance indicates the direction of the food relative to the Sun, and the length of the waggle indicates the distance. ______ a bee that waggles for one second is signaling food roughly a kilometer away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","Specifically,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The final sentence gives a precise detail about how waggle length encodes distance, which elaborates on the previous sentence. "Specifically" signals this kind of detail.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra had performed in the same concert hall for more than a century. When engineers discovered that the building's foundation was sinking, the musicians were forced to move to a converted warehouse across town. ______ the warehouse's acoustics proved so good that the orchestra decided to stay there permanently.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Surprisingly,","Similarly,","In summary,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It is unexpected that a temporary, converted warehouse would have acoustics good enough to replace a century-old hall. "Surprisingly" signals this unexpected outcome. "Consequently" would wrongly imply that the sinking foundation caused the good acoustics.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Because ants follow chemical trails left by other ants, a colony can quickly locate and exploit a new food source. ______ the same system can sometimes trap ants in a "death spiral": if the trail forms a loop, ants may follow one another in a circle until they die of exhaustion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ironically,","Accordingly,","Likewise,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The same mechanism that makes ants efficient can also lead to their death, an outcome that runs contrary to the system's usual benefit. "Ironically" signals this unexpected reversal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The board of the museum voted to return the sculpture to the country from which it had been taken in the 1890s. ______ the museum will display a high-resolution replica, along with a new exhibit explaining the sculpture's history and how it came to be in the collection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","On the contrary,","Similarly,","In its place,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The replica will occupy the position formerly held by the returned sculpture. "In its place" signals this substitution precisely. "On the contrary" signals a contradiction, and "Similarly" a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The language was spoken by fewer than fifty people in 1990, nearly all of them over the age of seventy. ______ thanks to a program that pairs fluent elders with young learners, more than five hundred people now speak it, including several dozen children who learned it as their first language.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Furthermore,","As a result,","Today, however,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence contrasts the language's dire situation in 1990 with its current revival. "Today, however" signals both the change in time and the contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The study's authors note that their sample included only college students in a single country. ______ they caution against assuming that their findings would apply to older adults or to people in other cultures.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","For this reason,","In contrast,","By the same token,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The caution described in the second sentence follows from the limitation noted in the first. "For this reason" signals this causal link. "Nevertheless" signals contrast, and "By the same token" introduces a parallel argument.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Earlier models of the island's climate predicted that rainfall would decline steadily over the century. ______ newer models, which account for changes in ocean currents, predict that rainfall will remain roughly stable but will arrive in fewer, more intense storms.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","Accordingly,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The newer models make a different prediction from the earlier ones. "By contrast" signals this difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The writer is best known for her novels, which have been translated into more than forty languages. ______ she considers herself primarily a poet and has said that she writes novels only "to pay for the poetry."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For instance,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The writer's view of herself as primarily a poet contrasts with her fame as a novelist. "Nevertheless" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The plant's seeds can remain dormant in the soil for decades. They germinate only after a fire, when heat cracks their hard outer coats and smoke chemicals signal that competing vegetation has been cleared. ______ in areas where fires have been suppressed for many years, the species has nearly disappeared.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Similarly,","Consequently,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because the seeds need fire to germinate, suppressing fires prevents new plants from growing. The near-disappearance is a result of the conditions described, so "Consequently" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Composer Florence Price wrote more than three hundred works, including four symphonies. ______ after her death in 1953, much of her music was forgotten, and dozens of her manuscripts were rediscovered only in 2009, in an abandoned house in Illinois.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","However,","Thus,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The neglect of her music after her death contrasts with the impressive body of work described in the first sentence. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Glaciologists once thought that the massive ice shelf was stable, anchored in place by an underwater ridge. ______ satellite images taken over the past decade show that warm ocean water has been seeping beneath the shelf and melting it from below, causing the shelf to thin by several meters each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Recently, however,","Likewise,","As a result,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence presents new evidence that contradicts the earlier belief. "Recently, however" signals both the shift in time and the contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first edition of the dictionary, published in 1828, contained about seventy thousand entries. ______ the most recent edition contains more than four hundred thousand, reflecting both the growth of the language and the addition of technical and scientific vocabulary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","For instance,","Even so,","By comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence compares the size of the most recent edition with that of the first. "By comparison" signals this contrast in scale.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$To reduce the number of birds killed by collisions with its glass towers, the city now requires new buildings to use patterned glass that birds can see. ______ it offers tax credits to owners of older buildings who apply special films to their windows.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Nevertheless,","In addition,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes a further measure the city takes toward the same goal. "In addition" signals this. "Instead" would imply that the tax credits replace the requirement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The chess program was not taught any strategy by its designers; it learned only the rules of the game. ______ by playing millions of games against itself, it developed strategies that grandmasters described as alien yet brilliant.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Nonetheless,","In other words,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It is surprising that a program given only the rules developed brilliant strategies. "Nonetheless" signals that this outcome occurred despite the limited starting knowledge. "Therefore" would imply that knowing only the rules caused brilliance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The sea otter is sometimes called a keystone species because its presence has an outsized effect on its ecosystem. Otters eat sea urchins, which graze on kelp. ______ where otters are abundant, urchin populations stay small, and kelp forests flourish, providing habitat for hundreds of other species.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","However,","Similarly,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final sentence describes the consequence of the feeding relationships just explained. "Thus" signals this conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most of the comet's surface is dark, reflecting only about four percent of the sunlight that strikes it. ______ a few small patches near its south pole are bright white, which scientists believe indicates exposed water ice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Moreover,","As such,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The bright patches contrast with the comet's mostly dark surface. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The trial was designed to last five years. ______ the drug's benefits were so clear after only two years that the review board stopped the study early and offered the drug to participants who had been receiving the placebo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Likewise,","In the end,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence explains how the trial actually concluded, contrary to the original plan. "In the end" signals the eventual outcome. "As a result" wrongly suggests that the five-year design caused the early stop.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The museum's collection of ancient coins is one of the largest in the world. ______ only a small fraction of it is on display; the rest is stored in climate-controlled vaults and is available only to researchers by appointment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Yet","Moreover,","Indeed,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The fact that only a small fraction is displayed contrasts with the size of the collection. "Yet" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new law requires that all packaging sold in the country be either recyclable or compostable by 2030. ______ manufacturers will have to redesign many common products, from potato-chip bags to toothpaste tubes, which currently combine several materials that cannot be separated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","On the other hand,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The redesign of products is a consequence of the new law's requirement. "As a result" signals this effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that bats are blind. ______ all bat species can see, and some, such as the large fruit bats of Asia and Africa, have eyesight that is sharper than that of humans in dim light.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Likewise,","Additionally,","In reality,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the false assumption in the first. "In reality" signals that what follows is the truth, in contrast to a misconception.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The painter worked slowly, often spending a year or more on a single canvas. ______ she produced fewer than sixty paintings during a career that lasted nearly half a century.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","In contrast,","Accordingly,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Her small total output follows from her slow working method. "Accordingly" signals a logical consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In the experiment, one group of mice was given access to an exercise wheel, while a second group was not. ______ the mice with wheels grew nearly twice as many new neurons in the hippocampus, a brain region associated with memory.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","After four weeks,","Nevertheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence reports the result of the experiment after it had run for some time. "After four weeks" signals the passage of time leading to the result. The other choices signal similarity, concession, or an example, none of which fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violin's tone depends in part on the density of its wood. ______ violins made from spruce that grew slowly during the unusually cold decades of the late 1600s—including many of Stradivari's—may owe some of their prized sound to the climate in which their trees grew.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Instead,","However,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence draws an inference based on the principle in the first: if density matters, then wood from slow-growing trees (denser) may explain the sound. "Thus" signals this inference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Early reviewers dismissed the band's first album as noisy and unfocused. ______ within a decade, many of the same critics were calling it one of the most influential records of its era.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Moreover,","Similarly,","Yet"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The later praise contrasts with the early dismissal. "Yet" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most metals, gallium melts at a temperature just above 29°C (84°F). ______ a solid piece of gallium will melt if a person holds it in the palm of their hand for a few minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Previously,","Consequently,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because gallium melts at a temperature below that of the human body, holding it will melt it. "Consequently" signals this result. "However" signals a contrast, "Previously" an earlier time, and "Similarly" a parallel case.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The village's terraced rice fields were carved into the mountainside more than two thousand years ago and are still farmed today. ______ as young people move to cities for work, fewer families are left to maintain the stone walls, and some terraces have begun to collapse.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Increasingly, though,","Similarly,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes a growing threat that contrasts with the long continuity described in the first. "Increasingly, though" signals both the contrast and the trend.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor rarely discusses the meaning of her work; she prefers to let viewers draw their own conclusions. ______ when a critic asked her what her towering steel spiral represented, she replied only, "Whatever you need it to."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Consequently,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her reply to the critic illustrates her habit of not explaining her work. "For example" signals this illustration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many of the region's farmers have switched from growing corn to growing sorghum. Sorghum requires roughly a third less water than corn, ______ it can survive the droughts that have become more frequent in recent years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["but","or","yet","and"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second clause adds a related advantage of sorghum to the first. "And" joins two parallel benefits. "But" and "yet" signal contrast, and "or" signals an alternative.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The theory predicted that the particle would decay in about a billionth of a second. ______ when physicists finally detected it, the particle lasted almost exactly as long as predicted, providing strong support for the theory.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","However,","Sure enough,","Conversely,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The detection confirmed the prediction. "Sure enough" signals that an outcome matched expectations. The other choices signal contrast or replacement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city's subway system was built in stages over more than a century. ______ its stations reflect a wide range of architectural styles, from ornate tiled halls of the 1900s to the bare concrete platforms of the 1970s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","In contrast,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The variety of styles results from construction over a long period. "As a result" signals this cause-and-effect relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The researchers asked volunteers to estimate the number of jelly beans in a jar. Individual guesses varied wildly, some too high by hundreds and others too low by hundreds. ______ the average of all the guesses came within a few beans of the true number.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably, though,","Therefore,","Likewise,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$It is surprising that wildly varying guesses produced an accurate average. "Remarkably, though" signals both the surprise and the contrast with the scattered individual guesses.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The poet's early work drew heavily on the forms of English Romanticism. ______ her mature work abandons rhyme and meter almost entirely, drawing instead on the rhythms of the Yoruba praise songs she heard as a child.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Similarly,","Indeed,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The mature work is described as different from the early work. "In contrast" signals this difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The insect's wings are covered in tiny spikes that are too small to see with the naked eye. ______ the spikes act like a bed of nails, puncturing the cell walls of any bacteria that land on the wing and killing them within minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Although the spikes are too small to see, they are still powerful enough to kill bacteria. "Nevertheless" signals that the second sentence is true despite what the first might suggest. "Consequently" would wrongly suggest that the spikes' small size causes their effect, and "Similarly" and "Instead" do not fit.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying the island's lizards introduced a predator, a larger ground-dwelling lizard, to six of twelve small islands. Within months, lizards on those six islands began spending more time high in the trees. ______ within a few generations, they had evolved stickier toe pads, which helped them cling to branches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Moreover,","Conversely,","Regardless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The final sentence adds a further, related change in the lizards' response to predators. "Moreover" signals this additional point that builds on the previous one.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city installed sensors on its streetlights to track traffic and air quality. ______ the data revealed that one intersection near an elementary school had pollution levels nearly double the city average during the morning drop-off period.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Within weeks,","In contrast,","Likewise,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reports what the data showed shortly after the sensors were installed. "Within weeks" signals the timing. No contrast or similarity is being drawn.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Traditional economic models assume that people make decisions rationally, weighing costs and benefits. ______ behavioral economists have documented many ways in which people systematically depart from this ideal, such as valuing an item more highly simply because they already own it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For instance,","Similarly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence presents findings that challenge the assumption in the first. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Maya developed a sophisticated calendar system. ______ they tracked a 260-day ritual cycle and a 365-day solar year simultaneously, and the two cycles realigned only once every fifty-two years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Specifically,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence provides details about the calendar system mentioned in the first. "Specifically" signals this elaboration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The museum's new director wanted to attract younger visitors. ______ she extended the museum's hours on Friday nights, added live music, and invited local artists to lead drop-in workshops.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","To this end,","Similarly,","In comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The actions described in the second sentence were taken to achieve the goal stated in the first. "To this end" signals actions taken for a purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Tomatoes were introduced to Europe from the Americas in the 1500s. For nearly two centuries, ______ many Europeans grew them only as ornamental plants, believing the fruit to be poisonous.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["however,","therefore,","for example,","similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Growing tomatoes only as ornaments, out of fear, contrasts with the expectation that an introduced food plant would be eaten. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some species of fish can change sex during their lifetimes. Clownfish, ______ all begin life as males; when the dominant female of a group dies, the largest male becomes female and takes her place.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["however,","therefore,","likewise,","for example,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Clownfish are given as an instance of fish that change sex. "For example" signals this illustration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first version of the software was praised for its speed but criticized for being difficult to use. ______ the developers spent the next year redesigning its interface, adding tutorials, and simplifying its menus.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Similarly,","In response,","Even so,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The redesign was a reaction to the criticism described in the first sentence. "In response" signals this relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Researchers once believed that adult brains could not grow new neurons. Studies in the 1990s, ______ found evidence of new neurons forming in the hippocampus of adult humans.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["therefore,","however,","likewise,","for instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The studies contradicted the earlier belief. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The river carries enormous amounts of sediment from the mountains to the sea. Where the river meets the ocean, the current slows and the sediment settles. ______ over thousands of years, this process has built a delta covering more than ten thousand square kilometers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Gradually,","Conversely,","Nonetheless,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The delta was built slowly as sediment accumulated over time. "Gradually" signals this slow process.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many of the novelist's readers assumed that her books were autobiographical. ______ she had never visited the remote Alaskan villages where most of them are set and based her descriptions entirely on archival research and interviews.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Additionally,","Similarly,","Actually,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the readers' assumption. "Actually" signals a correction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The company's profits fell for three consecutive quarters. ______ its stock price rose steadily during the same period, as investors grew optimistic about a new product the company was developing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Likewise,","Paradoxically,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A rising stock price during falling profits is an apparent contradiction. "Paradoxically" signals this seemingly contradictory situation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first photograph of a black hole, released in 2019, required data from eight telescopes on four continents. ______ the telescopes functioned as a single instrument with a dish as wide as Earth itself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Together,","Nevertheless,","Conversely,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes how the telescopes worked jointly. "Together" signals that they combined to act as one.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The library had planned to digitize its entire collection of historical newspapers within five years. ______ budget cuts have slowed the project, and at the current pace, it will take more than fifteen years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","Moreover,","In fact,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The slowdown contrasts with the original plan. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Wolves were reintroduced to the national park in 1995 after an absence of nearly seventy years. ______ the elk population, which had grown large in the wolves' absence, declined sharply, and willows and aspens along the riverbanks began to recover from decades of overgrazing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For instance,","Afterward,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The changes in elk and vegetation followed the wolves' reintroduction. "Afterward" signals this sequence in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's technique was nearly flawless. ______ critics complained that her performances lacked emotion and sounded more like demonstrations of skill than expressions of the music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Similarly,","Even so,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The critics' complaints arise despite her flawless technique. "Even so" signals this concession.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new drug was effective at reducing blood pressure in clinical trials. ______ it caused dizziness in about one in five patients, a side effect that limited its use among older adults, who are more likely to fall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Unfortunately,","Likewise,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The side effect is an undesirable aspect that contrasts with the drug's effectiveness. "Unfortunately" signals this negative development.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The mountain's glaciers have shrunk by more than half since 1900. ______ the communities in the valley below, which rely on glacial meltwater during the dry season, face growing water shortages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Water shortages are a consequence of the shrinking glaciers. "As a result" signals this.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The language spoken on the island has only three vowel sounds. ______ English, depending on the dialect, has as many as twenty.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Moreover,","In other words,","By contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence contrasts the number of vowel sounds in English with those of the island's language. "By contrast" signals this difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The architect insisted that the building's windows be made from glass produced using traditional methods. ______ she had panes manufactured by hand in a small workshop in Germany, at nearly ten times the cost of modern glass.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","Accordingly,","In comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Having the panes made by hand follows from her insistence on traditional glass. "Accordingly" signals this consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$For most of the twentieth century, scientists thought that the giant squid was too elusive ever to be filmed in its natural habitat. ______ in 2012, a team of researchers using a submersible with a special light that the squid could not see finally captured footage of one at a depth of about 600 meters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Then,","Similarly,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes a later event that broke the long-standing belief. "Then" signals this turn in the sequence of events.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The scientists did not expect that the bacteria would survive the experiment's extreme conditions. ______ the bacteria not only survived but continued to reproduce.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["To their surprise,","As expected,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bacteria's survival contradicted the scientists' expectations. "To their surprise" signals this unexpected outcome.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The trail was closed for most of the winter because of avalanche danger. ______ it reopened in May, after rangers had cleared fallen trees and repaired a damaged footbridge.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Conversely,","In other words,","Eventually,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The reopening came after a period of closure and repairs. "Eventually" signals that the event occurred after a delay.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most species of sharks must keep swimming to force water over their gills. The nurse shark, ______ can pump water over its gills while lying motionless on the sea floor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["for example,","therefore,","by contrast,","in addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The nurse shark is presented as an exception to the general rule. "By contrast" signals the difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The early printing press made books far cheaper to produce. ______ literacy rates in much of Europe rose significantly over the following two centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","In turn,","Nevertheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Cheaper books contributed to rising literacy; the second development followed from the first. "In turn" signals this chain of effects.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The candidate promised to lower taxes for working families. ______ she proposed raising the tax rate on incomes above one million dollars to pay for the cuts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","Instead,","Consequently,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a complementary proposal made alongside the first. "At the same time" signals two things done together. "Instead" would imply that she abandoned the tax-cut promise.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ice on the lake usually freezes solid by late December. ______ this year, unusually warm temperatures kept parts of the lake open until mid-February.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Similarly,","Accordingly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This year's late freeze contrasts with the usual pattern. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists had long assumed that the site was abandoned after a devastating fire. ______ new excavations revealed that residents rebuilt many of their homes on top of the burned ruins and continued living there for another two centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["So","And","But","Or"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The new evidence contradicts the earlier assumption. "But" signals the contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new train line was expected to reduce car traffic between the two cities. ______ traffic on the highway fell by nearly twenty percent in the line's first year of operation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Indeed,","In contrast,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The drop in traffic confirms the expectation. "Indeed" signals that what follows supports and confirms the preceding statement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The novelist kept a detailed diary for more than sixty years, filling over a hundred notebooks. ______ she requested that the diaries be burned after her death, and her executors honored her wish.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Regrettably for historians,","Consequently,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The destruction of the diaries is a loss from the perspective of those who might have studied them. "Regrettably for historians" captures this. "Consequently" would imply that keeping a diary caused her request.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The forest's oldest trees are more than a thousand years old. ______ some of the fungi living among their roots may be even older, forming underground networks that have persisted through many generations of trees.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["So","For example,","Instead,","Yet"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$It is surprising that something could be older than thousand-year-old trees, and the sentence sets up this contrast. "Yet" signals it. "So" signals a result, "For example" an illustration, and "Instead" a replacement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city's population grew by more than forty percent between 2000 and 2020. ______ the number of housing units grew by only twelve percent, which helps explain why rents rose so sharply during the period.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","For example,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes a development that occurred during the same period and contrasts with population growth. "Meanwhile" signals simultaneous events. "Similarly" would wrongly suggest housing kept pace.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The chef refuses to use any ingredient that must be shipped by air. ______ the restaurant's menu changes almost weekly, depending on what local farmers and fishers can supply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a consequence,","In contrast,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The frequently changing menu results from the chef's refusal to use air-shipped ingredients. "As a consequence" signals this.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The study tracked the health of more than 100,000 nurses over three decades. ______ it is one of the largest and longest-running investigations of women's health ever conducted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Instead,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence emphasizes and builds on the scale described in the first. "In fact" signals this emphasis.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Early telephone systems required human operators to connect each call by plugging cables into a switchboard. ______ automatic switching equipment, introduced in the early twentieth century, allowed callers to dial numbers directly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Consequently,","For example,","Later,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence describes a later development that replaced the earlier system. "Later" signals this sequence in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most birds, which have hollow bones that reduce their weight, penguins have dense, solid bones. ______ the heavier skeleton helps them dive deep beneath the surface without having to fight their own buoyancy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","In other words,","For penguins, though,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence explains why heavy bones—a disadvantage for flying birds—benefit penguins. "For penguins, though" signals the contrast between the general rule and the penguins' case.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new vaccine must be stored at −70°C, far colder than standard freezers can reach. ______ many rural clinics, which lack ultracold freezers, were unable to offer it during the first months of its availability.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Similarly,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rural clinics' inability to offer the vaccine follows from its storage requirement. "Consequently" signals this result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The composer wrote the opera in just six weeks. ______ it took her nearly ten years to find a company willing to stage it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ironically,","Therefore,","Likewise,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast between the speed of composition and the long wait for a production is unexpected. "Ironically" signals this incongruity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The bacteria that cause the disease can survive for weeks on dry surfaces. ______ hospitals must disinfect bed rails, door handles, and medical equipment frequently to prevent the bacteria from spreading between patients.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","In contrast,","For instance,","For this reason,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The need to disinfect frequently follows from the bacteria's ability to survive on surfaces. "For this reason" signals the cause-and-effect relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The archive contains more than two million photographs. ______ fewer than ten percent have been cataloged, which means that most of the collection remains effectively invisible to researchers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","Similarly,","Of these,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence refers to a portion of the photographs mentioned in the first. "Of these" signals that a subset is being discussed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The theory that the Moon formed when a Mars-sized body collided with the early Earth explains many features of the Moon's composition. ______ it does not fully explain why the Moon's rocks are so chemically similar to Earth's.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Still,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence notes a limitation of the theory despite its explanatory power. "Still" signals this concession.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$To protect the fragile mosses that grow on the volcanic slopes, the park requires hikers to stay on marked trails. ______ visitors who stray from the trails can be fined up to five hundred dollars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Instead,","Conversely,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds an enforcement measure that supports the rule in the first. "Moreover" signals this addition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The dancer trained in classical ballet for fifteen years before joining a modern dance company. ______ her movements combined the precise footwork of ballet with the floor-bound weight of modern dance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","For example,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Her blending of styles follows from her background in both. "As a result" signals this effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some scientists argue that the extinction of the large mammals at the end of the last ice age was caused mainly by climate change. ______ others contend that human hunting played the decisive role, noting that the extinctions often followed soon after humans arrived in a region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Similarly,","Conversely,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence presents an opposing view. "Conversely" signals contrasting positions.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The author's first two novels sold only a few hundred copies each. Her third, a mystery set in a remote lighthouse, ______ became an international best seller and was adapted into a film.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["consequently,","by contrast,","likewise,","for instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The success of the third novel contrasts with the poor sales of the first two. "By contrast" signals this difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The drought reduced the region's wheat harvest by nearly half. ______ bread prices in the capital doubled within three months, sparking protests.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Predictably,","Nevertheless,","In contrast,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rising bread prices are an expected consequence of a poor wheat harvest. "Predictably" signals an outcome that follows naturally from the situation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The engineers expected the bridge's steel cables to last at least a century. ______ inspections after just forty years found that salt spray from the nearby ocean had corroded many of the wires inside the cables.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Moreover,","In short,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The inspection results contradict the engineers' expectation; something different from what was expected happened. "Instead" signals this replacement of the expected outcome.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ancient city had no defensive walls, a fact that puzzled archaeologists for years. ______ the city may not have needed them: there is little evidence of warfare anywhere in the region during the period when the city flourished.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","As it turns out,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence resolves the puzzle described in the first by offering an explanation discovered later. "As it turns out" signals that the answer has emerged.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Hummingbirds can hover in place by beating their wings in a figure-eight pattern. ______ they generate lift on both the downstroke and the upstroke, unlike most birds, which generate lift mainly on the downstroke.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","In this way,","Nonetheless,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence explains the result of the figure-eight motion described in the first. "In this way" signals that the second sentence describes how the method works.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The museum's visitors are encouraged to touch many of the objects on display. ______ the oldest and most fragile artifacts are kept behind glass and may not be handled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Thus,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The restriction on fragile artifacts contrasts with the general encouragement to touch objects. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$When the factory closed in 1998, nearly a third of the town's residents lost their jobs. ______ the population fell from about 12,000 to fewer than 7,000 as families moved away in search of work.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Even so,","For instance,","Over the next decade,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The population decline happened gradually after the closing. "Over the next decade" signals this sequence and time span.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Earthworms have no lungs. ______ they absorb oxygen directly through their moist skin, which is why they must stay in damp soil to survive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Similarly,","Instead,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes what earthworms do in place of breathing with lungs. "Instead" signals this alternative.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The research team had expected that participants who slept less would perform worse on the memory test. ______ the participants who slept less than five hours scored nearly twenty percent lower than those who slept eight hours or more.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","As predicted,","Conversely,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The results matched the team's expectation. "As predicted" signals this confirmation. "Surprisingly" and "Nevertheless" signal unexpected or contrasting results.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The poet's first collection was praised for its formal precision. ______ some reviewers found the poems emotionally distant, as if the poet were more interested in craft than in feeling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","As a result,","In other words,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds a qualifying criticism alongside the praise. "At the same time" signals that a contrasting consideration exists alongside the first.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The device can purify up to a thousand liters of water a day using only sunlight. ______ it requires no fuel, no electricity, and no replacement filters, making it practical for remote villages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","In contrast,","Previously,","Furthermore,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence adds more advantages of the device. "Furthermore" signals additional supporting points.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the only way to cross the strait was by ferry, and storms often halted service for days at a time. ______ since the tunnel opened in 1994, travelers can make the crossing by train in about thirty-five minutes, regardless of the weather.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Thus,","Now,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes the present situation, which contrasts with the past described in the first. "Now" signals this change in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some researchers believe that the ancient carvings depict constellations. ______ the arrangement of dots on one stone closely matches the pattern of the Pleiades star cluster as it would have appeared in the night sky at the time the carving was made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","In support of this view,","Even so,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence offers evidence for the researchers' belief. "In support of this view" signals supporting evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Lightning is extremely hot, reaching temperatures of about 30,000°C. ______ when it strikes sand, it can melt the grains into hollow glass tubes called fulgurites.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nonetheless,","In contrast,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The formation of fulgurites results from lightning's extreme heat. "Consequently" signals this result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The neighborhood's community garden began with a single raised bed planted by two retired teachers. ______ it covers half a city block and supplies vegetables to more than sixty families.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","For instance,","Nevertheless,","Today,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence describes the garden's present state in contrast with its beginnings. "Today" signals this shift in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The company claims that its new phone is the most durable on the market. ______ independent testers found that its screen cracked when dropped from a height of just one meter, while competitors' screens survived falls from twice that height.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Indeed,","Accordingly,","However,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The test results contradict the company's claim. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violin was made in 1721 and is valued at more than ten million dollars. ______ its owner, a foundation, lends it to a young performer who plays it in concerts around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Rather than keep it in a vault,","In other words,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes the foundation choosing to put the valuable violin to use instead of storing it. "Rather than keep it in a vault" signals this contrast with what might be expected for so valuable an object.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that people who were told a wine was expensive rated it as tasting better than people who were told the same wine was cheap. ______ brain scans showed greater activity in regions associated with pleasure when participants believed they were drinking the expensive wine.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["What's more,","Even so,","In contrast,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds further, stronger evidence for the same finding. "What's more" signals this intensifying addition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some species of trees in the rainforest grow very slowly for decades in the shade of taller trees. ______ when a large tree falls and opens a gap in the canopy, these young trees can grow several meters in just a few years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["So","For instance,","Or","But"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The rapid growth after a gap opens contrasts with the slow growth in the shade. "But" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first edition of the encyclopedia was written almost entirely by a single scholar. ______ the current edition draws on the work of more than four thousand contributors from around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For example,","By contrast,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The many contributors to the current edition contrast with the single author of the first. "By contrast" signals this difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new bus routes were designed to connect the city's poorest neighborhoods with its largest employers. ______ commute times for residents of those neighborhoods fell by an average of twenty minutes each way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","As intended,","In contrast,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The shorter commutes are the outcome the routes were designed to produce. "As intended" signals that a result matched the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Early studies of the drug were promising: patients who took it reported fewer migraines than those who took a placebo. The studies, however, lasted only eight weeks. ______ researchers could not say whether the benefit would persist or whether side effects might emerge with longer use.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Similarly,","Consequently,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The short length of the studies is the reason researchers couldn't judge long-term effects, so a cause-and-effect transition is needed. "Nevertheless" signals a contrast that isn't there, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most hummingbirds defend feeding territories aggressively, chasing away any rival that approaches their flowers. The long-billed hermit, ______ follows a regular circuit among widely scattered flowers, visiting each one in turn and rarely confronting other birds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["by contrast,","as a result,","for example,","in addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hermit's behavior, a peaceful circuit, differs from the territorial behavior described for most hummingbirds, so "by contrast" is correct. "For example" would wrongly present the hermit as an instance of territorial behavior, and the other two signal addition and causation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Architect Diébédo Francis Kéré built his first school, in his home village of Gando, Burkina Faso, from clay bricks made by local residents. The thick walls kept classrooms cool without air-conditioning. ______ the building's broad, overhanging roof shaded the walls and let hot air escape through vents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Therefore,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second feature, the roof, adds to the first, the walls, as a way the building stayed cool, so the additive "Moreover" fits. "However" and "Instead" signal contrast or replacement, and "Therefore" would wrongly suggest the walls caused the roof's design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that a dog's wagging tail always signals friendliness. ______ researchers have found that the direction of the wag matters: dogs tend to wag more to the right when they see their owners and more to the left when they see an unfamiliar, dominant dog.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Likewise,","In fact,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The research complicates and corrects the common assumption, and "In fact" introduces a more accurate account. "Likewise" signals similarity, "Accordingly" signals a consequence of the assumption, and "Meanwhile" signals something happening at the same time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violinist practiced the concerto's opening passage for months, slowing it down to half speed and playing each note with a metronome. ______ she could play it flawlessly at full tempo, even under the pressure of a live audience.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Still,","Conversely,","For instance,","Eventually,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence describes the result reached after months of practice, so the time transition "Eventually" fits. "Conversely" signals an opposite case, "For instance" signals an example, and "Still" signals that something holds despite an obstacle, which misreads the relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The volcano had shown no signs of activity for more than five centuries, and many geologists considered it extinct. ______ seismometers placed on its slopes in 2017 detected swarms of small earthquakes, suggesting that magma was moving beneath the surface.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet","Similarly,","Thus","Furthermore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The earthquake data contradict the expectation that the volcano was extinct, so the contrast transition "Yet" fits. "Thus" signals a conclusion, "Furthermore" signals an addition in the same direction, and "Similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most metals, gallium melts at just under 30°C, low enough that a piece held in the hand will turn to liquid. ______ it remains liquid over an unusually wide range of temperatures, not boiling until it reaches about 2,400°C.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Hence,","Additionally,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence adds a second unusual property of gallium, so "Additionally" fits. "Specifically" would need the sentence to give a detail of the melting point, "Nonetheless" signals contrast, and "Hence" signals that the low melting point causes the high boiling point, which the text doesn't claim.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Critics praised the novelist's first book for its tight plotting and spare prose. Her second novel, ______ sprawls across six hundred pages and three generations, digressing frequently into essays on history and music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["as a result,","on the other hand,","in other words,","in the same way,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second novel's sprawling, digressive style contrasts with the first book's tight, spare style, so "on the other hand" fits. "In other words" signals restatement, "as a result" signals causation, and "in the same way" signals similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In laboratory tests, the new battery stored twice as much energy as conventional lithium-ion cells. ______ it lost much of its capacity after only fifty charging cycles, far fewer than the thousands that commercial batteries must withstand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","In short,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The battery's rapid loss of capacity is a drawback that contrasts with its impressive energy storage, so "However" fits. "Therefore" signals a consequence, "Likewise" signals similarity, and "In short" signals a summary.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Historian Rhys Isaac argued that eighteenth-century Virginia's courthouse days were more than legal proceedings; they were social events at which planters displayed their wealth and status before the community. ______ the courthouse lawn served as a stage on which the colony's social hierarchy was performed and reinforced.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","Even so,","By contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence restates Isaac's argument in a vivid way, so "In other words" fits. "By contrast" signals difference, "For example" would need a specific instance, and "Even so" signals concession.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The archaeologists expected to find evidence of a large permanent settlement at the site. ______ they uncovered only a few scattered hearths and stone tools, suggesting that the area was used as a seasonal hunting camp.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Similarly,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$What the archaeologists found replaced what they expected, so "Instead" fits. "Moreover" signals addition, "Similarly" signals likeness, and "Accordingly" signals that the finding followed from the expectation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Elephants communicate using rumbles too low for humans to hear. These infrasonic calls can travel several kilometers through the air. ______ they travel even farther through the ground, where other elephants detect the vibrations with sensitive cells in their feet and trunks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Consequently,","In contrast,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second point builds on the first (the calls travel far through air and even farther through the ground), so "Moreover" fits. "Nevertheless" and "In contrast" signal opposition, and "Consequently" suggests that traveling through air causes traveling through the ground.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city's new bus routes were designed to cut commute times in half. ______ ridership fell during the first year, because many residents found the redesigned network confusing and the new stops farther from their homes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Ironically,","Predictably,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A plan meant to improve service led to fewer riders, an outcome opposite to what was intended, so "Ironically" fits. "Predictably" contradicts the stated goal, "Specifically" signals a detail, and "Similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Hand-drawn animation requires artists to draw each frame individually, as many as twenty-four for every second of film. ______ a ninety-minute feature can require well over a hundred thousand drawings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Alternatively,","Thus,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The large number of drawings follows from the frame-by-frame requirement, so the result transition "Thus" fits. "Nonetheless" signals contrast, "Alternatively" signals another option, and "Meanwhile" signals simultaneity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage reduces employment, since businesses will hire fewer workers when labor costs rise. ______ a well-known 1994 study comparing fast-food restaurants in New Jersey and Pennsylvania found no such decline after New Jersey raised its wage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Hence,","Likewise,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The study's finding runs counter to the economists' prediction, so "However" fits. "Hence" signals a conclusion, "For example" would wrongly present the study as supporting the argument, and "Likewise" signals agreement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The poet's early work was rooted in the rural landscapes of her childhood: barns, orchards, and frozen ponds. ______ after she moved to the city in her thirties, her poems turned to subways, crowded markets, and the view from high-rise windows.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Similarly,","For instance,","Later,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence describes a later stage of the poet's career, so the time transition "Later" fits. "Similarly" ignores the change in subject matter, "For instance" signals an example, and "In fact" signals emphasis or correction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$A single honeybee colony may contain fifty thousand workers. ______ the colony's survival through winter depends on just one individual: the queen, who alone lays the eggs that replenish the population.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Therefore,","Furthermore,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite the colony's huge numbers, its survival depends on one bee. That's a contrast between expectation and reality, so "Nevertheless" fits. "Therefore" implies that many workers cause dependence on the queen, "Furthermore" signals addition, and "In other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Sea otters eat large numbers of sea urchins, which graze on kelp. Where otters were hunted to near-extinction, urchin populations exploded and kelp forests disappeared. ______ where otters have returned, kelp forests have often regrown within a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","Similarly,","Specifically,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence describes the reverse situation, otters returning instead of disappearing, with the reverse outcome, so "Conversely" fits. "Similarly" signals likeness, "Specifically" signals a detail, and "As a result" would make the regrowth a consequence of the otters' disappearance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The museum's collection of Roman coins is among the largest in Europe. ______ fewer than one percent of the coins are on display at any time; the rest are kept in climate-controlled storage beneath the building.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Indeed,","Therefore,","Even so,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Despite the collection's size, very few coins are shown, so the concessive "Even so" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "Indeed" signals emphasis of the previous point, and "Likewise" signals similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Elena Fuentes found that students who explained a concept aloud to a classmate remembered it better than students who simply reread their notes. ______ students who explained the concept to an imaginary listener performed nearly as well as those who explained it to a real one.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Interestingly,","Consequently,","For example,","Conversely,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second finding is a notable addition: even an imaginary listener helped. "Interestingly" introduces it as a surprising extension. "Consequently" signals a result the first finding doesn't produce, "For example" would need an instance of the first finding, and "Conversely" signals an opposite result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The mountain's northern face receives almost no direct sunlight during winter. ______ snow accumulates there to depths of several meters and often lingers well into July.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","As a result,","In contrast,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lack of sunlight causes snow to accumulate and linger, so "As a result" fits. "In contrast" and "Nevertheless" signal opposition, and "Similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Translators of ancient poetry face a difficult choice. They can preserve the original's meter and rhyme, ______ sacrificing some precision of meaning, or they can render the meaning exactly at the cost of the poem's music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["thereby","instead","however","similarly"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Preserving meter and rhyme leads to a loss of precision, so "thereby" (meaning "by doing so") fits. "However" signals contrast, "similarly" signals likeness, and "instead" signals replacement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Coffee plants produce caffeine in their leaves and seeds, where it acts as a natural pesticide that deters insects. ______ the plants also produce small amounts of caffeine in their nectar, where it seems to have the opposite effect: bees that taste it are more likely to remember the flower and return.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For this reason,","Curiously,","Accordingly,","In summary,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Caffeine attracting bees is a surprising contrast with its role as a pesticide, so "Curiously" fits. "Accordingly" and "For this reason" signal that the pesticide role causes the nectar effect, and "In summary" signals a conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first edition of the dictionary, published in 1928, defined about 400,000 words. ______ the second edition, published in 1989, defined more than 600,000, reflecting both new coinages and words that earlier editors had overlooked.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By comparison,","Nonetheless,","As a result,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence compares the second edition's word count with the first's, so "By comparison" fits. "As a result" implies the first edition caused the second's size, "For instance" signals an example, and "Nonetheless" signals contrast against an expectation that isn't set up.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Engineers initially designed the robot's legs to mimic those of a dog. ______ they discovered that the four-legged design struggled on loose sand, they borrowed instead from the six-legged gait of desert beetles.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Although","Unless","Because of","When"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The engineers changed the design at the point when they discovered the problem, so "When" fits. "Although" signals concession, "Because of" requires a noun phrase rather than a clause, and "Unless" signals a condition that makes no sense here.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Critics of the proposed high-speed rail line argue that its construction costs will far exceed the official estimates. ______ large infrastructure projects frequently run over budget; the city's last two transit projects each cost nearly twice what was projected. But supporters contend that the line's long-term economic benefits will justify even a substantial overrun.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Granted,","Similarly,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The writer briefly concedes the critics' point before "But" introduces the supporters' rebuttal, so the concessive "Granted" fits. "Therefore" would present the overruns as a conclusion from the critics' argument, "Instead" signals replacement, and "Similarly" signals a parallel rather than a concession.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The fossil's teeth were long, curved, and serrated, the kind typically associated with meat-eating dinosaurs. ______ chemical analysis of the tooth enamel revealed carbon isotope ratios consistent with a diet composed mostly of plants.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In particular,","Accordingly,","Indeed,","Surprisingly, though,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The chemical evidence contradicts what the teeth's shape suggests, so a contrast is needed, and "Surprisingly, though" supplies it. "Indeed" and "In particular" would treat the chemical evidence as supporting or specifying the meat-eating inference, and "Accordingly" would present it as a consequence of that inference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Political scientist Hana Moreau studied voter turnout in cities that moved local elections to coincide with national ones. Turnout in those local elections rose dramatically, in some cases tripling. ______ the change did not appear to alter which candidates won: incumbents were reelected at about the same rate as before.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Notably, however,","Likewise,","In other words,","For this reason,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second result (no change in outcomes) contrasts with what the turnout surge might lead readers to expect, so "Notably, however" fits. "In other words" signals restatement, "For this reason" signals that higher turnout caused the unchanged outcomes, and "Likewise" signals a parallel effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most sharks, which must keep swimming to push water over their gills, the nurse shark can rest motionless on the seafloor. It pumps water over its gills using muscles in its mouth and throat. ______ it can spend much of the day lying in caves and crevices, emerging to hunt at night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","By contrast,","Thus,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The shark's ability to pump water explains why it can lie still for long periods, so the result transition "Thus" fits. "Even so" signals concession, "For example" would require an example of pumping water, and "By contrast" signals a difference that isn't present.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In most languages, words for colors appear in a predictable order: if a language has only two color terms, they mean roughly "dark" and "light"; a third term almost always means "red." ______ a language with a word for "blue" will nearly always have words for "green" and "yellow" as well.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In short,","As a result,","Similarly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The final sentence gives another instance of the same predictable pattern, so "Similarly" fits. "However" signals contrast, "As a result" would claim that the earlier patterns cause this one, and "In short" would require a summary rather than a new example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The novel's protagonist insists throughout the first half of the book that she has no interest in returning to her hometown. ______ she keeps a key to her childhood house on a chain around her neck, a detail the narrator mentions in nearly every chapter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","Moreover,","Yet","Hence"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The key on her necklace contradicts her stated lack of interest, so "Yet" fits. "Hence" signals a result, "Moreover" would add a point in the same direction, and "Specifically" signals a detail of the previous claim.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The study found that participants who slept fewer than six hours were more likely to catch a cold after exposure to the virus. ______ the researchers caution that the study was observational: it shows an association between short sleep and illness, not that one causes the other.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Still,","Thus,","For instance,","Furthermore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The researchers qualify the finding with a limitation, so the concessive "Still" fits. "Thus" signals that the caution follows from the finding, "Furthermore" signals an additional finding, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Before the 1850s, few Americans had ever tasted a tomato that wasn't cooked; many believed the raw fruit was mildly poisonous. ______ by the end of the century, tomatoes were among the most widely eaten vegetables in the country, served raw in salads and sandwiches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For this reason,","Similarly,","In addition,","Within a few decades, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The final sentence describes a change over time that contrasts with the earlier wariness, so "Within a few decades, however" fits. "For this reason" signals cause, "In addition" signals addition, and "Similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most migrating songbirds fly at night, navigating partly by the stars. ______ the birds can become disoriented by the lights of cities, and thousands die each year in collisions with illuminated buildings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Alternatively,","Consequently,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because the birds fly at night and navigate by the stars, city lights can disorient them, so the result transition "Consequently" fits. "Nevertheless" signals contrast, "For example" would need an example of navigation, and "Alternatively" signals an option.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many early photographs show subjects with stern, unsmiling faces. Historians once attributed this to long exposure times, which made holding a smile difficult. ______ exposure times had dropped to a fraction of a second by the 1880s, yet stiff expressions remained the norm for decades, suggesting that cultural expectations about dignity in portraits played a larger role.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Likewise,","But","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The final sentence challenges the historians' explanation, so the contrast transition "But" fits. "Thus" would present the evidence as following from the earlier explanation, "Likewise" signals agreement, and "For instance" signals an example of it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In the experiment, mice raised in cages with running wheels, tunnels, and toys developed more connections between neurons than mice raised in empty cages. ______ the enriched mice learned to navigate mazes significantly faster.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["They instead","They nonetheless","They therefore still","They also"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Faster maze learning is an additional finding consistent with the first, so "They also" fits. "Instead" implies replacement, "nonetheless" implies a contrast with the first finding, and "therefore still" combines a cause with a concession that the text doesn't set up.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Wind turbines generate electricity only when the wind blows, which makes their output difficult for grid operators to predict. ______ new forecasting models that combine weather data with machine learning can now predict a wind farm's output a day in advance with errors of less than ten percent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","In other words,","Increasingly, though,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The improved forecasts counter the difficulty described in the first sentence, so "Increasingly, though" fits. "Therefore" would claim that unpredictability causes accurate forecasts, "In other words" signals restatement, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The composer completed only the first two movements of the symphony before his death. ______ the work, known today as the "Unfinished" Symphony, has become one of the most frequently performed pieces in the orchestral repertoire.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nonetheless,","In fact,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Despite being incomplete, the symphony became widely performed, so the concessive "Nonetheless" fits. "Accordingly" signals a result, "In fact" signals an intensification of the previous point, and "Specifically" signals a detail.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Volunteers in the citizen-science project classified images of hundreds of thousands of galaxies in the project's first year. ______ the volunteers identified an entirely new class of compact green galaxies, nicknamed "green peas," which professional astronomers had overlooked.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In the process,","In comparison,","On the contrary,","Even so,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The discovery happened during the classification work, so "In the process" fits. "On the contrary" signals contradiction, "Even so" signals concession, and "In comparison" signals a comparison, none of which matches the relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$For decades, nutrition guidelines warned against eating eggs because of their high cholesterol content. ______ research has since shown that, for most people, dietary cholesterol has only a small effect on cholesterol levels in the blood, and many guidelines have dropped the warning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Likewise,","However,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Newer research contradicts the older warning, so "However" fits. "Therefore" would make the new research a consequence of the warning, and "Likewise" and "In addition" would treat it as continuing in the same direction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The desert tortoise spends as much as 95 percent of its life underground, in burrows it digs with its powerful forelimbs. ______ the burrows protect the tortoise from both summer heat exceeding 60°C at the surface and winter frosts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless, these","These","Instead, these","Conversely, these"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The final sentence explains the benefit of the burrows just mentioned, with no contrast or reversal, so no transition is needed: "These" works. "Instead," "Nevertheless," and "Conversely" signal contrasts that the text doesn't support.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some scholars have read the play as a critique of the monarchy. ______ the playwright was a favorite of the king, received a royal pension, and dedicated two other works to members of the royal family, facts that complicate that reading.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["After all,","Yet","For instance,","Thus"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The facts about the playwright's royal favor complicate the scholars' reading, so the contrast transition "Yet" fits. "After all" would present the facts as support for the reading, "Thus" would present them as a conclusion drawn from it, and "For instance" would offer them as an example of the critique.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Chess grandmasters can recall the positions of pieces on a board after looking at it for just five seconds, far better than novices can. ______ when pieces are placed randomly, in positions that could never arise in a real game, grandmasters recall them no better than novices do.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For this reason,","Strikingly, however,","Similarly,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The random-position result contrasts with the grandmasters' usual superior recall, so "Strikingly, however" fits. "For this reason" signals cause, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "In other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The city's oldest bridge was scheduled for demolition in 1975. Residents organized protests, collected thousands of signatures, and persuaded a local historian to document the bridge's significance. ______ the city council voted to restore the bridge rather than replace it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Nevertheless,","Instead,","Ultimately,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The council's vote is the final outcome of the residents' efforts, so "Ultimately" fits. "Instead" signals replacement of the previous action, "Nevertheless" signals contrast, and "For example" signals an illustration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Lichens look like single organisms, but each is actually a partnership between a fungus and an alga or cyanobacterium. The fungus provides structure and absorbs water and minerals. ______ the alga or cyanobacterium produces food through photosynthesis.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Its partner, as a result,","Its partner, in turn,","Its partner, by contrast, then","Its partner, for instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence describes the partner's reciprocal contribution, so "in turn" fits. "By contrast, then" adds a mismatched sequence word, "as a result" makes the fungus's work cause photosynthesis, and "for instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many runners believe that stretching before a race prevents injury. Several large studies, ______ have found that pre-exercise stretching has little or no effect on injury rates, though warming up with light activity may help.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["similarly,","for example,","therefore,","however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The studies contradict the runners' belief, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a conclusion, "similarly" signals agreement, and "for example" signals an instance of the belief.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Monarch caterpillars feed only on milkweed, a plant that contains toxins called cardenolides. The caterpillars store these toxins in their bodies. ______ adult monarchs taste bitter to birds, which quickly learn to avoid them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","Similarly,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Storing the toxins makes the adults bitter, so the cause-and-effect transition "As a result" fits. "In contrast" and "Nevertheless" signal opposition, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The tallest sand dunes on Earth, in China's Badain Jaran Desert, rise more than 400 meters. ______ the desert also contains more than one hundred small lakes, fed by groundwater, nestled among the dunes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Consequently,","For instance,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lakes in a desert of enormous dunes are unexpected, so "Surprisingly" fits. "Consequently" claims that the dunes cause the lakes, "In other words" signals restatement, and "For instance" signals an example of tall dunes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The company's first electric scooter sold poorly; customers complained that its battery lasted less than an hour. ______ the company's engineers redesigned the battery pack, extending its range to nearly four hours, and sales of the second model quadrupled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","In summary,","In response,","On the other hand,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The redesign was a reaction to the customers' complaints, so "In response" fits. "On the other hand" signals contrast, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "In summary" signals a conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Medieval maps often placed Jerusalem at the center of the world and filled distant regions with imaginary creatures. Such maps were not meant primarily for navigation. ______ they expressed a religious worldview, showing viewers how the known world related to sacred history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Rather,","For example,","Consequently,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence replaces the denied purpose (navigation) with the actual purpose, so "Rather" fits. "Similarly" signals likeness, "Consequently" signals that not being meant for navigation caused the worldview, and "For example" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Octopuses can change the color of their skin in a fraction of a second. ______ most species are thought to be colorblind, having only one type of light-sensitive cell in their eyes, which has led researchers to investigate whether the skin itself can detect light.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Likewise,","In short,","Puzzlingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Animals that match colors expertly but appear to be colorblind present a puzzle, so "Puzzlingly" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "Likewise" signals similarity, and "In short" signals a summary.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The 1918 influenza pandemic killed an estimated fifty million people worldwide. Unusually, the disease was especially deadly for healthy adults between the ages of twenty and forty. ______ most influenza strains are most dangerous to the very young and the very old.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Furthermore,","By contrast,","As a result,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Typical flu strains affect different age groups than the 1918 strain did, so "By contrast" fits. "As a result" signals a cause, "Furthermore" signals addition, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The author spent eleven years researching the biography, interviewing more than three hundred people and reading thousands of letters. ______ the book contains details about its subject's early life that no previous biographer had uncovered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Not surprisingly,","Even so,","On the other hand,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$New details are an expected result of such extensive research, so "Not surprisingly" fits. "Even so" and "On the other hand" signal contrast, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Coastal mangrove forests store carbon in their soils at rates up to four times higher than tropical rainforests on land. ______ they protect shorelines from storm surges and provide nursery habitat for many commercially important fish.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","In other words,","Instead,","Beyond this,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Storm protection and fish habitat are benefits in addition to carbon storage, so "Beyond this" fits. "Instead" signals replacement, "Nonetheless" signals contrast, and "In other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Philosopher Iris Murdoch was an accomplished novelist as well as a scholar. In her philosophical writing, she argued that moral life consists less in dramatic choices than in the quality of our everyday attention to others. ______ her novels are filled with characters whose moral failures stem from a self-absorbed inability to see the people around them clearly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","Fittingly,","Otherwise,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The novels' themes match her philosophical argument about attention, so "Fittingly" signals the appropriate correspondence. "Conversely" signals opposition, "Admittedly" signals concession, and "Otherwise" signals an alternative condition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The research team expected that crows raised in the laboratory would need to watch adults before they could use sticks to extract insects from logs. ______ young crows that had never seen a tool used began probing with twigs on their own within a few weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["To their surprise,","Similarly,","Specifically,","For this reason,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The young crows' behavior contradicts the team's expectation, so "To their surprise" fits. "For this reason" signals cause, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "Specifically" signals a detail of the expectation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Copper is an excellent conductor of electricity, but it is heavy and increasingly expensive. ______ engineers designing long-distance power lines usually choose aluminum, which conducts electricity somewhat less well but weighs about a third as much.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","For example,","In contrast,","For these reasons,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Copper's weight and cost explain why engineers choose aluminum, so "For these reasons" fits. "In contrast" and "Nevertheless" signal opposition, and "For example" signals an illustration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The Library of Alexandria is often said to have been destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. ______ historians now believe the library declined gradually over several centuries, as funding dwindled and scholars left for other centers of learning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","In addition,","Likewise,","In reality,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The historians' view corrects the popular story, so "In reality" fits. "Accordingly" signals consequence, and "Likewise" and "In addition" signal agreement or continuation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Ravens in one study learned to trade tokens for food, choosing a token that could be exchanged for a better reward later over a smaller reward available immediately. ______ the birds showed a capacity for planning previously documented mainly in great apes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In doing so,","Instead,","Even so,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By choosing the delayed reward, the ravens demonstrated planning, so "In doing so" links the action to what it showed. "Instead" signals replacement, "Even so" signals concession, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most modern cities are built on grids, with streets meeting at right angles. Many medieval European towns, ______ grew organically, their winding lanes following old footpaths, property lines, and the contours of the land.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["likewise,","for example,","however,","therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Medieval towns' organic growth contrasts with modern grids, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "for example" would present them as examples of grids, and "likewise" signals similarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The new telescope's mirror is 6.5 meters across, much larger than that of its predecessor. ______ the telescope can collect enough light to observe galaxies that formed only a few hundred million years after the Big Bang.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Because of this size,","Unlike this size,","Besides this size,","Despite this size,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The larger mirror is what lets the telescope collect so much light, so "Because of this size" fits. "Despite" signals contrast, "Besides" signals addition, and "Unlike" signals difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many readers assume that Mary Shelley's <i>Frankenstein</i> is a story about a monster. ______ the novel spends far more time examining its creator, Victor Frankenstein, whose ambition and neglect of his creation drive the tragedy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Moreover,","Hence","Yet"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The novel's actual focus contrasts with the common assumption, so "Yet" fits. "Hence" signals a result, and "Moreover" and "Similarly" signal agreement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Before the invention of the marine chronometer, sailors could determine their latitude fairly easily by measuring the angle of the sun at noon. ______ longitude remained a problem; without an accurate clock, navigators had no reliable way to measure how far east or west they had traveled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Longitude, likewise,","Longitude, however,","Longitude, therefore,","Longitude, for instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Longitude's difficulty contrasts with how easily latitude could be found, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "likewise" signals similarity, and "for instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$A 2019 survey found that 70 percent of the region's farmers had adopted no-till methods, which reduce soil erosion. ______ only 15 percent had adopted cover crops, another practice recommended by soil scientists, largely because of the extra cost of seed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Above all,","By comparison,","For this reason,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence compares adoption rates for two practices, so "By comparison" fits. "For this reason" signals cause, "Similarly" ignores the large difference, and "Above all" signals emphasis.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's recording of the Bach sonatas was praised for its technical precision. Several critics, ______ found the performances emotionally distant, arguing that precision had been achieved at the expense of expression.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["therefore,","in fact,","though,","for instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The critics' complaint contrasts with the praise, so "though" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "in fact" signals emphasis, and "for instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The trees in the experimental plot were watered twice as often as those in the control plot. ______ they grew nearly 40 percent taller over five years, though their wood was less dense.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Accordingly,","Similarly,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The extra watering produced the extra growth, so "Accordingly" fits. "Nevertheless" and "Instead" signal contrast, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Native to the Andes, potatoes were cultivated there for thousands of years before Spanish explorers brought them to Europe in the 1500s. ______ Europeans regarded them with suspicion for nearly two centuries, and they became a staple food in most of the continent only in the 1700s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","In conclusion,","At first,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence describes the initial reaction followed by later adoption, so the time transition "At first" fits. "In conclusion" signals a summary, "For instance" signals an example, and "Likewise" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The company argued that its new pesticide breaks down within days and poses no risk to bees. Independent researchers, ______ detected the chemical in pollen collected from hives more than a month after spraying.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["as a result,","by contrast,","likewise,","in other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The researchers' finding contradicts the company's claim, so "by contrast" fits. "As a result" signals cause, "likewise" signals agreement, and "in other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Before the 1970s, most scientists believed that the deep ocean floor was nearly lifeless, since no sunlight reaches it. ______ in 1977, researchers aboard the submersible <i>Alvin</i> discovered thriving communities of tube worms, clams, and crabs clustered around hydrothermal vents near the Galápagos Islands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In short,","Thus,","Then,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The 1977 discovery is the next event in a sequence that overturned the old belief, so "Then" fits. "Thus" would present the discovery as a conclusion from the belief, "Similarly" signals agreement, and "In short" signals a summary.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most bird species build a new nest every breeding season. Bald eagles, ______ often return to the same nest year after year, adding new sticks each time; one nest in Ohio grew to weigh nearly two tons.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["for example,","on the other hand,","consequently,","in addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Bald eagles' reuse of nests contrasts with the usual pattern, so "on the other hand" fits. "Consequently" signals a result, "for example" would present eagles as examples of building new nests, and "in addition" signals addition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Economists often assume that people make decisions by carefully weighing costs and benefits. ______ behavioral economist Ruth Adeyemi has shown that when shoppers face more than about a dozen options, many simply choose nothing at all.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As this assumption predicts,","Challenging this assumption,","Like this assumption,","Building on this assumption,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adeyemi's finding, that too many options lead people to choose nothing, cuts against the assumption of careful cost-benefit weighing, so "Challenging this assumption" fits. The other choices present the finding as consistent with or derived from the assumption.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The glacier lost about 30 meters of thickness between 1990 and 2010. ______ it lost another 30 meters in just the following eight years, a sign that its melting is accelerating.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Alarmingly,","Conversely,","In other words,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The same loss in far less time shows acceleration, a troubling development, so "Alarmingly" fits. "Conversely" signals reversal, "For example" signals an illustration of the first loss, and "In other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In the early 1900s, few people believed that a heavier-than-air machine could fly under its own power. ______ when the Wright brothers made their first successful flights in 1903, many newspapers declined to report the news, assuming it was a hoax.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","In contrast,","However,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Widespread disbelief explains why newspapers dismissed the news, so the result transition "Thus" fits. "However" and "In contrast" signal opposition, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Sunflowers are famous for tracking the sun across the sky. Only young plants do this, ______ once a sunflower matures and its head opens, it stops moving and faces east permanently.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["in addition;","however;","therefore;","for example;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second clause qualifies the famous behavior, which applies only to young plants and not mature ones, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "for example" an illustration, and "in addition" an added point.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Historians long believed that the Vikings never reached the Americas, dismissing Norse sagas describing a land called Vinland as legend. ______ in 1960, explorers Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad found the remains of a Norse settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Therefore,","Instead,","Then,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The 1960 discovery is the next event in the story, the one that overturned the long-held belief, so "Then" fits. "Therefore" implies the belief caused the discovery, "Likewise" signals agreement, and "Instead" signals replacement of a previous action.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Engineers can make concrete stronger by adding more cement to the mix. ______ cement production is responsible for roughly 8 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, so stronger concrete often comes at a substantial environmental cost.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","For instance,","Unfortunately,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The environmental cost is an undesirable downside to the method described, so "Unfortunately" fits. "Consequently" claims that adding cement causes cement's share of emissions, "For instance" signals an example, and "Similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The poet wrote in both Spanish and English, often switching between them within a single line. ______ her poems resist translation, since any translator must decide whether to preserve the shifts or flatten them into one language.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In comparison,","For this reason,","Nevertheless,","On the contrary,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The poet's switching between languages is the reason her poems resist translation, so "For this reason" fits. "On the contrary" and "Nevertheless" signal opposition, and "In comparison" signals a comparison.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Zebra stripes were once thought to serve as camouflage, helping the animals blend into tall grass. Recent experiments suggest a different function. ______ horses wearing striped coats were landed on by biting flies far less often than horses wearing solid coats.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Similarly,","Instead,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The experiment provides details of the "different function" just mentioned, so "Specifically" fits. "Instead" would repeat the contrast already made, "Nonetheless" signals concession, and "Similarly" signals a parallel.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$A typical commercial airliner cruises at about 900 kilometers per hour. The Concorde, ______ cruised at more than twice the speed of sound, crossing the Atlantic in under three and a half hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["similarly,","for example,","as a result,","by contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The Concorde's speed differs sharply from that of typical airliners, so "by contrast" fits. "For example" would present the Concorde as a typical airliner, "as a result" signals cause, and "similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra had struggled financially for years, and in 2015 its board considered disbanding it. ______ a series of free outdoor concerts drew thousands of new listeners, and within three years the orchestra had doubled its subscriber base.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That summer, for instance,","That summer, similarly,","That summer, accordingly,","That summer, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The concerts' success reversed the orchestra's decline, so "however" fits. "Accordingly" signals that the near-disbanding caused the success, "similarly" signals a parallel, and "for instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water bears, can survive being dried out for years. When water returns, they rehydrate and resume normal activity within hours. ______ tardigrades have survived exposure to the vacuum of space, extreme radiation, and temperatures near absolute zero.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even more remarkably,","As a result,","In contrast,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final sentence adds even more extreme examples of survival, so "Even more remarkably" fits. "As a result" claims that surviving dryness causes surviving space, "In contrast" signals opposition, and "In other words" signals restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The novelist insisted that her books were not autobiographical. ______ readers who knew her well recognized her mother, her childhood home, and her first marriage in thinly disguised form throughout her fiction.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Moreover,","Still,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Readers' recognition of her life in the books contrasts with her denial, so "Still" fits. "Thus" signals a result, and "In fact" and "Moreover" would treat the sentence as reinforcing her denial.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Some species of bamboo flower only once every several decades. ______ all the plants of a given species, even those growing on different continents, tend to flower in the same year, after which they die.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Stranger still,","For this reason,","On the other hand,","In summary,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Synchronized flowering across continents is an even more surprising fact, so "Stranger still" fits. "For this reason" signals cause, "On the other hand" signals contrast, and "In summary" signals a conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Romans built roads that lasted for centuries. Their engineers dug deep trenches and layered them with stones of decreasing size, topped by tightly fitted paving slabs. ______ water drained away quickly, and the roads resisted frost damage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Apart from this construction,","Despite this construction,","Because of this construction,","Like this construction,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The layered construction caused the good drainage and frost resistance, so "Because of this construction" fits. "Despite" signals contrast, "Apart from" signals exclusion, and "Like" signals comparison.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The study's authors acknowledged that their sample was small, only forty-two participants. ______ they argued that the effect they observed was large and consistent enough to justify a larger follow-up study.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The authors argue for the finding's value despite the small sample, so "Nevertheless" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, and "Likewise" and "In addition" signal continuation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In most mammals, males are larger than females. Among spotted hyenas, ______ females are larger and more aggressive than males and dominate them in social interactions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["similarly,","however,","therefore,","for example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Spotted hyenas reverse the typical pattern, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "for example" would present hyenas as typical, and "similarly" signals likeness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Fireflies produce light through a chemical reaction that releases almost no heat. ______ nearly all of the energy from the reaction is emitted as light, making firefly light one of the most efficient forms of illumination known.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Still,","On the contrary,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence restates "almost no heat" as "nearly all of the energy is emitted as light," so "In other words" fits. "On the contrary" signals contradiction, "Still" signals concession, and "For example" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many early computer programmers were women, hired in the 1940s and 1950s because programming was considered clerical work. ______ as the field gained prestige and higher salaries in the 1960s and 1970s, the proportion of women in it began to decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For instance,","Similarly,","Later,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence moves forward in time to describe a later change, so "Later" fits. "Thus" signals a result, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The country's population grew steadily throughout the twentieth century. ______ birth rates have fallen sharply since 2000, and demographers project that the population will begin shrinking within the next decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Recently, likewise,","Recently, therefore,","Recently, however,","Recently, for example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Falling birth rates contrast with the steady growth of the previous century, so "however" fits. "Therefore" signals a result, "likewise" signals continuation, and "for example" signals an illustration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Plants cannot run from insects that eat them, but they are far from defenseless. When a caterpillar chews on a tomato leaf, ______ the plant releases chemicals that make its leaves harder to digest and attract wasps that prey on caterpillars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["nevertheless,","in contrast,","for instance,","as a result of this,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The tomato is an example of how plants defend themselves, so "for instance" fits. "In contrast" and "nevertheless" signal opposition. "As a result of this" would make the clause a consequence of plants being far from defenseless, rather than an illustration of it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Great Green Wall is an initiative to restore degraded land across Africa's Sahel region.<br>• It was launched in 2007 by the African Union.<br>• Its original goal was a continuous belt of trees 8,000 km long.<br>• Planners later shifted to a "mosaic" approach: restoring farmland, grazing land, and water sources rather than only planting trees.<br>• Local farmers had found that many planted seedlings died, while native trees regrown from existing roots survived.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the initiative's approach changed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Green Wall now restores farmland, grazing land, and water sources across the Sahel rather than only planting a belt of trees.","Native trees regrown from existing roots survived in the Sahel, while many seedlings planted as part of the Great Green Wall died.","Launched by the African Union in 2007, the Great Green Wall originally aimed to create a continuous belt of trees 8,000 km long stretching all the way across Africa's Sahel region.","After farmers found that planted seedlings often died while regrown native trees survived, the Great Green Wall shifted from a tree belt to a mosaic of restored land."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice links the cause (farmers' observations about seedlings) to the change in approach. The choice beginning "The Great Green Wall…" describes the new approach without a reason, the choice beginning "Native trees regrown from…" gives the reason without the change, and the choice beginning "Launched by the African…" describes only the original goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Kintsugi is a Japanese technique for repairing broken pottery.<br>• Cracks are filled with lacquer mixed with powdered gold or silver.<br>• The repair is meant to be visible rather than hidden.<br>• The practice is associated with the idea that an object's history, including damage, adds to its beauty.<br>• It is thought to have originated in the late fifteenth century.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the philosophy behind kintsugi. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In kintsugi, broken pottery is repaired with lacquer mixed with powdered gold or silver, making the cracks shimmer.","Kintsugi makes repairs visible rather than hiding them, reflecting the idea that an object's history, including its damage, adds to its beauty.","Kintsugi originated in Japan in the late fifteenth century as a technique for repairing broken pottery.","Kintsugi, a Japanese repair technique thought to date to the late fifteenth century, fills cracks in pottery with lacquer mixed with gold or silver."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is the philosophy, and only the correct choice explains the idea that damage adds to beauty. The other choices accurately describe the method or origin but say nothing about the philosophy.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied two groups of sticklebacks, a small fish.<br>• One group lived in a lake with trout, a predator; the other lived in a lake without trout.<br>• Sticklebacks in the trout lake had more bony armor plates.<br>• Sticklebacks in the trout-free lake had fewer plates but grew faster.<br>• Researchers concluded that armor provides protection but has a cost.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's conclusion and the evidence that supports it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers compared sticklebacks living in a lake with trout, a predator, with sticklebacks living in a lake without trout.","Sticklebacks living with trout had more armor plates, while those in a trout-free lake had fewer plates but grew faster, suggesting that armor protects but has a cost.","Sticklebacks in a trout-free lake grew faster than sticklebacks in a lake with trout, which had more armor plates.","Researchers who compared two groups of sticklebacks concluded that the bony armor plates of the fish provide protection against predators but also come with a cost."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal requires both the conclusion and its support. The correct choice gives both. The choice beginning "Researchers who compared two…" gives only the conclusion, the choice beginning "Researchers compared sticklebacks living…" gives only the design, and the choice beginning "Sticklebacks in a trout-free…" gives evidence without the conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Zora Neale Hurston (1891–1960) was a novelist and anthropologist.<br>• She studied anthropology with Franz Boas at Barnard College.<br>• In the late 1920s, she collected folktales and songs in Florida and Louisiana.<br>• Her 1935 book <i>Mules and Men</i> presented this folklore.<br>• Her novel <i>Their Eyes Were Watching God</i> (1937) draws on the speech patterns she recorded.$q$, $q$The student wants to show how Hurston's anthropological work influenced her fiction. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hurston studied anthropology with Franz Boas at Barnard College before collecting folktales and songs in Florida and Louisiana in the late 1920s and publishing them in 1935.","Hurston's novel <i>Their Eyes Were Watching God</i> draws on the speech patterns she recorded while collecting folklore in Florida and Louisiana.","In 1935, Hurston published <i>Mules and Men</i>, a book that presented the folktales and songs she had collected in the South.","Zora Neale Hurston, who lived from 1891 to 1960, was both a novelist and an anthropologist."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice connects her field recordings to a novel. <i>Mules and Men</i> in the choice beginning "In 1935, Hurston published…" is a nonfiction folklore collection, so it doesn't show influence on fiction, and the other choices describe training or her dual career without connecting them.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Venus and Earth are similar in size and mass.<br>• Venus's surface temperature averages about 465°C.<br>• Its atmosphere is about 96% carbon dioxide.<br>• The thick atmosphere traps heat through a runaway greenhouse effect.<br>• Venus is hotter than Mercury, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Venus is hotter than Mercury. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Venus's atmosphere is about 96% carbon dioxide, and its surface temperature averages about 465°C.","Venus, which is similar to Earth in size and mass, has a surface temperature averaging about 465°C.","Venus is hotter than Mercury, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun and Venus is similar in size and mass to Earth, with a surface temperature averaging about 465°C.","Although Mercury is closer to the Sun, Venus is hotter because its thick, carbon dioxide–rich atmosphere traps heat through a runaway greenhouse effect."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is an explanation. Only the correct choice gives the cause, the greenhouse effect of the thick atmosphere. The choice beginning "Venus's atmosphere is about…" lists the facts without linking them, and the choice beginning "Venus is hotter than…" states the comparison without explaining it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Linguists classify about 7,000 languages into roughly 140 families.<br>• The Indo-European family includes English, Hindi, and Russian.<br>• It has about 3.3 billion native speakers.<br>• The Niger-Congo family includes Swahili, Yoruba, and Zulu.<br>• It contains more than 1,500 languages, more than any other family.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the two language families in terms of size. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Both the Indo-European family and the Niger-Congo family are among the roughly 140 language families classified by linguists.","The Indo-European family includes English, Hindi, and Russian, while the Niger-Congo family includes Swahili, Yoruba, and Zulu, among roughly 140 families classified by linguists.","The Indo-European family has about 3.3 billion native speakers, while the Niger-Congo family has more languages than any other family, more than 1,500.","With more than 1,500 languages, Niger-Congo is the largest of the roughly 140 language families that linguists recognize."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice contrasts the two families on two measures of size: number of speakers and number of languages. The choice beginning "The Indo-European family includes…" compares member languages, not size, the choice beginning "With more than 1,500…" describes only one family, and the choice beginning "Both the Indo-European family…" notes a similarity rather than a contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1977, Kenyan biologist Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement.<br>• The movement paid rural women to plant trees.<br>• It has planted more than 51 million trees in Kenya.<br>• The trees provided firewood, reduced soil erosion, and gave women an income.<br>• Maathai won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the benefits of the Green Belt Movement to an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The trees planted by the movement provided firewood, reduced soil erosion, and gave women an income, and its founder later received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004.","Since 1977, the Green Belt Movement has planted more than 51 million trees in Kenya.","The Green Belt Movement, founded in Kenya in 1977, paid rural women to plant trees, which gave them an income while supplying firewood and reducing soil erosion.","Wangari Maathai, who founded the Green Belt Movement in 1977, won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An unfamiliar audience needs the movement introduced, and the goal is its benefits. The correct choice does both. The choice beginning "The trees planted by…" lists benefits but doesn't identify the movement ("the movement" assumes prior knowledge). The others give a prize or a tree count rather than benefits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Antikythera mechanism was found in a shipwreck off a Greek island in 1901.<br>• It dates to roughly the second century BCE.<br>• It contains at least 30 interlocking bronze gears.<br>• It was used to predict the positions of the Sun and Moon and the dates of eclipses.<br>• No device of comparable complexity is known from the following thousand years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how advanced the mechanism was for its time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Built around the second century BCE, the Antikythera mechanism used at least 30 bronze gears, a complexity unmatched by any known device for the next thousand years.","Discovered off a Greek island in 1901, the Antikythera mechanism contains at least 30 interlocking bronze gears.","The Antikythera mechanism, which dates to the second century BCE, was recovered from a shipwreck off a Greek island.","The Antikythera mechanism, found in a shipwreck off a Greek island in 1901, was used to predict the positions of the Sun and Moon as well as the dates of eclipses."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$To emphasize how advanced the device was for its time, the sentence needs its date and a comparison with later technology. Only the correct choice includes the comparison. The choice beginning "Discovered off a Greek…" gives the gear count without establishing that it was unusual for the era.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Most clouds form when air rises and cools.<br>• Contrails are clouds that form behind aircraft.<br>• They form when hot, humid exhaust mixes with cold air at high altitude.<br>• Some contrails disappear within seconds; others spread into cirrus-like clouds lasting hours.<br>• Persistent contrails can trap heat, contributing to warming.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how contrails form differently from most clouds. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While most clouds form when rising air cools, contrails form when an aircraft's hot, humid exhaust mixes with cold air at high altitude.","Persistent contrails, which can spread into cirrus-like clouds, trap heat and contribute to warming, unlike most other clouds.","Contrails, the clouds that form behind aircraft, can either disappear within seconds or spread into cirrus-like clouds lasting hours.","Contrails form when hot, humid exhaust from aircraft mixes with cold air at high altitude, sometimes creating persistent clouds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is a difference in formation. Only the correct choice contrasts both formation processes. The choice beginning "Contrails form when hot…" explains contrail formation without the comparison, the choice beginning "Contrails, the clouds that…" addresses duration, and the choice beginning "Persistent contrails, which can…" makes a comparison about warming that the notes don't support.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers gave two groups of students the same math test.<br>• Group A was told the test measured innate ability.<br>• Group B was told the test measured skills that improve with practice.<br>• Group B students attempted more of the hardest problems.<br>• The two groups' overall scores did not differ significantly.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a finding of the study that might surprise readers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Students who were told that a math test measured skills that improve with practice attempted more of the test's hardest problems.","Researchers gave two groups of students the same math test but told each group something different about what it measured.","Students told that the test measured improvable skills attempted more hard problems, yet their overall scores were no better than those of students told it measured innate ability.","In the study, one group of students was told that a math test measured innate ability, while another group was told that the same test measured skills that improve with practice."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The surprising result is that attempting more hard problems didn't produce higher scores, and only the correct choice combines those findings. The choice beginning "Students who were told…" gives an expected result on its own, and the others describe the design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Hokusai's <i>The Great Wave off Kanagawa</i> is a Japanese woodblock print from around 1831.<br>• It was printed in thousands of copies and sold cheaply.<br>• It used Prussian blue, a synthetic pigment recently imported from Europe.<br>• The print later influenced European artists such as Claude Monet and Vincent van Gogh.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the two-way artistic exchange between Japan and Europe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hokusai's <i>The Great Wave</i> used Prussian blue, a pigment imported from Europe, and in turn influenced European artists such as Monet and van Gogh.","Printed in thousands of copies, Hokusai's <i>The Great Wave</i> used Prussian blue, a synthetic pigment.","Hokusai's <i>The Great Wave</i>, a Japanese woodblock print sold cheaply in thousands of copies around 1831, later influenced European artists such as Claude Monet and Vincent van Gogh.","Hokusai's <i>The Great Wave</i> is a Japanese woodblock print that was sold cheaply in thousands of copies around 1831."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A two-way exchange requires influence in both directions: a European pigment used in Japan, and the Japanese print influencing European artists. Only the correct choice includes both. The choice beginning "Hokusai's The Great Wave…" shows only one direction, and the choice beginning "Printed in thousands of…" omits that the pigment came from Europe.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Axolotls are salamanders native to lakes near Mexico City.<br>• Unlike most amphibians, they keep juvenile features, including gills, into adulthood.<br>• They can regrow lost limbs, parts of their hearts, and even parts of their brains.<br>• Scientists study them to understand tissue regeneration.<br>• Wild populations are critically endangered.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why axolotls are of interest to scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because axolotls can regrow lost limbs and even parts of their hearts and brains, scientists study them to understand how tissue regenerates.","Unlike most amphibians, axolotls keep juvenile features into adulthood and can regrow lost limbs.","Although scientists study axolotls, wild populations of these salamanders are critically endangered.","Axolotls, salamanders native to lakes near Mexico City, keep their gills and other juvenile features into adulthood, unlike most other amphibians."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice connects the regeneration ability to the scientific interest. The choice beginning "Unlike most amphibians, axolotls…" mentions regeneration but doesn't explain the scientific interest, and the others describe appearance or conservation status.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Erie Canal opened in 1825, linking the Hudson River to Lake Erie.<br>• Before the canal, shipping a ton of goods from Buffalo to New York City cost about $100.<br>• After the canal opened, the cost fell to about $10.<br>• Travel time dropped from about three weeks to eight days.<br>• New York City soon became the nation's busiest port.$q$, $q$The student wants to quantify the canal's effect on shipping. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Opened in 1825, the Erie Canal connected the Hudson River with Lake Erie, a distance once traveled in about three weeks.","After the Erie Canal opened, the cost of shipping a ton of goods from Buffalo to New York City fell from about $100 to $10, and travel time dropped from three weeks to eight days.","The Erie Canal, which opened in 1825, linked the Hudson River to Lake Erie and greatly reduced the cost and time of shipping, helping make New York City the nation's busiest port.","The Erie Canal dramatically reduced the cost and time of shipping goods between Buffalo and New York City."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$To quantify means to give numbers. Only the correct choice gives before-and-after figures for both cost and time. The choice beginning "The Erie Canal dramatically…" describes the effect only in general terms, and the others describe the canal's route or later importance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Soprano Leontyne Price was born in Laurel, Mississippi, in 1927.<br>• In 1955, she sang the title role in an NBC television broadcast of <i>Tosca</i>.<br>• She was the first Black singer to perform opera on network television.<br>• In 1961, she made her Metropolitan Opera debut in <i>Il Trovatore</i>.<br>• The audience applauded for 42 minutes after the performance.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the reception of Price's Metropolitan Opera debut. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Leontyne Price, born in Mississippi in 1927, made her Metropolitan Opera debut in <i>Il Trovatore</i> in 1961.","When Leontyne Price made her Metropolitan Opera debut in <i>Il Trovatore</i> in 1961, the audience applauded for 42 minutes.","Six years after her televised <i>Tosca</i>, Leontyne Price made her Metropolitan Opera debut.","In 1955, Leontyne Price became the first Black singer to perform opera on network television, singing the title role in <i>Tosca</i>."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Reception" refers to how the audience responded. Only the correct choice mentions the 42 minutes of applause. The other choices identify the debut or her earlier television milestone without describing a response.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Desert locusts usually live alone and avoid one another.<br>• When crowded, they undergo a change called gregarization.<br>• Gregarious locusts change color, become more active, and seek out other locusts.<br>• The change is triggered by repeated touching of their hind legs.<br>• Swarms can contain billions of locusts and devastate crops.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what triggers locusts to begin swarming. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["When crowding causes repeated touching of their hind legs, desert locusts undergo gregarization, changing from solitary insects into ones that seek each other out.","Desert locusts usually live alone and avoid one another, but sometimes they form enormous swarms.","Swarms of desert locusts can contain billions of insects and devastate crops across large regions.","Desert locusts that undergo gregarization change color, become more active, and seek out other locusts, eventually forming swarms of billions that can devastate crops."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is the trigger. Only the correct choice names it: repeated touching of the hind legs caused by crowding. The choice beginning "Desert locusts that undergo…" describes the change without its cause, and the others describe swarms or the locusts' usual behavior.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers surveyed 1,200 residents of a city about their use of public parks.<br>• Residents living within 400 meters of a park visited an average of 3.1 times per week.<br>• Residents living more than 1 kilometer away visited an average of 0.6 times per week.<br>• Frequent park visitors reported lower stress levels.<br>• The city plans to build parks in neighborhoods currently lacking them.$q$, $q$The student wants to support the city's plan using the survey's findings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The city plans to build new parks in neighborhoods that currently lack them, according to a survey of 1,200 residents.","Residents who visited parks frequently reported lower stress levels than those who visited less often, a survey found.","Since residents near parks visited about five times as often as those living far away, and frequent visitors reported less stress, new parks in underserved neighborhoods could benefit residents.","A survey of 1,200 residents found that those living within 400 meters of a park visited an average of 3.1 times per week, and the city now plans to build parks in neighborhoods lacking them."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Supporting the plan requires linking proximity to use and use to benefits, then applying this to new parks. The correct choice does all three. The other choices present only one finding or misattribute the plan to the survey.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Physicist Chien-Shiung Wu (1912–1997) designed a 1956 experiment involving radioactive cobalt-60.<br>• The experiment tested a principle called parity conservation.<br>• Physicists had assumed nature does not distinguish between left and right.<br>• Wu's results showed that, in certain nuclear decays, nature does.<br>• Theorists Tsung-Dao Lee and Chen Ning Yang, who proposed the test, won the 1957 Nobel Prize; Wu did not.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the significance of Wu's experimental results. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Chien-Shiung Wu, who lived from 1912 to 1997, was a physicist who designed experiments with radioactive materials.","Theorists Lee and Yang, who proposed the test that Wu carried out, received the 1957 Nobel Prize, but Wu did not.","Wu's 1956 experiment overturned a long-held assumption, showing that in certain nuclear decays nature does distinguish between left and right.","In 1956, Chien-Shiung Wu designed an experiment using radioactive cobalt-60 to test parity conservation, a principle proposed for testing by theorists Lee and Yang."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The significance lies in overturning the assumption about left and right, which only the correct choice states. The choice beginning "In 1956, Chien-Shiung Wu…" describes the experiment without its result, and the choice beginning "Theorists Lee and Yang…" concerns recognition rather than the result's significance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mount Everest's height was measured by Indian surveyors in 1856 as 8,840 meters.<br>• A 1955 survey measured it at 8,848 meters.<br>• A 2020 joint survey by China and Nepal measured 8,848.86 meters.<br>• Measurements differ partly because of snow depth and differing reference points for sea level.<br>• The peak is also rising slowly because of tectonic activity.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why measurements of Everest's height have varied. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Measurements of Everest's height have varied partly because surveyors have dealt differently with snow depth and with where sea level is defined to be.","Everest's height was measured at 8,840 meters in 1856, 8,848 meters in 1955, and 8,848.86 meters in 2020.","Because of tectonic activity, Mount Everest is slowly rising, so it is taller today than in 1856.","A 2020 joint survey by China and Nepal measured Mount Everest at 8,848.86 meters, nearly nine meters more than the figure Indian surveyors reported in 1856."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice gives the reasons for the variation. The choice beginning "Everest's height was measured…" shows that the measurements varied but not why, the choice beginning "A 2020 joint survey…" reports a single measurement, and the choice beginning "Because of tectonic activity…" gives a factor (rising) that the notes don't present as the reason the measurements differ.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Novelist Kazuo Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki, Japan, in 1954.<br>• His family moved to England when he was five.<br>• His early novels, such as <i>A Pale View of Hills</i>, are set partly in Japan.<br>• <i>The Remains of the Day</i> (1989) is narrated by an English butler.<br>• He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2017.$q$, $q$The student wants to show the range of Ishiguro's settings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ishiguro, who won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Literature, wrote <i>The Remains of the Day</i> in 1989.","Ishiguro's novels range from <i>A Pale View of Hills</i>, set partly in Japan, to <i>The Remains of the Day</i>, narrated by an English butler.","Born in Nagasaki in 1954, Ishiguro moved to England with his family at the age of five.","Ishiguro's early novels, such as <i>A Pale View of Hills</i>, are set partly in Japan, where he was born in 1954 before moving to England at age five."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Showing range requires contrasting settings from different works. Only the correct choice pairs a Japanese setting with an English one. The choice beginning "Ishiguro's early novels, such…" describes one setting only, and the others concern biography or awards.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers compared two methods for teaching fractions.<br>• Method 1 used number lines; Method 2 used pie diagrams.<br>• After six weeks, Method 1 students scored 18 points higher on a fractions test.<br>• The gap was largest on problems comparing fractions with different denominators.<br>• Both groups scored similarly on problems with like denominators.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify where the advantage of number lines was greatest. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Students taught with number lines outperformed those taught with pie diagrams most on problems comparing fractions with different denominators.","After six weeks, students taught with number lines scored 18 points higher on a fractions test than students who had been taught with pie diagrams instead.","Researchers compared two methods for teaching fractions, one using number lines and one using pie diagrams.","Students taught with number lines and students taught with pie diagrams scored similarly on problems with like denominators."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is the location of the greatest advantage, which only the correct choice identifies. The choice beginning "After six weeks, students…" gives the overall advantage, the choice beginning "Students taught with number lines and…" reports where there was no advantage, and the choice beginning "Researchers compared two methods…" describes the design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Tarantula hawks are wasps that hunt tarantulas.<br>• A female paralyzes a tarantula with her sting.<br>• She drags it to a burrow and lays a single egg on it.<br>• The larva feeds on the living but paralyzed spider.<br>• Adult tarantula hawks feed mainly on flower nectar.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the diets of tarantula hawk larvae and adults. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A female tarantula hawk paralyzes a tarantula and lays a single egg on it, and the larva feeds on the spider.","Tarantula hawks paralyze tarantulas with their stings and then drag them into burrows.","Adult tarantula hawks, wasps known for hunting tarantulas, feed mainly on flower nectar.","Tarantula hawk larvae feed on paralyzed tarantulas, but adults feed mainly on flower nectar."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice gives both diets and contrasts them. The choice beginning "A female tarantula hawk…" gives only the larval diet, the choice beginning "Adult tarantula hawks, wasps…" only the adult diet, and the choice beginning "Tarantula hawks paralyze tarantulas…" describes hunting behavior.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Fast fashion refers to clothing produced quickly and cheaply to follow trends.<br>• The average consumer buys 60% more clothing than in 2000.<br>• Each item is kept about half as long.<br>• The fashion industry produces about 10% of global carbon emissions.<br>• Some brands now offer repair services and resale programs.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that consumer habits contribute to fast fashion's environmental impact. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Since 2000, consumers have bought 60% more clothing and kept each item half as long, habits that fuel an industry responsible for about 10% of global carbon emissions.","Some fashion brands have begun offering repair services and resale programs to customers who want to keep clothing longer.","The fashion industry produces about 10% of global carbon emissions, though some brands now offer repair services and resale programs intended to keep clothing in use longer.","Fast fashion refers to clothing that is produced quickly and cheaply in order to follow the latest trends."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The argument requires both consumer habits and the environmental impact, linked together. Only the correct choice provides both. The choice beginning "The fashion industry produces…" gives the impact but no consumer habits, and the others define the term or describe brand responses.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sign languages are full natural languages with their own grammar.<br>• American Sign Language (ASL) and British Sign Language (BSL) are mutually unintelligible.<br>• ASL is more closely related to French Sign Language.<br>• This is because a French teacher, Laurent Clerc, helped found the first American school for deaf students in 1817.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a surprising relationship between sign languages. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sign languages, including American Sign Language and French Sign Language, are full natural languages with their own grammar.","In 1817, French teacher Laurent Clerc helped found the first school for deaf students in the United States.","American Sign Language and British Sign Language are mutually unintelligible, even though both are full natural languages with their own grammar and are used in English-speaking countries.","Although the United States and Britain share a spoken language, ASL is closer to French Sign Language, because a French teacher helped found the first American school for deaf students."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The surprising relationship is ASL's closeness to French rather than British Sign Language, and the correct choice states and explains it. The choice beginning "American Sign Language and…" states that ASL and BSL differ without the explanation, and the others give background only. The shared spoken language is common knowledge used to frame the surprise.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sociologist Mira Castell interviewed 80 remote workers.<br>• 70% said they were more productive at home.<br>• 55% said they felt more isolated from colleagues.<br>• Workers who had video calls at least three times a week reported less isolation.<br>• Castell recommends regular video check-ins for remote teams.$q$, $q$The student wants to present Castell's recommendation and the evidence behind it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Castell, a sociologist, recommends that remote teams schedule regular video check-ins.","Because remote workers who had video calls at least three times a week reported less isolation, Castell recommends regular video check-ins for remote teams.","Castell found that 70% of the remote workers she interviewed said they were more productive at home.","Of the 80 remote workers Castell interviewed, 55% said that they felt more isolated from colleagues, even though 70% said they were more productive at home."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice gives the recommendation and the evidence that directly supports it. The choice beginning "Castell, a sociologist, recommends…" gives the recommendation alone, and the choices beginning "Castell found that 70%…" and "Of the 80 remote…" give findings that don't support the recommendation by themselves.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Tasmanian devil is a carnivorous marsupial found only in Tasmania.<br>• Since 1996, a contagious facial cancer has killed about 80% of the population.<br>• The cancer spreads when devils bite one another during feeding and mating.<br>• Some devil populations now show genetic changes linked to cancer resistance.<br>• These changes appeared within just a few generations.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly the devils have responded to the disease. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The facial cancer affecting Tasmanian devils spreads when the animals bite one another during feeding and mating.","Since 1996, a contagious facial cancer spread by biting has killed about 80% of Tasmania's devil population.","Some populations of Tasmanian devils, carnivorous marsupials found only in Tasmania, now show genetic changes linked to cancer resistance.","Within just a few generations, some Tasmanian devil populations have developed genetic changes linked to resistance to the facial cancer."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal concerns the speed of the response, and only the correct choice mentions "just a few generations." The choice beginning "Some populations of Tasmanian…" describes the resistance without its speed, and the others describe the disease.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Bauhaus was a German school of art and design (1919–1933).<br>• It aimed to unite fine art with craft and industrial production.<br>• Students studied weaving, metalwork, and furniture design alongside painting.<br>• Many of its designs, such as tubular steel chairs, were meant for mass production.<br>• The school closed under political pressure in 1933.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the Bauhaus's goal with a specific example. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Bauhaus, a German school of art and design that operated from 1919 until 1933, aimed to unite fine art with craft and industry.","The Bauhaus, which aimed to unite fine art with craft and industrial production, closed under political pressure in 1933.","At the Bauhaus, students studied weaving, metalwork, and furniture design alongside painting until the school closed in 1933.","Seeking to unite art with industrial production, the Bauhaus produced designs such as tubular steel chairs intended for mass manufacture."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice states the goal and supplies a concrete example that embodies it. The choice beginning "The Bauhaus, a German…" states the goal without an example, the choice beginning "At the Bauhaus, students…" lists subjects without linking them to the goal, and the fourth pairs the goal with the school's closing.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ecologists studied two types of urban rooftops in Toronto.<br>• Conventional rooftops reached surface temperatures of up to 70°C in summer.<br>• Green roofs, covered with plants, reached a maximum of about 30°C.<br>• Green roofs also absorbed about 60% of rainfall.<br>• Installation costs for green roofs are higher.$q$, $q$The student wants to compare the two rooftops' summer temperatures. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although green roofs cost more to install than conventional roofs, they absorbed about 60% of rainfall in a Toronto study.","Ecologists in Toronto studied two kinds of rooftops: conventional rooftops and green roofs covered with plants.","In summer, conventional rooftops in Toronto reached surface temperatures of up to 70°C, while green roofs peaked at about 30°C.","Green roofs in Toronto, which are covered with plants, reached a maximum surface temperature of about 30°C during the summer months."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comparison requires both temperatures. Only the correct choice gives them. The choice beginning "Green roofs in Toronto…" gives only the green roof's temperature, the third discusses rainfall and cost, and the choice beginning "Ecologists in Toronto studied…" describes the study's subjects.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ada Lovelace (1815–1852) wrote notes on Charles Babbage's proposed Analytical Engine.<br>• Her notes included a method for calculating Bernoulli numbers with the machine.<br>• This is often called the first published computer program.<br>• She also suggested the machine could manipulate symbols, such as musical notes, not just numbers.<br>• The Analytical Engine was never built.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Lovelace saw possibilities beyond calculation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ada Lovelace, who lived from 1815 to 1852, wrote notes on a calculating machine proposed by Charles Babbage.","Lovelace's notes on the Analytical Engine included a method for calculating Bernoulli numbers, often called the first published program.","Lovelace suggested that Babbage's Analytical Engine could manipulate symbols such as musical notes, not merely perform numerical calculations.","Although Babbage's Analytical Engine was never built, Lovelace wrote detailed notes describing how the machine could be used."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice describes her vision beyond numbers. The choice beginning "Lovelace's notes on the…" describes a calculation, which is precisely what the goal asks to go beyond, and the others give background.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Atacama Desert in Chile is one of the driest places on Earth.<br>• Some weather stations there have never recorded rain.<br>• Its high altitude and clear skies make it ideal for astronomy.<br>• It hosts major observatories, including ALMA, a radio telescope array.<br>• Its soil is so dry that NASA has tested Mars rovers there.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Atacama's dryness makes it useful to scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Atacama is one of the driest places on Earth, it hosts major observatories and has been used by NASA scientists.","The Atacama Desert in Chile, one of the driest places on Earth, includes weather stations that have never recorded rain.","The Atacama's soil is so dry that NASA has used the desert to test rovers designed to explore Mars.","The Atacama's high altitude and clear skies have made it home to major observatories, including the ALMA radio telescope array."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice links dryness specifically to a scientific use. The choice beginning "The Atacama's high altitude…" attributes the astronomy to altitude and clear skies rather than dryness, the choice beginning "The Atacama Desert in…" describes dryness without a use, and the fourth vaguely links dryness to uses without explaining how.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Psychologists asked 300 people to predict how happy they would be a year after a major event.<br>• Participants predicted that winning a prize would raise their happiness greatly.<br>• They predicted that losing a job would lower it greatly.<br>• A year later, happiness had shifted far less than predicted in both cases.<br>• Researchers call this tendency "impact bias."$q$, $q$The student wants to define impact bias for an audience unfamiliar with the term. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Impact bias is the tendency to overestimate how much a major event, good or bad, will change one's happiness over time.","After a year, participants' happiness had shifted far less than they had predicted, whether they had won a prize or lost a job.","Researchers use the term \"impact bias\" to describe a tendency they observed among 300 people asked to predict their future happiness.","In one study, people predicted that winning a prize would greatly raise their happiness, but a year later it had changed far less."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Defining the term requires stating what it is. Only the correct choice gives a general definition. The choices beginning "In one study, people…" and "After a year, participants'…" describe results without naming the bias, and the choice beginning "Researchers use the term…" names the term without explaining it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Mongol Empire's postal relay system was called the Yam.<br>• Stations were placed about every 30–50 kilometers.<br>• Riders could change horses at each station.<br>• Messages could travel up to 300 kilometers per day.<br>• Foreign travelers, including Marco Polo, described the system.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Yam achieved its speed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because riders could change horses at stations spaced every 30–50 kilometers, messages in the Mongol Yam could travel up to 300 kilometers per day.","Foreign travelers, including Marco Polo, described the Yam, a postal relay system operated by the Mongol Empire.","The Mongol Empire's postal relay system, called the Yam, could carry messages as far as 300 kilometers in a single day, as foreign travelers such as Marco Polo described.","The Yam had stations every 30–50 kilometers, and foreign visitors such as Marco Polo wrote about the system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct choice explains the mechanism behind the speed: fresh horses at closely spaced stations. The choice beginning "The Mongol Empire's postal…" gives the speed without the cause, and the others mention travelers' accounts.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two studies examined the effect of background music on reading.<br>• Study 1 (2015) found that instrumental music did not affect reading comprehension.<br>• Study 2 (2019) found that music with lyrics reduced comprehension by 12%.<br>• Both studies used college students as participants.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the studies' findings as consistent with a single conclusion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A 2015 study found that instrumental music did not affect reading comprehension, but a 2019 study found that background music did reduce it, so the studies' findings conflict.","A 2019 study found that background music with lyrics reduced college students' reading comprehension by 12%.","Both a 2015 study and a 2019 study used college students as participants to examine the effect of background music on reading.","Taken together, the studies suggest that lyrics, rather than music itself, interfere with reading: instrumental music had no effect, while music with lyrics reduced comprehension."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice synthesizes both findings into one conclusion about lyrics. The choice beginning "A 2015 study found…" presents the studies as conflicting by omitting the lyrics distinction, the choice beginning "Both a 2015 study…" describes methods, and the choice beginning "A 2019 study found…" reports one study only.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Lake Baikal in Siberia is the world's deepest lake, at 1,642 meters.<br>• It holds about 20% of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.<br>• It is about 25 million years old, the oldest lake on Earth.<br>• About 80% of its animal species are found nowhere else.<br>• Scientists link this high endemism to the lake's age and isolation.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many of Baikal's species are unique. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Scientists link the fact that about 80% of Baikal's animal species are found nowhere else to the lake's great age and isolation.","Lake Baikal holds about 20% of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water and is home to many unique animal species.","About 80% of the animal species in Lake Baikal, the world's deepest lake, are found nowhere else on Earth.","Lake Baikal is about 25 million years old, making it the oldest lake on Earth, and it is also the deepest at 1,642 meters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct choice gives the explanation scientists offer. The choice beginning "About 80% of the…" states the fact without a cause, and the others list impressive figures without linking them to endemism.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Kente is a woven cloth originating with the Asante people of Ghana.<br>• It is woven in narrow strips, which are then sewn together.<br>• Each pattern and color has a name and meaning.<br>• Gold represents wealth; green represents growth and renewal.<br>• It was traditionally worn by royalty on important occasions.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how meaning is conveyed through kente. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kente cloth was traditionally worn by Asante royalty on important occasions, often featuring gold and green.","Kente cloth, which originated with the Asante people of Ghana and was traditionally worn by royalty, is woven in narrow strips that are later sewn together.","Originating with the Asante people of Ghana, kente is a woven cloth whose strips are sewn together into larger pieces.","In kente cloth, each pattern and color carries a name and meaning; gold, for example, represents wealth, and green represents growth and renewal."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice explains that meaning lies in named patterns and colors, with examples. The choice beginning "Kente cloth was traditionally…" mentions colors without their meanings, and the others describe construction or origins.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A team tested a new water filter in 12 villages.<br>• Before the filter, 34% of children had diarrheal illness each month.<br>• After one year, the rate dropped to 19%.<br>• In 12 comparison villages without the filter, the rate stayed near 33%.<br>• The filter costs about $15 per household.$q$, $q$The student wants to show that the drop in illness was likely caused by the filter. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 12 villages without the filter, the monthly rate of diarrheal illness among children stayed near 33% throughout the year.","After one year of using the new water filter, which costs about $15 per household, the rate of diarrheal illness among children in 12 villages dropped from 34% to 19%.","The new water filter, which costs about $15 per household, was tested in 12 villages, where it reduced childhood illness.","Illness rates fell from 34% to 19% in villages that received the filter but stayed near 33% in comparable villages without it, suggesting the filter caused the drop."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Showing causation requires the comparison group: illness dropped only where the filter was used. Only the correct choice includes both groups. The choice beginning "After one year of…" reports the drop without a comparison, which leaves other explanations open.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Hedy Lamarr (1914–2000) was a Hollywood film star.<br>• During World War II, she co-invented a frequency-hopping radio system with composer George Antheil.<br>• The system was designed to keep torpedo guidance signals from being jammed.<br>• The U.S. Navy did not adopt it during the war.<br>• Related techniques are now used in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the lasting impact of Lamarr's invention. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["During World War II, Lamarr and composer George Antheil co-invented a frequency-hopping system to prevent jamming of torpedo signals.","Techniques related to the frequency-hopping system that Lamarr co-invented during World War II are used today in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.","Although Lamarr co-invented a frequency-hopping radio system, the U.S. Navy did not adopt it during World War II.","Hedy Lamarr, a Hollywood film star who lived from 1914 to 2000, was also the co-inventor of a radio system."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lasting impact concerns modern uses, which only the correct choice mentions. The choice beginning "During World War II…" describes the invention's original purpose, the choice beginning "Although Lamarr co-invented a…" its lack of wartime adoption, and the choice beginning "Hedy Lamarr, a Hollywood…" her dual career.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers placed identical wallets on streets in 40 countries.<br>• Some wallets held no money; others held about $13.<br>• In 38 of 40 countries, wallets with money were returned more often than empty ones.<br>• Economists had predicted the opposite, expecting people to keep money.<br>• The researchers suggest that people dislike seeing themselves as thieves.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's results as contradicting expectations. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In a study spanning 40 countries, researchers placed identical wallets on streets, some empty and some holding about $13, to see how often people would return them.","The researchers suggest that people return wallets containing money because they dislike seeing themselves as thieves.","In 38 of 40 countries, wallets that held about $13 were returned more often than wallets that held no money at all.","Contrary to economists' predictions, wallets containing money were returned more often than empty wallets in 38 of the 40 countries studied."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice presents the result together with the expectation it contradicts. The choice beginning "In 38 of 40…" gives the result without the expectation, the choice beginning "In a study spanning…" describes the design, and the choice beginning "The researchers suggest that…" gives an interpretation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Glass frogs live in Central and South American rainforests.<br>• Their skin is partly transparent, revealing internal organs.<br>• When sleeping, they pack about 89% of their red blood cells into their liver.<br>• This makes their bodies two to three times more transparent.<br>• The transparency is thought to help them hide from predators.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the mechanism that increases glass frogs' transparency. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Glass frogs, which live in Central and South American rainforests, have partly transparent skin that reveals their internal organs and may help them hide from predators.","Glass frogs become two to three times more transparent while sleeping, which may help them avoid predators.","The transparency of glass frogs is thought to help them hide from predators while they sleep in rainforest leaves.","While sleeping, glass frogs pack about 89% of their red blood cells into their liver, making their bodies two to three times more transparent."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The mechanism is hiding red blood cells in the liver, which only the correct choice describes. The choice beginning "Glass frogs become two…" states the effect without the mechanism, and the others describe appearance or purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The printing press reached Venice around 1469.<br>• By 1500, Venice had about 150 print shops, more than any other European city.<br>• Aldus Manutius, a Venetian printer, introduced small, portable books.<br>• He also popularized italic type, which fit more words on a page.<br>• These innovations made books cheaper and easier to carry.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Aldus Manutius changed books. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By 1500, Venice, where Aldus Manutius worked as a printer, had about 150 print shops, more than any other European city.","Aldus Manutius introduced small, portable books and popularized space-saving italic type, innovations that made books cheaper and easier to carry.","Venetian printer Aldus Manutius popularized italic type, a typeface that was developed to fit more words on each page.","The printing press reached Venice around 1469, and by 1500 the city had about 150 print shops, including that of the Venetian printer Aldus Manutius."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice describes both innovations and their effect on books. The choice beginning "Venetian printer Aldus Manutius…" describes only one innovation, the choice beginning "The printing press reached…" mentions small books without their effect, and the choice beginning "By 1500, Venice, where…" concerns Venice's print shops.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Nurse Florence Nightingale served in the Crimean War (1853–1856).<br>• She found that more soldiers died from disease than from battle wounds.<br>• She used a "rose diagram" to show monthly causes of death.<br>• Her visual data persuaded officials to improve hospital sanitation.<br>• Death rates in military hospitals fell sharply afterward.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how Nightingale used data to bring about change. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Florence Nightingale, who served as a nurse in the Crimean War, created a type of chart known as a rose diagram.","During the Crimean War, Nightingale found that more soldiers died from disease than from wounds suffered in battle, and she recorded the monthly causes of death.","After the Crimean War, death rates in military hospitals fell sharply, in part because of improved sanitation.","Nightingale's rose diagram, showing that disease killed more soldiers than wounds did, persuaded officials to improve hospital sanitation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice connects her data visualization to the resulting policy change. The choice beginning "During the Crimean War…" gives a finding without the change, the choice beginning "Florence Nightingale, who served…" names the chart without its effect, and the choice beginning "After the Crimean War…" gives an outcome without her data.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The kakapo is a flightless, nocturnal parrot native to New Zealand.<br>• It is the world's heaviest parrot, weighing up to 4 kilograms.<br>• Introduced predators reduced it to 51 birds by 1995.<br>• Conservationists moved all kakapo to predator-free islands.<br>• The population had grown to about 250 by 2022.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the success of the conservation effort. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The kakapo, a flightless and nocturnal parrot native to New Zealand, is the world's heaviest parrot species.","Introduced predators had reduced the kakapo population to just 51 birds by 1995, prompting conservationists to move all of the remaining birds to islands.","Conservationists moved every remaining kakapo, a flightless New Zealand parrot, to islands free of predators.","After conservationists moved all kakapo to predator-free islands, the population grew from 51 birds in 1995 to about 250 by 2022."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Success is shown by the population's recovery after the intervention, which only the correct choice reports. The choice beginning "Conservationists moved every remaining…" describes the effort without its result, and the choice beginning "Introduced predators had reduced…" describes the decline.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Author Octavia Butler (1947–2006) wrote science fiction.<br>• Her novel <i>Kindred</i> (1979) sends a Black woman from 1970s California back to a Maryland plantation in the early 1800s.<br>• Butler said she wanted readers to feel slavery rather than just learn about it.<br>• <i>Kindred</i> is widely taught in schools.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain Butler's purpose in using time travel in <i>Kindred</i>. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["<i>Kindred</i>, which Butler published in 1979 and which is now widely taught, sends a Black woman from 1970s California back to a Maryland plantation.","Butler's 1979 novel <i>Kindred</i>, a work of science fiction about slavery, is now widely taught in schools.","By sending a modern woman back to a nineteenth-century plantation, Butler hoped to make readers feel slavery rather than just learn about it.","Octavia Butler, who lived from 1947 to 2006, used time travel in her science fiction novel <i>Kindred</i>."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice connects the time-travel premise to Butler's stated purpose. The choice beginning "Kindred, which Butler published…" describes the plot without the purpose, and the others give publication or biographical details.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A 2021 survey asked 2,000 adults where they get news.<br>• 48% said social media at least sometimes.<br>• Among adults aged 18–29, the figure was 70%.<br>• Among adults 65 and older, it was 26%.<br>• Television remained the most common source overall.$q$, $q$The student wants to show how news habits differ by age. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A 2021 survey of 2,000 adults found that television remained the most common source of news among those surveyed.","In a 2021 survey, 70% of adults aged 18–29 got news from social media at least sometimes, compared with only 26% of those 65 and older.","In a 2021 survey, 70% of adults aged 18–29 said that they got news from social media at least some of the time.","In a 2021 survey of 2,000 adults, 48% said they got news from social media at least sometimes, though television was most common."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Showing a difference by age requires figures for at least two age groups, which only the correct choice provides. The choice beginning "In a 2021 survey, 70% of adults aged 18–29 said…" gives one group, and the others report overall figures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Stonehenge's large sarsen stones came from about 25 km away.<br>• Its smaller bluestones came from the Preseli Hills in Wales, about 225 km away.<br>• The bluestones weigh 2–5 metric tons each.<br>• How they were moved remains debated.<br>• One theory proposes sledges pulled over wooden rails.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the challenge of moving the bluestones. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["One theory about Stonehenge's construction proposes that stones were moved on sledges pulled over wooden rails.","The bluestones at Stonehenge came from the Preseli Hills in Wales, which are located about 225 km from the monument.","Each weighing 2–5 metric tons, Stonehenge's bluestones were somehow moved about 225 km from Wales, a feat whose method is still debated.","Stonehenge's larger sarsen stones came from about 25 km away, while its smaller bluestones came from the Preseli Hills in Wales, about 225 km away."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Emphasizing the challenge calls for weight, distance, and the unresolved method together, as in the correct choice. The choice beginning "The bluestones at Stonehenge…" gives distance alone, the choice beginning "Stonehenge's larger sarsen stones…" compares sources, and the choice beginning "One theory about Stonehenge's…" presents a proposed solution.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Dandelion seeds travel on a tuft of bristles called a pappus.<br>• Researchers found that air flowing through the pappus forms a stable vortex ring above it.<br>• This "separated vortex ring" had never been observed in nature before.<br>• It allows seeds to travel up to 150 km in some conditions.<br>• The pappus is about 90% empty space.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what is new about the researchers' discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Air flowing through the pappus of a dandelion seed, which is about 90% empty space, forms a stable vortex ring that allows the seed to travel up to 150 km.","The pappus, a tuft of bristles that carries dandelion seeds through the air, is about 90% empty space.","The researchers found that air passing through a dandelion seed's pappus forms a stable vortex ring above it, a type of flow never before observed in nature.","Dandelion seeds can sometimes travel as far as 150 km, carried by a tuft of bristles called a pappus."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$What is new is that this kind of vortex had never been seen in nature, which only the correct choice states. The choice beginning "Air flowing through the…" describes the vortex and its effect without its novelty, and the others give structural or distance facts.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1848, the Seneca Falls Convention issued the Declaration of Sentiments.<br>• It was modeled on the Declaration of Independence.<br>• It stated that "all men and women are created equal."<br>• It demanded women's right to vote, among other rights.<br>• Women in the U.S. gained the national right to vote in 1920.$q$, $q$The student wants to show how the Declaration of Sentiments drew on an earlier document. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Issued at the Seneca Falls Convention in 1848, the Declaration of Sentiments stated that \"all men and women are created equal.\"","Women's right to vote, demanded in the 1848 Declaration of Sentiments, was finally secured nationally in 1920.","The Declaration of Sentiments, issued in 1848 at the Seneca Falls Convention, demanded women's right to vote, which women nationally gained in 1920, among other rights.","Modeled on the Declaration of Independence, the 1848 Declaration of Sentiments adapted its famous phrase to state that \"all men and women are created equal.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice names the model and shows how its language was adapted. The fourth quotes the phrase without identifying the earlier document, and the others describe demands or outcomes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Urban foxes in London were compared with rural foxes.<br>• Urban foxes had shorter, wider snouts.<br>• Their skulls resembled those of domesticated dogs.<br>• Researchers suggest the shape suits scavenging food waste rather than hunting.<br>• The changes appeared over a relatively short period.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the researchers' explanation for the difference in skull shape. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Compared with rural foxes, foxes living in London had shorter, wider snouts and skulls resembling those of dogs.","Researchers suggest that urban foxes' shorter, wider snouts suit scavenging food waste rather than hunting prey.","The skulls of urban foxes in London resemble those of domesticated dogs more than those of rural foxes.","Changes in the skull shape of London's urban foxes appeared over a relatively short period of time."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice gives the researchers' explanation. The others describe the difference or its timing without explaining it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tracked 500 bumblebees fitted with tiny radio tags.<br>• Bees from colonies near pesticide-treated fields made foraging trips 25% shorter.<br>• They brought back 30% less pollen per trip.<br>• Colonies near untreated fields grew normally.<br>• The pesticide is widely used on canola crops.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the effect of the pesticide on foraging. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Bumblebees from colonies near pesticide-treated fields made 25% shorter foraging trips and brought back 30% less pollen per trip.","Researchers tracked 500 bumblebees fitted with tiny radio tags to study a pesticide widely used on canola crops.","Bumblebee colonies located near fields that had not been treated with the pesticide grew normally, the researchers found.","The pesticide, which is widely used on canola crops, was studied by researchers who tracked bumblebees near treated fields."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct choice reports the measured effects on foraging. The choice beginning "Bumblebee colonies located near…" describes untreated colonies, and the others describe methods or the pesticide's use.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Philosopher Simone Weil (1909–1943) wrote about attention.<br>• She argued that attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity.<br>• She believed school exercises were valuable mainly for training attention.<br>• This was true, she said, even if a student never solved the problem.<br>• Her essays were published after her death.$q$, $q$The student wants to present Weil's unusual view of schoolwork. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Weil argued that attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity, a view she developed in essays.","Weil, a philosopher whose essays were published after her death, wrote extensively about the nature of attention, which she considered the rarest form of generosity.","Simone Weil, who lived from 1909 to 1943, believed that schoolwork could be valuable for students.","Weil argued that school exercises were valuable mainly for training attention, even when a student never solved the problem."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The unusual element is that the value lies in training attention regardless of success, which only the correct choice states. The choice beginning "Simone Weil, who lived…" is vague, the choice beginning "Weil argued that attention…" concerns generosity, and the choice beginning "Weil, a philosopher whose…" gives background.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Saturn's moon Enceladus is about 500 km across.<br>• Its south pole sprays plumes of water vapor and ice into space.<br>• The Cassini spacecraft flew through the plumes in 2008 and later.<br>• Cassini detected salt, silica, and organic molecules in the plumes.<br>• Scientists infer a global saltwater ocean beneath the icy crust.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how scientists learned about Enceladus's hidden ocean. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Plumes of water vapor and ice spray into space from the south pole of Enceladus, one of Saturn's moons.","Scientists believe that Saturn's moon Enceladus, which is about 500 km across and sprays plumes of water vapor and ice, has a global saltwater ocean beneath its crust.","By flying through plumes erupting from Enceladus's south pole, Cassini detected salt and other materials that led scientists to infer an ocean beneath the ice.","The Cassini spacecraft flew through plumes from Enceladus in 2008 and detected salt, silica, and organic molecules."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice connects the method (sampling the plumes) to the inference about the ocean. The choice beginning "The Cassini spacecraft flew…" describes the sampling without the inference, and the choice beginning "Scientists believe that Saturn's…" gives the inference without the method.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mexican muralist Diego Rivera painted <i>Detroit Industry</i> in 1932–1933.<br>• The murals cover all four walls of a courtyard at the Detroit Institute of Arts.<br>• They depict workers at the Ford River Rouge automobile plant.<br>• Rivera spent months sketching inside the plant.<br>• Some critics at the time called the murals un-American; others praised them.$q$, $q$The student wants to indicate how Rivera prepared for the murals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Rivera's <i>Detroit Industry</i> murals, which cover a courtyard's four walls, depict workers at Ford's River Rouge plant.","When Rivera's <i>Detroit Industry</i> murals were unveiled, some critics called them un-American while others praised them.","Diego Rivera painted his <i>Detroit Industry</i> murals at the Detroit Institute of Arts in 1932 and 1933.","Before painting <i>Detroit Industry</i>, Rivera spent months sketching workers inside Ford's River Rouge automobile plant."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice describes his preparation. The others describe the murals' subject, reception, or date.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers trained dogs to sniff samples from people with and without COVID-19.<br>• The dogs correctly identified infected samples 94% of the time.<br>• A standard rapid antigen test was correct about 80% of the time in the same study.<br>• The dogs worked faster than laboratory tests.<br>• Each dog required weeks of training.$q$, $q$The student wants to compare the accuracy of the dogs with that of the rapid test. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers trained dogs to sniff samples from people with and without COVID-19 and compared them with a rapid test.","Although each dog needed weeks of training, the dogs in the study worked faster than standard laboratory tests.","In the study, trained dogs identified infected samples correctly 94% of the time, compared with about 80% for a standard rapid antigen test.","Trained dogs identified samples from people infected with COVID-19 correctly 94% of the time, working faster than laboratory tests, though each dog required weeks of training."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An accuracy comparison needs both accuracy figures, which only the correct choice provides. The choice beginning "Trained dogs identified samples…" compares speed rather than accuracy, and the choice beginning "Researchers trained dogs to…" describes the design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The city of Curitiba, Brazil, launched a bus rapid transit (BRT) system in 1974.<br>• Buses run in dedicated lanes.<br>• Passengers pay before boarding at tube-shaped stations.<br>• These features let buses operate nearly as fast as subways at a fraction of the cost.<br>• More than 150 cities have since adopted BRT systems.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Curitiba's system was efficient. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Curitiba's bus rapid transit system, launched in 1974, operates nearly as fast as a subway at a fraction of the cost.","Passengers in Curitiba's bus rapid transit system wait for buses at distinctive tube-shaped stations.","With dedicated lanes and payment before boarding, Curitiba's buses could operate nearly as fast as subways at a fraction of the cost.","Curitiba, Brazil, launched its bus rapid transit system with tube-shaped stations in 1974, and more than 150 cities have since adopted similar systems."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An explanation of efficiency needs the features that produce it, as in the correct choice. The choice beginning "Curitiba's bus rapid transit…" states the efficiency without the reasons, and the others give history or station design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Linguist Lera Boroditsky studied speakers of Kuuk Thaayorre, an Aboriginal Australian language.<br>• The language uses cardinal directions (north, south, east, west) instead of "left" and "right."<br>• Speakers say things like "There's an ant on your southeast leg."<br>• Speakers stayed oriented even in unfamiliar places.<br>• Boroditsky argues that language can shape how people think about space.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the Kuuk Thaayorre example to support Boroditsky's argument. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In Kuuk Thaayorre, an Aboriginal Australian language, speakers say things like \"There's an ant on your southeast leg.\"","Unlike English, the Aboriginal Australian language Kuuk Thaayorre uses cardinal directions instead of \"left\" and \"right,\" so speakers say things like \"There's an ant on your southeast leg.\"","Kuuk Thaayorre speakers, whose language uses cardinal directions instead of \"left\" and \"right,\" stay oriented even in unfamiliar places, supporting the idea that language shapes spatial thinking.","Linguist Lera Boroditsky argues that the languages people speak can shape the ways they think about space."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Supporting the argument requires linking the language feature to a cognitive effect, and then to the argument. Only the correct choice does so. The choices beginning "In Kuuk Thaayorre, an…" and "Unlike English, the Aboriginal…" describe the language, and the choice beginning "Linguist Lera Boroditsky argues…" states the argument without evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mansa Musa ruled the Mali Empire in the early fourteenth century.<br>• In 1324, he made a pilgrimage to Mecca with a caravan of thousands.<br>• He gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value there dropped for years.<br>• His journey made Mali famous in Europe and the Middle East.<br>• A 1375 European map shows him holding a gold nugget.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the scale of Mansa Musa's wealth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mansa Musa's pilgrimage made Mali famous, and a 1375 European map shows him holding a gold nugget.","Mansa Musa, who ruled the Mali Empire in the early fourteenth century, made a pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324.","In 1324, Mansa Musa traveled to Mecca with a caravan made up of thousands of people.","During his 1324 pilgrimage, Mansa Musa gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value there dropped for years."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The gold that depressed prices in Cairo best shows the scale of his wealth. The choice beginning "In 1324, Mansa Musa…" gives the caravan's size, which is suggestive but doesn't directly show wealth, and the others give dates or fame.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Most plants need sunlight for photosynthesis.<br>• The ghost plant (<i>Monotropa uniflora</i>) is white and lacks chlorophyll.<br>• It gets nutrients by tapping into fungi connected to the roots of nearby trees.<br>• The trees supply the fungi with sugars made through photosynthesis.<br>• The ghost plant can thus grow on dark forest floors.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the ghost plant indirectly benefits from photosynthesis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because it gets nutrients from fungi connected to the roots of nearby trees rather than from sunlight, the ghost plant can grow on dark forest floors.","Though it cannot photosynthesize, the ghost plant taps fungi that receive sugars from nearby trees, which make those sugars through photosynthesis.","The ghost plant gets its nutrients by tapping into fungi that are connected to the roots of nearby trees.","The ghost plant is white and lacks chlorophyll, so unlike most plants it cannot carry out photosynthesis."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The indirect benefit is the chain from trees' photosynthesis to fungi to the ghost plant, which only the correct choice traces. The choice beginning "The ghost plant gets…" stops at the fungi, and the others describe the plant's appearance or habitat.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Harlem Renaissance was a flowering of Black art and literature in the 1920s.<br>• Langston Hughes wrote poems inspired by the rhythms of jazz and blues.<br>• His 1926 collection was titled <i>The Weary Blues</i>.<br>• Some Black critics argued that his poems emphasized the wrong aspects of Black life.<br>• Hughes defended writing about ordinary people.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the debate over Hughes's work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hughes's 1926 poetry collection, <i>The Weary Blues</i>, drew on the rhythms of jazz and blues and was criticized by some reviewers during the Harlem Renaissance.","Langston Hughes, a leading poet of the Harlem Renaissance, wrote poems inspired by the rhythms of jazz and blues.","While some Black critics argued that Hughes's poems emphasized the wrong aspects of Black life, Hughes defended writing about ordinary people.","The Harlem Renaissance, a flowering of Black art and literature, took place during the 1920s."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A debate needs both sides, which only the correct choice gives. The choice beginning "Hughes's 1926 poetry collection…" mentions criticism without its substance or Hughes's response, and the others give background.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers measured noise levels in 20 hospital wards.<br>• Average nighttime noise was 52 decibels.<br>• The World Health Organization recommends no more than 30 decibels at night in hospital rooms.<br>• Patients in noisier wards reported poorer sleep.<br>• Common sources included alarms, carts, and staff conversations.$q$, $q$The student wants to show that hospital noise exceeds recommended levels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The World Health Organization recommends that noise in hospital rooms be kept at no more than 30 decibels at night.","Patients in noisier hospital wards reported poorer sleep, and common noise sources in the 20 wards studied included alarms, carts, and staff conversations.","Nighttime noise in the hospital wards averaged 52 decibels, well above the 30-decibel maximum recommended by the World Health Organization.","Researchers measured nighttime noise in 20 hospital wards and found an average level of 52 decibels."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Showing that noise exceeds the recommendation requires both the measured level and the recommended maximum. Only the correct choice has both.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The pistol shrimp snaps its oversized claw shut at high speed.<br>• The snap creates a bubble that collapses with a loud crack.<br>• The collapse briefly reaches temperatures near 4,700°C.<br>• The shock wave stuns or kills small prey.<br>• Colonies of pistol shrimp can interfere with submarine sonar.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the pistol shrimp captures prey. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["When the pistol shrimp's collapsing bubble cracks, it briefly reaches temperatures near 4,700°C.","The pistol shrimp has an oversized claw that it can snap shut at high speed, producing a loud crack.","The pistol shrimp snaps its claw shut so fast that it creates a bubble whose collapse sends out a shock wave that stuns or kills prey.","Colonies of pistol shrimp, which snap their oversized claws at high speed and create collapsing bubbles reaching 4,700°C, can interfere with submarine sonar."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice traces the full sequence from snap to bubble to shock wave to prey. The choice beginning "The pistol shrimp has…" stops at the crack, and the choices beginning "When the pistol shrimp's…" and "Colonies of pistol shrimp…" give striking facts unrelated to hunting.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A public library launched a program lending tools, such as drills and ladders.<br>• In its first year, it lent tools 4,300 times.<br>• 62% of borrowers had not used the library in the previous year.<br>• Library card sign-ups rose 15%.<br>• Staff hope to add sewing machines and cameras.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that the program attracted new library users. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In its first year, the library's tool-lending program, which offers drills and ladders, lent tools a total of 4,300 times, and staff hope to add sewing machines.","Library staff hope to expand the tool-lending program by adding items such as sewing machines and cameras.","The public library launched a program that allows patrons to borrow tools such as drills and ladders.","Of those who borrowed tools in the program's first year, 62% had not used the library in the previous year, and card sign-ups rose 15%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Evidence of new users comes from the share of borrowers new to the library and the rise in sign-ups. The choice beginning "In its first year…" shows use but not new users, and the others describe plans or the program.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Composer Florence Price (1887–1953) was the first Black woman to have a symphony performed by a major U.S. orchestra.<br>• The Chicago Symphony performed her Symphony in E Minor in 1933.<br>• Her music blends European classical forms with African American spirituals.<br>• Many of her manuscripts were found in an abandoned house in 2009.<br>• Since then, her works have been increasingly performed and recorded.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the recent revival of interest in Price's music. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Florence Price's music, including the Symphony in E Minor performed in 1933, blends European classical forms with the melodies of African American spirituals.","After many of her manuscripts were discovered in an abandoned house in 2009, Florence Price's works have been increasingly performed and recorded.","In 1933, the Chicago Symphony performed Florence Price's Symphony in E Minor, making her a pioneer among composers.","Florence Price was the first Black woman to have a symphony performed by a major orchestra in the United States."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The revival follows from the 2009 discovery, which only the correct choice connects to increased performances. The others describe her historic achievement or her style.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Sahara contains vast underground aquifers.<br>• The Nubian Sandstone Aquifer holds an estimated 150,000 cubic kilometers of water.<br>• Most of this water is "fossil water" that collected over 10,000 years ago.<br>• It is recharged extremely slowly, if at all.<br>• Libya pumps it to cities through the Great Man-Made River project.$q$, $q$The student wants to caution that the aquifer's water is not a renewable resource. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through its Great Man-Made River project, Libya pumps water from beneath the Sahara to its cities.","The Nubian Sandstone Aquifer, beneath the Sahara, holds an estimated 150,000 cubic kilometers of water.","The Sahara contains vast underground aquifers, including the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer, which holds an estimated 150,000 cubic kilometers of water that Libya pumps to its cities.","Although the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer holds vast amounts of water, most of it is fossil water that collected over 10,000 years ago and is recharged extremely slowly, if at all."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The caution depends on the water's age and negligible recharge, both of which only the correct choice includes. The choice beginning "The Sahara contains vast…" mentions fossil water without explaining that it isn't replenished, and the others describe size or use.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two theories explain why the Leaning Tower of Pisa leans.<br>• Theory 1: the soft, clay-rich soil compressed unevenly under its weight.<br>• Theory 2: builders used foundations only 3 meters deep.<br>• Engineers today consider both factors responsible.<br>• Stabilization work in 1990–2001 reduced the lean by about 45 cm.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the current view of what caused the lean. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Stabilization work carried out between 1990 and 2001 reduced the lean of the Leaning Tower of Pisa by about 45 cm.","One theory holds that the Leaning Tower of Pisa leans because the soft, clay-rich soil beneath it compressed unevenly.","Engineers today attribute the Leaning Tower of Pisa's tilt both to soft, clay-rich soil and to foundations only 3 meters deep.","There are two competing theories that explain why the Leaning Tower of Pisa leans, one involving soil and one involving foundations."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The current view holds both factors responsible, as only the correct choice states. The fourth wrongly presents the theories as competing, the choice beginning "One theory holds that…" gives one theory, and the choice beginning "Stabilization work carried out…" concerns stabilization.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Marie Tharp (1920–2006) was an American geologist and cartographer.<br>• In the 1950s, she mapped the floor of the Atlantic Ocean using sonar data.<br>• She identified a rift valley running along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.<br>• Her colleague initially dismissed the finding as "girl talk."<br>• The rift became key evidence for the theory of plate tectonics.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the scientific importance of Tharp's discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In the 1950s, Marie Tharp used sonar data to map the floor of the Atlantic Ocean, identifying a rift valley.","The rift valley that Marie Tharp identified along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge became key evidence for the theory of plate tectonics.","Marie Tharp's colleague initially dismissed her identification of a rift valley along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge as \"girl talk.\"","Marie Tharp, an American geologist and cartographer, lived from 1920 to 2006 and mapped the Atlantic Ocean floor."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Importance is shown by the rift's role as evidence for plate tectonics, which only the correct choice states. The choice beginning "In the 1950s, Marie…" describes the discovery, the choice beginning "Marie Tharp's colleague initially…" its initial reception, and the choice beginning "Marie Tharp, an American…" her biography.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some birds, such as Clark's nutcrackers, cache thousands of seeds each fall.<br>• A nutcracker can hide up to 30,000 seeds in a season.<br>• It recovers many of them months later, even under snow.<br>• The bird's hippocampus, a brain region tied to spatial memory, is enlarged.<br>• Unrecovered seeds can grow into new trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to connect the nutcracker's behavior to its brain. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The hippocampus, a brain region tied to spatial memory, is enlarged in some birds, such as Clark's nutcrackers.","Seeds that Clark's nutcrackers hide but fail to recover can grow into new trees, helping forests regenerate.","The Clark's nutcracker, which recovers many of its cached seeds months later, has an enlarged hippocampus, a brain region tied to spatial memory.","A Clark's nutcracker can hide up to 30,000 seeds in a single season and recover many of them months later, even under snow, while unrecovered seeds grow into trees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice links the behavior (recovering seeds) to the brain feature. The choice beginning "A Clark's nutcracker can…" describes behavior alone, the fourth the brain alone, and the choice beginning "Seeds that Clark's nutcrackers…" an ecological effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 2019, a team created a map of food access in Baltimore.<br>• About 23% of residents lived in "healthy food priority areas."<br>• These areas lacked supermarkets within a quarter mile.<br>• Rates were highest among Black residents, at 31%.<br>• The city has used the map to target new grocery programs.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a disparity revealed by the map. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Baltimore has used a 2019 map of food access to target new grocery programs to the areas that need them most.","In 2019, a team created a map showing which parts of Baltimore were healthy food priority areas.","While about 23% of Baltimore residents lived in healthy food priority areas, the rate among Black residents was higher, at 31%.","A 2019 map found that about 23% of Baltimore residents lived in areas lacking supermarkets within a quarter mile, and the city has used it to target grocery programs."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A disparity requires comparing a group with the overall rate. Only the correct choice does this. The choice beginning "A 2019 map found…" gives the overall rate alone, and the others describe the map or its use.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Voyager Golden Records were launched aboard two spacecraft in 1977.<br>• Each contains sounds and images meant to represent life on Earth.<br>• Contents include greetings in 55 languages and music from many cultures.<br>• The committee was chaired by astronomer Carl Sagan.<br>• The spacecraft have now left the solar system's heliosphere.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the contents of the Golden Records. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The committee that selected the contents of the Voyager Golden Records was chaired by astronomer Carl Sagan.","Launched in 1977 aboard two spacecraft, the Voyager Golden Records, chosen by a committee chaired by Carl Sagan, have now left the heliosphere.","Each Voyager Golden Record contains sounds and images, and each was launched aboard a spacecraft in 1977.","Meant to represent life on Earth, the Voyager Golden Records include greetings in 55 languages and music from many cultures."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice gives specific contents. The fourth refers vaguely to "sounds and images," and the others describe the launch, location, or committee.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some scientists propose reintroducing large herbivores to European landscapes, an approach called rewilding.<br>• At Oostvaardersplassen in the Netherlands, deer, cattle, and horses were released in the 1980s.<br>• Without predators, populations grew rapidly.<br>• During harsh winters, thousands of animals starved.<br>• Managers now cull animals to keep populations in check.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a challenge that rewilding projects can face. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Managers at Oostvaardersplassen in the Netherlands now cull animals as part of the site's rewilding project.","Rewilding, which involves reintroducing large herbivores such as deer, cattle, and horses, has been proposed by some scientists for European landscapes.","In the 1980s, deer, cattle, and horses were released at Oostvaardersplassen, a rewilding site in the Netherlands.","At Oostvaardersplassen, herbivores released without predators multiplied rapidly, and thousands starved in harsh winters, forcing managers to cull animals."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice shows the challenge: unchecked population growth causing starvation and requiring culls. The choice beginning "Managers at Oostvaardersplassen in…" mentions culling without explaining the problem behind it, and the others define rewilding or describe the release.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers asked participants to write about a recent conflict.<br>• Group 1 wrote in the first person ("I").<br>• Group 2 wrote from a third-person perspective, using their own name.<br>• Group 2 reported less anger and more insight afterward.<br>• Researchers call this "self-distancing."$q$, $q$The student wants to summarize the study's method and main finding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants who wrote about a recent conflict from a third-person perspective reported less anger, a result researchers call self-distancing.","Researchers use the term \"self-distancing\" to describe writing about one's own experiences from a third-person perspective.","Researchers asked one group of participants to write about a conflict in the first person and another to write in the third person.","When participants wrote about a conflict in the third person rather than the first, they reported less anger and more insight afterward."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A summary of method and finding must describe the comparison and the result. The correct choice does both. The choice beginning "Researchers asked one group…" gives only the method. The choice beginning "Participants who wrote about…" gives the result but omits the first-person comparison group. The fourth defines a term.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Cuttlefish can change skin color and texture in under a second.<br>• Researchers placed cuttlefish on checkerboard backgrounds.<br>• On large squares, cuttlefish displayed bold, high-contrast patterns.<br>• On small squares, they displayed fine, mottled patterns.<br>• Cuttlefish appear to match the scale of background features.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the researchers' conclusion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cuttlefish are able to change both the color and the texture of their skin in less than one second, an ability that researchers have studied using checkerboards.","Cuttlefish appear to match the scale of features in their background.","On large checkerboard squares, cuttlefish displayed bold, high-contrast patterns, while on small squares they displayed fine, mottled ones.","Researchers placed cuttlefish, which can change the color and texture of their skin in under a second, on checkerboard backgrounds with squares of different sizes."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is the general statement that cuttlefish match background scale. The choice beginning "On large checkerboard squares…" reports the observations that support the conclusion, and the others describe the animal's ability or the method.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Inca Empire had no written alphabet.<br>• Officials recorded information using quipus: cords with knots.<br>• Knot types and positions represented numbers in a base-10 system.<br>• Quipus recorded census data, taxes, and storehouse inventories.<br>• Some scholars think quipus may also have recorded narratives.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how quipus stored numerical information. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lacking a written alphabet, the Inca Empire relied on quipus, cords with knots, to record a wide variety of important information.","On quipus, the types and positions of knots represented numbers in a base-10 system.","Inca officials, who had no written alphabet, used quipus to record census data, tax payments, and the contents of storehouses throughout the empire.","Although quipus were knotted cords used by Inca officials, some scholars think they may also have recorded narratives as well as numbers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice explains how numbers were encoded. The other choices describe what quipus recorded or who used them, which doesn't answer how they stored numbers.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied 1,000 children's books published from 1960 to 2020.<br>• Female main characters appeared in 25% of books in the 1960s.<br>• They appeared in 41% of books in the 2010s.<br>• Male animal characters still outnumbered female animal characters 2 to 1 in the 2010s.$q$, $q$The student wants to show that progress toward gender balance was incomplete. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Female main characters rose from 25% to 41% of children's books, but male animal characters still outnumbered female ones 2 to 1 in the 2010s.","Female main characters appeared in only 25% of children's books in the 1960s, a share that climbed to 41% by the 2010s according to researchers.","Researchers studied 1,000 children's books published between 1960 and 2020 to see how often female main characters appeared in them over the decades.","In the 2010s, male animal characters outnumbered female animal characters by 2 to 1."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Showing incomplete progress requires both the gain and the remaining imbalance. The choice beginning "Female main characters appeared…" shows only progress, the choice beginning "In the 2010s, male…" only the imbalance, and the choice beginning "Researchers studied 1,000 children's…" describes the study.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed mold killing bacteria in a petri dish.<br>• The mold produced a substance he named penicillin.<br>• Fleming struggled to purify it and largely abandoned the work.<br>• In 1940, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain purified penicillin and tested it on mice.<br>• Mass production during World War II saved many soldiers' lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that developing penicillin required work beyond Fleming's discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928 but could not purify it; that step was achieved by Florey and Chain in 1940.","Howard Florey and Ernst Chain tested penicillin on mice in 1940.","In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold growing in one of his petri dishes was killing bacteria, and he named the substance it produced penicillin.","During World War II, the mass production of penicillin, a substance discovered by Alexander Fleming, saved the lives of many soldiers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice contrasts Fleming's discovery with the later purification by others. The choice beginning "In 1928, Alexander Fleming…" describes only the discovery, the choice beginning "During World War II…" credits Fleming alone, and the choice beginning "Howard Florey and Ernst…" omits the link to Fleming's unfinished work.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Rain gardens are shallow planted depressions that collect runoff from roofs and pavement.<br>• They allow water to soak into the ground instead of flowing into storm drains.<br>• Plants and soil filter pollutants from the water.<br>• A typical rain garden absorbs about 30% more water than an equal area of lawn.<br>• Many cities offer rebates for installing them.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how rain gardens reduce water pollution. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Rain gardens absorb about 30% more water than lawns, keeping runoff out of storm drains.","Many cities now offer rebates to residents who install rain gardens, shallow depressions planted to collect runoff from roofs and pavement.","A typical rain garden, a shallow planted depression that collects runoff from roofs and pavement, absorbs about 30% more water than an equal area of lawn does.","Rain gardens collect runoff and let it soak into the ground, where plants and soil filter out pollutants."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice explains the filtering that reduces pollution. The choice beginning "A typical rain garden…" concerns water absorption rather than pollution, the choice beginning "Many cities now offer…" concerns incentives, and the choice beginning "Rain gardens absorb about…" is a bare definition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Painter Jacob Lawrence created <i>The Migration Series</i> (1940–41), 60 panels.<br>• The series depicts the Great Migration of Black Americans from the South to Northern cities.<br>• Each panel has a caption written by Lawrence.<br>• He used a limited palette, applying one color at a time to all panels.<br>• This method gave the series visual unity.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Lawrence achieved unity across the series. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Each of the 60 panels in Jacob Lawrence's <i>The Migration Series</i>, which depicts the movement of Black Americans to Northern cities, has a caption he wrote.","Lawrence gave <i>The Migration Series</i> visual unity by using a limited palette, applying one color at a time across all panels.","Each of the 60 panels in <i>The Migration Series</i> pairs an image with a caption that Lawrence wrote himself.","Jacob Lawrence's <i>The Migration Series</i>, created in 1940 and 1941, consists of 60 panels depicting the Great Migration of Black Americans from the South."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice names the technique and its unifying effect. The other choices describe the series' subject, size, or captions.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The okapi lives in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo.<br>• It has striped legs like a zebra.<br>• Its closest living relative is the giraffe.<br>• Like giraffes, okapis have long, flexible tongues.<br>• Western scientists did not describe the okapi until 1901.$q$, $q$The student wants to clarify a common misconception about the okapi's relationships. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though its striped legs resemble a zebra's, the okapi's closest living relative is the giraffe.","Like its relative the giraffe, the okapi has a long, flexible tongue that it uses while feeding in the rainforests of central Africa.","The okapi, which lives in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, was not described by Western scientists until 1901.","Like giraffes, its closest living relatives, okapis have long, flexible tongues."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The misconception arises from the zebra-like stripes. Only the correct choice addresses it by contrasting the stripes with the actual relationship. The choice beginning "Like its relative the…" states the relationship without the misconception.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers analyzed 3 million online restaurant reviews.<br>• Reviews of expensive restaurants used more words related to sensuality.<br>• Reviews of cheap restaurants used more words related to addiction, like "craving."<br>• Negative reviews often used language similar to descriptions of trauma.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a contrast in how reviewers write about restaurants at different price levels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Reviews of expensive restaurants used more sensual words, while reviews of cheap restaurants used more words related to addiction, like \"craving.\"","An analysis of 3 million online restaurant reviews found that reviews of expensive restaurants tended to use more words related to sensuality.","Reviews of cheap restaurants often used words related to addiction, such as \"craving.\"","Researchers who analyzed 3 million online restaurant reviews found that negative reviews often used language similar to descriptions of trauma."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A contrast by price level needs both expensive and cheap restaurants. Only the correct choice gives both. The choice beginning "An analysis of 3…" gives one side, and the choice beginning "Researchers who analyzed 3…" concerns negative reviews.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Philosopher Hannah Arendt reported on the 1961 trial of Adolf Eichmann.<br>• She expected to find a monstrous figure.<br>• Instead, she found a bureaucrat focused on following orders and advancing his career.<br>• She coined the phrase "the banality of evil."<br>• The phrase remains widely discussed and disputed.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what led Arendt to coin her famous phrase. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Philosopher Hannah Arendt reported on the 1961 trial of Adolf Eichmann for a general audience, observing him closely throughout the proceedings.","Expecting a monster at Eichmann's trial but finding a career-minded bureaucrat, Arendt coined the phrase \"the banality of evil.\"","Arendt's phrase \"the banality of evil\" has been widely discussed and disputed since the 1961 Eichmann trial.","Hannah Arendt's phrase \"the banality of evil,\" which she coined after reporting on a famous 1961 trial, remains widely discussed and disputed today."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice explains what prompted the phrase: the gap between expectation and observation. The others mention the phrase or the trial without the reason.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Lithium-ion batteries power most electric cars.<br>• Sodium-ion batteries use abundant, inexpensive sodium.<br>• They store about 30% less energy by weight than lithium-ion batteries.<br>• They perform better in cold temperatures.<br>• Some carmakers plan to use them in low-cost models.$q$, $q$The student wants to present both an advantage and a disadvantage of sodium-ion batteries. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike lithium-ion batteries, which power most electric cars, sodium-ion batteries rely on sodium.","Sodium-ion batteries perform better in the cold than lithium-ion batteries but store about 30% less energy by weight.","Sodium-ion batteries store about 30% less energy by weight than lithium-ion batteries do.","Sodium-ion batteries use sodium, an abundant and inexpensive element, and some carmakers plan to use them in their low-cost electric models."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice includes both an advantage (cold performance) and a disadvantage (lower energy density). The choice beginning "Sodium-ion batteries use sodium…" gives advantages only, the choice beginning "Sodium-ion batteries store about…" a disadvantage only, and the choice beginning "Unlike lithium-ion batteries, which…" neither.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami struck 14 countries.<br>• On Simeulue Island, Indonesia, only 7 of about 78,000 residents died.<br>• Islanders had passed down a story called <i>smong</i> after a 1907 tsunami.<br>• The story taught people to flee to high ground when the sea recedes.<br>• Researchers credit the oral tradition with saving lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the role of oral tradition in disaster survival. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami struck 14 countries, but on Indonesia's Simeulue Island, only 7 of about 78,000 residents lost their lives, a remarkably low death toll.","Because of <i>smong</i>, a story passed down since 1907 urging people to flee when the sea recedes, only 7 of Simeulue's 78,000 residents died in the 2004 tsunami.","Researchers credit an oral tradition with saving lives on Simeulue Island in Indonesia during the 2004 tsunami.","After a tsunami struck their island in 1907, the residents of Simeulue passed down a story called <i>smong</i>."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice links the story's lesson to the low death toll. The choice beginning "The 2004 Indian Ocean…" gives the outcome without the tradition, the choice beginning "Researchers credit an oral…" makes the claim without specifics, and the choice beginning "After a tsunami struck…" describes the story without its effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers measured how long shoppers spent in two versions of a store.<br>• Version A played slow-tempo music.<br>• Version B played fast-tempo music.<br>• Shoppers in Version A stayed 17% longer and spent 38% more.<br>• The researchers studied one grocery store over nine weeks.$q$, $q$The student wants to acknowledge a limitation of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers compared two versions of a grocery store, one playing slow-tempo music and one playing fast-tempo music, to measure how long shoppers stayed.","Because the researchers studied only one grocery store, it is unclear whether slow music would have the same effect elsewhere.","Shoppers who heard slow-tempo music stayed 17% longer and spent 38% more than shoppers who heard fast-tempo music, according to the nine-week study.","The study, which lasted nine weeks, found that slow music increased both shopping time and spending."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A limitation concerns how far the results can be generalized, and the single-store design is one. Only the correct choice states it. The choice beginning "The study, which lasted…" gives a detail without framing it as a limitation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The coelacanth is a fish once known only from fossils.<br>• Scientists believed it had gone extinct about 66 million years ago.<br>• In 1938, a living coelacanth was caught off South Africa.<br>• Museum curator Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer recognized its significance.<br>• A second species was found in Indonesia in 1998.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the 1938 catch was surprising. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Museum curator Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer recognized the significance of the coelacanth caught off South Africa in 1938.","The 1938 catch of a living coelacanth was surprising because scientists believed the fish had gone extinct about 66 million years earlier.","In 1938, a living coelacanth, a fish previously known only from fossils, was caught off the coast of South Africa and identified by a museum curator.","A living coelacanth was caught off South Africa in 1938, and a second species was found in Indonesia in 1998."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice gives the reason for surprise: the fish was thought long extinct. The choice beginning "In 1938, a living…" implies the surprise but doesn't explain it, and the others concern recognition or a later find.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Architect Zaha Hadid (1950–2016) was born in Baghdad.<br>• For years, many of her designs were considered impossible to build.<br>• Her first major completed building, a fire station in Germany, opened in 1993.<br>• Advances in computer modeling later made her curving forms buildable.<br>• In 2004, she became the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how technology affected Hadid's career. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hadid's curving designs, made buildable in part by computer modeling, earned her the Pritzker Prize in 2004.","Hadid's designs were long considered impossible to build until advances in computer modeling made her curving forms buildable.","Hadid's first major completed building, a fire station in Germany that opened in 1993, came after years of designs considered impossible to build.","Zaha Hadid, who was born in Baghdad in 1950, became the first woman to win architecture's prestigious Pritzker Prize in 2004."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice links technology to her designs becoming buildable. The choice beginning "Hadid's first major completed…" mentions her unbuildable designs but not technology, and the others give biographical facts.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ecologists counted insects at 63 nature reserves in Germany from 1989 to 2016.<br>• The total mass of flying insects fell by 76%.<br>• The decline occurred even in protected areas.<br>• Causes are uncertain but may include pesticide use and habitat loss on nearby farmland.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize why the decline alarmed ecologists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Pesticide use and habitat loss on nearby farmland may explain why flying insects declined by 76%.","The causes of the insect decline are uncertain but may include pesticide use and loss of habitat on farmland near the reserves.","Between 1989 and 2016, the mass of flying insects at German nature reserves fell by 76%, even though the areas were protected.","Ecologists counted flying insects at 63 nature reserves in Germany over a period of nearly three decades, from 1989 until 2016."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The alarming features are the size of the decline and that it happened in protected areas, which only the correct choice includes. The others describe the method or possible causes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two historians studied the causes of the 1929 stock market crash.<br>• Historian A emphasized excessive borrowing to buy stocks.<br>• Historian B emphasized weak regulation of banks.<br>• Both agreed that public panic accelerated the crash.$q$, $q$The student wants to identify a point on which the historians agreed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasized excessive borrowing to buy stocks as a cause of the 1929 crash, while Historian B emphasized weak regulation of banks.","Historian A and Historian B each identified a different underlying cause of the 1929 crash.","Both historians agreed that public panic accelerated the 1929 stock market crash.","Two historians studied the causes of the 1929 stock market crash, each emphasizing a different factor that contributed to the collapse of prices."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice states the point of agreement. The choices beginning "Historian A emphasized excessive…" and "Two historians studied the…" describe differences, and the choice beginning "Historian A and Historian…" gives one historian's view.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Spider silk is stronger by weight than steel.<br>• Spiders cannot be farmed like silkworms because they are territorial and eat each other.<br>• Scientists inserted spider silk genes into bacteria and yeast.<br>• These microbes produce silk proteins that can be spun into fibers.<br>• The fibers are being tested for use in sutures and lightweight armor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why scientists turned to microbes to produce spider silk. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike silkworms, spiders produce silk that is stronger by weight than steel.","Spider silk is stronger by weight than steel, and fibers spun from silk proteins are being tested for sutures and lightweight armor.","Scientists inserted spider silk genes into bacteria and yeast, which produce silk proteins that can then be spun into useful fibers for medicine.","Because spiders are territorial and eat each other, they cannot be farmed, so scientists inserted silk genes into bacteria and yeast."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice gives the reason for using microbes and connects it to that approach. The choice beginning "Scientists inserted spider silk…" describes the method without the reason, and the choice beginning "Unlike silkworms, spiders produce…" gives a reason with no connection to microbes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Great Barrier Reef experienced mass coral bleaching in 2016, 2017, 2020, and 2022.<br>• Bleaching occurs when high water temperatures cause coral to expel algae living in its tissues.<br>• Bleached coral can recover if temperatures drop soon enough.<br>• Recovery typically takes 10–15 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why frequent bleaching events threaten recovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Barrier Reef experienced mass coral bleaching events in 2016, 2017, 2020, and 2022, according to researchers who monitor the reef.","Coral typically needs 10–15 years to recover from bleaching, yet the reef bleached four times between 2016 and 2022.","Bleached coral can recover if temperatures drop soon enough, a process that typically takes 10–15 years.","Bleaching occurs when high water temperatures cause coral to expel the algae living in its tissues, though bleached coral can recover if waters cool."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The threat comes from the mismatch between the recovery time and the frequency of events, which only the correct choice presents. The choice beginning "The Great Barrier Reef…" lists the events without the recovery time, and the choice beginning "Bleaching occurs when high…" explains the mechanism.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Kenyan runner Tegla Loroupe won the New York City Marathon in 1994.<br>• She was the first African woman to win a major marathon.<br>• She founded a foundation that organizes peace races in conflict regions.<br>• The races bring together members of rival communities.<br>• In 2016, she led the first Refugee Olympic Team.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how Loroupe uses running to promote peace. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tegla Loroupe, who founded a foundation devoted to peace, led the first Refugee Olympic Team at the 2016 Olympic Games.","Loroupe's foundation organizes peace races in conflict regions that bring together members of rival communities.","After winning the 1994 New York City Marathon, Loroupe went on to lead the first Refugee Olympic Team.","In 1994, Kenyan runner Tegla Loroupe won the New York City Marathon, becoming the first African woman to win a major marathon."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice describes how running promotes peace: races that unite rival communities. The others give athletic achievements or an unrelated role.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers taught crows to associate a masked face with being captured.<br>• Years later, crows scolded people wearing that mask.<br>• Crows that had never been captured also scolded the mask.<br>• Researchers concluded that crows learn about dangerous humans from other crows.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the evidence for the researchers' conclusion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Crows that had been captured by masked researchers scolded people wearing that mask years later.","Researchers taught crows to associate a particular masked face with being captured, and years later the crows still scolded people wearing it.","Even crows that had never been captured scolded the mask associated with capture, suggesting they learned of the danger from other crows.","Researchers concluded that crows learn about dangerous humans from one another, in addition to learning from their own experiences."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The key evidence for social learning is that crows never captured also scolded the mask. Only the correct choice gives it. The choice beginning "Researchers taught crows to…" shows individual memory, not social learning, and the choice beginning "Researchers concluded that crows…" states the conclusion without evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Library of Congress's "Chronicling America" project has digitized over 20 million newspaper pages.<br>• The pages date from 1756 to 1963.<br>• Optical character recognition makes the text searchable.<br>• Historians have used it to trace how stories spread between newspapers.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how a feature of the project supports historical research. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Library of Congress's \"Chronicling America\" project has digitized more than 20 million pages from newspapers dating from 1756 to 1963.","Historians have used the \"Chronicling America\" project to trace how stories spread between newspapers.","Historians have used the \"Chronicling America\" project, run by the Library of Congress, to research a variety of topics in American history.","Because optical character recognition makes the project's pages searchable, historians can trace how stories spread between newspapers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice links a feature (searchability) to a research use. The choice beginning "The Library of Congress's…" describes scale, the choice beginning "Historians have used the "Chronicling America" project…" mentions use vaguely, and the choice beginning "Historians have used the "Chronicling America" project…" gives a date range.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers compared sleep in hunter-gatherer communities in Tanzania, Namibia, and Bolivia.<br>• People slept an average of 6.4 hours per night.<br>• This is similar to or less than sleep in industrial societies.<br>• Many had assumed that modern technology had reduced human sleep.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a finding that challenges a common assumption. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Many people had assumed that modern technology, including electric lighting, had reduced the amount of sleep humans get each night.","Researchers compared sleep in hunter-gatherer communities in three countries, Tanzania, Namibia, and Bolivia, and found that people there slept an average of 6.4 hours per night.","Hunter-gatherers slept about 6.4 hours a night, no more than people in industrial societies, challenging the idea that technology has reduced human sleep.","People in the hunter-gatherer communities slept 6.4 hours per night."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice gives the finding, the comparison, and the assumption it challenges. The choice beginning "People in the hunter-gatherer…" gives the finding without the comparison, and the others give the design or the assumption alone.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Poet Joy Harjo is a member of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation.<br>• In 2019, she became the first Native American U.S. Poet Laureate.<br>• Her poems often draw on Muscogee stories and history.<br>• She is also a saxophonist who performs her poetry with music.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how Harjo combines art forms. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Joy Harjo's poems often draw on the stories and history of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation, of which she is a member.","Harjo, the first Native American U.S. Poet Laureate, draws on Muscogee stories in her poems.","In 2019, Joy Harjo, a member of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation, became the first Native American to serve as Poet Laureate of the United States.","A saxophonist as well as a poet, Joy Harjo performs her poetry with music."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice describes the combination of poetry and music. The others describe her honors or her sources.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers placed fake fruit of different colors in a forest.<br>• Birds took red fruit more often than green fruit.<br>• Mammals showed no color preference.<br>• Many bird-dispersed plants produce red fruit.<br>• Researchers suggest fruit color evolved partly to attract birds.$q$, $q$The student wants to connect the experimental results to a broader pattern in plants. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In an experiment, birds took red fake fruit more often than green fake fruit, while mammals in the same forest showed no color preference at all.","Birds preferred red fake fruit, and many bird-dispersed plants produce red fruit, suggesting that fruit color evolved partly to attract birds.","Researchers placed fake fruit of several different colors in a forest to see which colors birds and mammals would prefer to take.","Many plants whose seeds are dispersed by birds produce red fruit."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Connecting the results to the broader pattern requires both the bird preference and the prevalence of red fruit, as in the correct choice. The choice beginning "In an experiment, birds…" gives results only, and the choice beginning "Many plants whose seeds…" gives the pattern only.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Treaty of Westphalia (1648) ended the Thirty Years' War.<br>• It recognized the sovereignty of individual states within the Holy Roman Empire.<br>• Scholars often cite it as the origin of the modern system of independent states.<br>• Some historians argue this view oversimplifies the treaty's actual terms.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the scholarly debate about the treaty's significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Treaty of Westphalia, signed in 1648, ended the Thirty Years' War and recognized the sovereignty of individual states within the Holy Roman Empire.","Scholars often cite the 1648 Treaty of Westphalia, which ended the Thirty Years' War, as the origin of the modern system of independent states.","Some historians argue that the common view of the Treaty of Westphalia oversimplifies its actual terms.","While many scholars cite the Treaty of Westphalia as the origin of the modern state system, some historians argue that this view oversimplifies its terms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A debate requires both positions, which only the correct choice presents. The choice beginning "Scholars often cite the…" gives the common view only, and the choices beginning "The Treaty of Westphalia…" and "Some historians argue that…" describe the treaty's terms.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers gave participants a puzzle to solve.<br>• Some participants were told to take a 10-minute break midway.<br>• During the break, half did an undemanding task; half rested.<br>• The undemanding-task group solved 41% more puzzles afterward.<br>• Researchers suggest mind-wandering during simple tasks aids creative problem-solving.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the researchers' suggestion and the result that prompted it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some participants who were given a puzzle to solve were told to take a 10-minute break midway, during which half rested and half did a simple task.","Participants who did an undemanding task during a break solved 41% more puzzles, leading researchers to suggest that mind-wandering aids creative problem-solving.","Taking a 10-minute break midway through a puzzle helped participants solve 41% more puzzles afterward.","Researchers suggest that the mind-wandering that occurs during simple tasks can help people solve problems creatively, especially when they take a 10-minute break midway through a puzzle."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice gives both the result and the suggestion it prompted. The choice beginning "Researchers suggest that the…" gives only the suggestion, the choice beginning "Some participants who were…" only the method, and the choice beginning "Taking a 10-minute break…" an imprecise result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Panama Canal uses locks to lift ships 26 meters above sea level.<br>• Each ship passage uses about 200 million liters of fresh water from Gatun Lake.<br>• A 2023 drought lowered Gatun Lake's water level.<br>• Authorities reduced the number of daily ship crossings.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the drought affected canal traffic. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because each ship passage uses about 200 million liters of water from Gatun Lake, a drought that lowered the lake led authorities to reduce crossings.","The Panama Canal uses a system of locks to lift ships 26 meters above sea level as they pass between the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.","In 2023, a drought lowered the water level of Gatun Lake, and authorities reduced the number of ships allowed to cross the Panama Canal each day.","Each ship passage through the Panama Canal's locks uses about 200 million liters of fresh water."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Explaining why requires the link between the canal's water use and the drought, which only the correct choice makes. The choice beginning "In 2023, a drought…" reports the drought and the reduction without the causal link.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first transatlantic telegraph cable, completed in 1858, allowed messages to cross the ocean in minutes rather than the ten days required by ship. ______ the cable's insulation failed after only three weeks, and a durable connection was not established until 1866.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Unfortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The cable's quick failure was an undesirable turn after its promising start, so "Unfortunately" fits. "Consequently" would claim that fast messaging caused the failure, "Similarly" signals a parallel, and "For instance" signals an example.$q$),
  ($q$conv-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Once the votes were tallied______the results were announced to a stunned crowd.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Once the votes were tallied' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The city's water tower had stood for over a century______no one wanted to see it demolished.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The experiment's outcome confirmed the hypothesis______heat does accelerate the reaction.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the hypothesis was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Employees______who complete the training will receive a certificate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Who complete the training' is a restrictive (essential) clause identifying which employees, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe, ______ was passed down through four generations, calls for a pinch of saffron.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the recipe, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Ticket sales exceeded expectations______therefore, the band added a second show.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A conjunctive adverb like 'therefore' joining two independent clauses requires a semicolon before it (and a comma after).$q$),
  ($q$conv-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Exhausted from the overnight flight______the team went straight to bed instead of sightseeing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Exhausted from the overnight flight' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The rain continued for hours______the streets began to flood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction must be separated by a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum acquired three new pieces this year______a bronze sculpture, an oil painting, and a hand-woven tapestry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$In the years following the merger______the company's revenue nearly tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'In the years following the merger' is an introductory phrase and requires a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Critics were divided on the film's pacing______audiences overwhelmingly loved it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The award, ______ given annually since 1962 to the reporter whose work most advances the public interest, honors excellence in local journalism.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been","that has been","who has been","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas describes a thing (the award), not the reporter named later in the sentence, so it needs the thing-pronoun 'which' with a complete verb phrase; 'who has been' is tempting because a person is mentioned nearby, but the clause still modifies 'award.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bakery sold out of croissants by nine a.m.______the line had formed before sunrise.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," because "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related, so a semicolon correctly joins them without a coordinating conjunction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The flock of geese______south every autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["migrates","migrate","have migrated","are migrating"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Flock' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'migrates.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the coach nor the players______satisfied with the referee's call.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' or 'neither/nor' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the applicants______required to submit three letters of recommendation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Each' is singular, regardless of the plural noun in the prepositional phrase that follows it, so the singular verb 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the guests arrive, she______dinner for two hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will have been cooking","will cook","cooked","is cooking"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'By the time' with a future event signals the future perfect progressive tense, 'will have been cooking,' to show an action continuing up to that future point; 'will cook' is tempting because it is also future tense, but it lacks the progressive aspect needed to show the two-hour duration.$q$),
  ($q$conv-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The data______surprising, prompting the team to repeat the experiment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In formal Standard English, 'data' is treated as a plural noun (the plural of 'datum'), so it takes the plural verb 'were.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The company reminded its employees to submit______expense reports by Friday.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'employees' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The twins each brought______own laptop to the study session.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The twins' is the plural antecedent, so 'their' is the only pronoun among the choices that agrees with it correctly.$q$),
  ($q$conv-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Walking through the old district, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["I finally saw the cathedral's spires come into view.","the cathedral's spires finally came into view.","a faded guidebook listed the cathedral's spires among the highlights.","the narrow streets led directly to the cathedral's spires."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Walking through the old district' describes a person, so the noun immediately after the comma must be the walker, 'I'; a guidebook or a set of streets cannot walk, even though both sentences read smoothly on their own.$q$),
  ($q$conv-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The internship taught her to manage budgets, delegate tasks, and______client meetings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lead","leading","how to lead","she led"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence lists parallel infinitive actions ('to manage,' '[to] delegate,' '[to] lead'), so 'lead' maintains the parallel structure.$q$),
  ($q$conv-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The award recognizes work that is original, rigorous, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["clearly written","clarity in writing","written with clarity","the writing is clear"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel adjective phrases ('original, rigorous, and ___'), and 'clearly written' matches that adjectival form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Yesterday, the technician inspected the wiring and______that a fuse had blown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["determined","determines","will determine","determining"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('Yesterday...inspected'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'determined,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the two finalists, the second speaker was the______prepared.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The storm knocked out power across the county______residents relied on generators for nearly a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," "," and, "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses ('The storm knocked out power...' and 'residents relied on generators...') must be joined with a semicolon (or a comma plus conjunction); only the semicolon is offered correctly here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The committee, along with its three advisors,______meeting every Thursday afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["holds","hold","holding","have held"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Along with its three advisors' is a parenthetical phrase, not part of the subject. The true subject is the singular 'committee,' which requires the singular verb 'holds.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Having rehearsed the presentation a dozen times______the intern still felt nervous walking into the boardroom.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," and "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An introductory participial phrase ('Having rehearsed...times') must be separated from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The city council recommended that the new ordinance______before the next fiscal year begins.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["take effect","takes effect","will take effect","took effect"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Recommended that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form of the verb, 'take effect,' regardless of the subject's number or the sentence's tense; the ordinary past-tense form 'took effect' is tempting but is indicative, not subjunctive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Only the applicants______submitted every required document were invited to interview.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","who, ",", who",", who,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'who submitted every required document' is essential (restrictive) to identifying which applicants are meant, so it takes no surrounding commas; ', who' is the closest trap because it would be correct only if the clause were nonessential, merely adding extra detail about applicants already identified.$q$),
  ($q$conv-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The workshop teaches participants not only how to write persuasive essays but also______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["how to revise them effectively","how revising them is done effectively","how effective their revisions are","revising them effectively"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not only...but also' requires matching grammatical forms; since the first element is the infinitive phrase 'how to write persuasive essays,' the second must match with 'how to revise them effectively.' The other choices keep the word 'how' or a gerund but drop the parallel infinitive structure, so they break the parallelism.$q$),
  ($q$conv-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe calls for three ingredients______flour, sugar, and butter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list that follows a complete independent clause ('The recipe calls for three ingredients').$q$),
  ($q$conv-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the manager nor the employees______aware of the policy change until the memo arrived.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In 'neither...nor' constructions, the verb agrees with the nearer subject. Since 'employees' (plural) is nearer, the plural verb 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The bridge closed for repairs at midnight______traffic was rerouted through downtown for the remainder of the week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction joining them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Everyone in the auditorium______asked to silence their phones before the performance began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The indefinite pronoun 'everyone' is grammatically singular, so it takes the singular verb 'was.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$After weeks of drought______the first heavy rainfall finally soaked the parched fields.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After weeks of drought' is an introductory prepositional phrase and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The band members loaded______instruments onto the bus before the sun rose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'band members' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The chef's pantry contained only the essentials______olive oil, garlic, and sea salt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the fire crew arrived, the flames______the roof.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already consumed","would have consumed","have already consumed","were already consuming"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the roof was consumed before another past event (the crew's arrival), the past perfect 'had already consumed' is required; 'would have consumed' is tempting because it also pairs two past events, but the conditional perfect implies an unmet hypothetical condition rather than simply reporting what had already happened.$q$),
  ($q$conv-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The final vote______a stunning, unanimous 7-to-2 decision—reshaped the court's approach to digital privacy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a stunning, unanimous 7-to-2 decision' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Scanning the horizon for storm clouds, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the sailor decided to postpone the voyage.","the voyage required postponing before nightfall.","storm clouds gathered quickly along the horizon.","the weather forecast warned of an approaching storm."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Scanning the horizon for storm clouds' describes a person performing the action, so the noun immediately after the comma must be that person, 'the sailor'; the voyage, the clouds, and the forecast cannot scan the horizon, even though each sentence is otherwise well formed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The negotiations stalled for weeks______both sides finally agreed to bring in a mediator.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and lack a coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon rather than a comma, which would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The award goes to______the committee considers most deserving.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whomever","whoever","whom","who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the clause, the pronoun functions as the object of 'considers' ('the committee considers whom'), so the objective case 'whomever' is required even though the whole clause follows the preposition 'to'; 'whoever' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were instead the subject of 'considers,' which it is not.$q$),
  ($q$conv-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Startled by the sudden thunderclap______the toddler began to cry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Startled by the sudden thunderclap' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the three routes into town, the coastal road is the______scenic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most","more","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing three or more items, the superlative form 'most' is required rather than the comparative 'more,' which is used for exactly two.$q$),
  ($q$conv-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The panel featured three experts______Dr. Alavi, a virologist; Ms. Ferreira, an epidemiologist; and Mr. Osei, a public health researcher.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause, and because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items; the semicolon is the closest trap, since semicolons do correctly separate the list's items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The corporation announced that______quarterly earnings had exceeded projections.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","their","it is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive pronoun 'its' (no apostrophe) is needed to show the corporation's earnings; 'it's' is a contraction of 'it is' and does not fit here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The council approved the new budget______but several residents voiced concerns at the meeting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'But' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new policy is intended both to reduce costs and to______employee satisfaction.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["improve","improving","improvement of","the improvement in"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Both...and' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the infinitive 'to reduce,' the second must match with the base verb 'improve' after 'to.' 'Improving' is the closest trap because it still reads smoothly after 'to,' but pairing 'to' with a gerund breaks the required infinitive parallel with 'to reduce.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Books______written before 1900 are displayed in the rare collections room.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Written before 1900' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which books are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The old photographs had______in the attic for decades before anyone rediscovered them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lain","laid","lay","layed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The intransitive verb 'lie' (to rest in a place) takes the past participle 'lain' after 'had.' 'Laid' is the closest trap because it is the past participle of the similar-sounding verb 'lay,' but 'lay' is transitive and requires a direct object, which this sentence does not have.$q$),
  ($q$conv-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The proposal was, however______far too costly for the city's current budget.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'However' interrupts a single independent clause rather than joining two clauses, so it must be enclosed in a pair of commas; since the first comma is already given, a matching comma is needed here. The semicolon is the closest trap because 'however' often pairs with a semicolon when it joins two independent clauses, but here it merely interrupts one clause, which calls for commas instead.$q$),
  ($q$conv-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Fifteen minutes______enough time to finish the quiz, according to the instructor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When an amount of time is treated as a single unit rather than individual minutes, it takes a singular verb, so 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bridge was closed for inspection______consequently, commuters faced long delays for a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Consequently' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Her weekend routine consists of jogging, reading, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cooking","to cook","she cooks","cooked meals"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('jogging, reading, and ___'), so 'cooking' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the trail was covered in ice______the rangers closed it to hikers until spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Because the trail was covered in ice' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$One of the boxes in the storage room______missing when the inventory was taken.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun 'one,' not the object of the preposition 'boxes,' so the singular verb 'was' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Even though the museum extended its hours for the holidays______attendance remained lower than expected.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Even though the museum extended its hours for the holidays' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so it must be joined to the independent clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The negotiations proceeded______than either side had anticipated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more smoothly","smoother","more smooth","smoothly more"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb 'proceeded' must be modified by an adverb, not an adjective, so 'more smoothly' is correct. 'Smoother' is the closest trap because it is a natural-sounding comparative, but as an adjective it cannot modify the verb 'proceeded.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After centuries of naked-eye observation______astronomers finally photographed a black hole directly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After centuries of naked-eye observation' is an introductory prepositional phrase and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The school of herring______toward deeper water every evening.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["moves","move","have moved","are moving"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'School' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'moves.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The cathedral's stained-glass windows survived two world wars______a stray ember from a nearby fire nearly destroyed them in 1970.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Last spring, archaeologists excavated the site and______several intact pottery shards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["uncovered","uncover","will uncover","uncovering"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('excavated'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'uncovered,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The central bank's decision______a quarter-point rate cut, its first in three years—surprised most analysts who had expected no change.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a quarter-point rate cut, its first in three years' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The research team ensured that______data collection methods met ethical standards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","their","his","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Team' functions as a singular collective noun acting as one unit, so it takes the singular possessive 'its,' not the plural 'their.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the wetland absorbs excess rainfall______nearby towns rarely experience severe flooding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Because the wetland absorbs excess rainfall' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The chef's morning routine includes tasting sauces, plating desserts, and______the walk-in cooler.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["restocking","restock","to restock","she restocks"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('tasting,' 'plating,' and ___), so 'restocking' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The expedition relied on three specialists______Dr. Nkemelu, a glaciologist; Ms. Petrova, a mountaineer; and Mr. Diallo, a logistics coordinator.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause, and because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items; the semicolon is the closest trap, since semicolons do correctly separate the list's items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$There______several dialects spoken across the small island chain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject that follows, 'several dialects,' is plural, so the verb must agree with it: 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Adjusting the aperture for low light______the photographer captured the dim alleyway in sharp detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Adjusting the aperture for low light' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the two compounds tested, the second proved the______stable under heat.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The fault line, ______ runs beneath three counties, has not produced a major earthquake in decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the fault line, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The board insisted that every member______the annual report before the vote.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["review","reviews","reviewed","will review"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Insisted that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form of the verb, 'review,' regardless of the subject's number; 'reviews' is tempting because it agrees with the singular 'member,' but ordinary subject-verb agreement does not apply inside a subjunctive clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The flight was rerouted due to turbulence______and passengers arrived nearly an hour late.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'And' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Researchers______the ancient manuscript for nearly a decade, and they have yet to translate it fully.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have studied","studied","study","will study"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The action began in the past and continues into the present ('for nearly a decade...have yet to'), so the present perfect 'have studied' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Plants______native to arid climates often store water in thick, fleshy leaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Native to arid climates' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which plants are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Designed to withstand extreme temperatures, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the rover continued transmitting data from the Martian surface.","the Martian surface posed new engineering challenges for the team.","mission engineers monitored the rover's systems around the clock.","the data collected revealed surprising temperature swings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Designed to withstand extreme temperatures' describes the rover, so the noun immediately after the comma must be 'the rover'; the surface, the engineers, and the data were not themselves designed for that purpose, even though each choice forms a complete, sensible sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Inflation slowed in the first quarter______unemployment climbed to its highest level in five years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$City planners asked residents to submit______feedback on the proposed bike lanes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'residents' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After tuning each string by ear______the violinist began the rehearsal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After tuning each string by ear' is an introductory phrase and requires a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The number of stray cats in the neighborhood______significantly since the shelter opened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has decreased","have decreased","decrease","are decreasing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The number of' is treated as singular, so it takes the singular verb 'has decreased'; this differs from 'a number of,' which takes a plural verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The coin______a rare 1943 copper penny, one of only a dozen known to exist—sold at auction for more than one million dollars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a rare 1943 copper penny, one of only a dozen known to exist' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The entomologist observed the colony for three days and then______her findings in a field journal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["recorded","records","will record","recording"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('observed'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'recorded,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The old-growth forest had never been logged______conservationists fought hard to keep it that way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The majority of the ballots______already been counted by midnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had","has","having","was"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In 'the majority of the ballots,' the verb agrees with the plural noun 'ballots,' so the plural 'had' is correct, not the singular 'has.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The cipher revealed a single truth______the message had been hidden in plain sight all along.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the truth was, following a complete independent clause; the semicolon is the closest trap because it could also join these two independent clauses, but it lacks the colon's specific signal that what follows defines or explains the 'single truth' just mentioned.$q$),
  ($q$conv-87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The dough had______for two hours before the baker shaped it into loaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["risen","raised","rose","raise"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The intransitive verb 'rise' (to go up on its own) takes the past participle 'risen' after 'had'; 'raised' is the past participle of the transitive verb 'raise,' which requires a direct object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The marathon route wound through the historic district______but organizers rerouted it after the storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'But' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The exhibit's centerpiece was praised as bold, inventive, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["thought-provoking","provoking thought","thoughts were provoked","provoker of thought"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel adjectives ('bold, inventive, and ___'), and 'thought-provoking' matches that adjectival form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The novel______won the award last year has been translated into a dozen languages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'won the award last year' is essential to identifying which novel is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If the sample______contaminated, the results would have shown far greater variance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had been","was","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The main clause 'would have shown' signals a past unreal conditional, which requires the past perfect 'had been' in the if-clause. 'Were' is the closest trap because it is the correct subjunctive form for a present contrary-to-fact condition, but this sentence is about a past, completed event, which needs the past perfect instead.$q$),
  ($q$conv-92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Consumer demand for sustainable fabrics grew rapidly______nonetheless, many manufacturers were slow to adapt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Nonetheless' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The volcano erupted without warning, and ash______the sky for nearly a week afterward.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["darkened","darkens","will darken","darkening"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('erupted'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'darkened,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The author's debut work______a slim volume of interconnected short stories, published when she was only twenty-two—went largely unnoticed until decades after her death.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a slim volume of interconnected short stories, published when she was only twenty-two' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The newly discovered fossil is older than______specimen found in the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["any other","any","all","every"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the fossil is itself a specimen found in the region, it must be compared to 'any other specimen' to exclude itself from the comparison. 'Any' is the closest trap because it almost completes the idiom correctly, but without 'other' it illogically includes the fossil among the things it is being compared to.$q$),
  ($q$conv-96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The beekeeper checked the hive every week______the colony's honey production still declined sharply in late summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Although the bridge had been inspected twice______engineers still worried about its structural integrity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Although the bridge had been inspected twice' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The warehouse lost power during the storm______the backup generators kicked in within seconds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The jury reached a unanimous decision______the defendant was guilty beyond a reasonable doubt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the decision was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Volunteers______available on weekends were assigned to the overnight shift.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Available on weekends' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which volunteers are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The lighthouse, ______ has guided ships since 1887, was recently restored by local volunteers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the lighthouse, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The proposal lacked sufficient funding______moreover, it ignored community feedback entirely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Moreover' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Drenched by the sudden downpour______the hikers sought shelter under a rocky outcrop.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Drenched by the sudden downpour' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The gallery's new exhibit features three mediums______oil painting, charcoal sketching, and mixed-media collage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The forecast predicted clear skies all weekend______a sudden storm rolled in Saturday afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The lighthouse keeper's logbook, ______ kept meticulously since 1901 by three generations of the same family, records every ship that has passed the point.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been","that has been","who has been","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas describes a thing (the logbook), not the family mentioned later in the sentence, so it needs the thing-pronoun 'which' with a complete verb phrase; 'who has been' is tempting because people are mentioned nearby, but the clause still modifies 'logbook.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The negotiations dragged on for months______yet both sides eventually reached a fair settlement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Yet' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's base______a slab of unpolished granite, cut from a single quarry block—weighs nearly two tons.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a slab of unpolished granite, cut from a single quarry block' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The itinerary included stops in three cities______Lisbon, a coastal capital; Porto, a riverside hub; and Braga, a historic university town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause, and because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items; the semicolon is the closest trap, since semicolons do correctly separate the list's items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The results were, indeed______surprising to everyone on the research team.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Indeed' interrupts a single independent clause rather than joining two clauses, so it must be enclosed in a pair of commas; since the first comma is already given, a matching comma is needed here. The semicolon is the closest trap because interrupters like 'indeed' can also join two independent clauses with a semicolon before and comma after, but here 'indeed' only interrupts one clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Even though the airline added extra flights for the holidays______demand still outpaced the available seats.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Even though the airline added extra flights for the holidays' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so it must be joined to the independent clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The software update______fixed the security flaw was released without any public announcement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'fixed the security flaw' is essential to identifying which update is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The______testimony contradicted the physical evidence presented at trial.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["witness's","witnesses'","witnesses","witness"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only one witness is referenced here, so the singular possessive is formed by adding an apostrophe and 's': 'witness's.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The______complaints finally prompted the city to repave the entire street.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["residents'","resident's","residents","residents's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since many residents are complaining, the plural possessive 'residents'' is required, formed by adding only an apostrophe after the final s.$q$),
  ($q$conv-115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$______research on coral bleaching was published in a leading science journal after three years of fieldwork.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Maria and Carlos's","Maria's and Carlos's","Maria and Carlos'","Maria's and Carlos'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because Maria and Carlos jointly produced one shared piece of research, only the final name takes the possessive marker, and a singular name ending in 's,' like Carlos, still takes 's' to form that possessive: 'Carlos's.' Marking both names as possessive ('Maria's and Carlos's') would wrongly imply two separate studies, and dropping the 's' after Carlos ('Carlos'') is the wrong way to form a singular possessive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The company revised______return policy after receiving dozens of customer complaints.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","its'","it is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive 'its' (no apostrophe) shows that the policy belongs to the company; 'it's' is a contraction of 'it is,' and 'its'' is not a standard word.$q$),
  ($q$conv-117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee could not determine______proposal had been submitted first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","who's","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whose' is the possessive form needed here, since the proposal belongs to someone; 'who's' is a contraction of 'who is,' which does not fit this context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Many city parks have cut back their watering schedules this summer______for example, the botanical garden now waters its lawns only twice a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'For example' introduces a second independent clause, so like a conjunctive adverb it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma. The comma is the closest trap because 'for example' is often set off by commas when it interrupts a single clause, but here it introduces a whole new independent clause, which requires the stronger semicolon break.$q$),
  ($q$conv-119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The ferry service was suspended due to high winds______so passengers had to wait at the terminal overnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'So' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The chef plated the dessert with great precision______and the diners applauded as it arrived at the table.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'And' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The keynote speaker______a leading expert in renewable energy, discussed the future of solar storage technology.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A leading expert in renewable energy' is a nonessential appositive renaming 'the keynote speaker,' so it must be set off with a pair of commas; since the closing comma is already given, the opening comma is needed here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The scholarship committee selected the applicant______the faculty panel had unanimously recommended.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","which","that"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the clause, the pronoun functions as the object of 'recommended' ('the panel had recommended whom'), so the objective case 'whom' is required; 'who' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were the subject of 'recommended' rather than its object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe requires______flour, sugar, and eggs, all of which must be measured precisely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Flour, sugar, and eggs' serves as the direct object of 'requires,' so nothing should separate the verb from its object. The colon is the closest trap because colons often introduce lists, but a colon cannot separate a verb from its own direct object the way it can follow a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The construction crew finished the east wing ahead of schedule______meanwhile, the west wing fell further behind.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Meanwhile' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Whenever the river crests above flood stage______the town opens its emergency shelters without delay.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whenever the river crests above flood stage' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Since the archive had no digital backup of the records______staff spent weeks re-scanning every page by hand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Since the archive had no digital backup of the records' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so a comma is needed to join it to the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Everyone in the office dreaded the______latest round of budget cuts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["boss's","bosses'","boss'","bosses"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a singular noun ending in 's' ('boss'), Standard English still adds 's to form the possessive: 'boss's'; 'boss'' omits the required s.$q$),
  ($q$conv-128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee's final report—delayed for nearly a year by budget disputes, lawsuits, and staff turnover______was finally released to the public.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'delayed for nearly a year by budget disputes, lawsuits, and staff turnover' already contains internal commas separating its list, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can close the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside ends.$q$),
  ($q$conv-129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Dr. Alvarez, ______ has led the clinic for over a decade, announced her retirement last week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","that","which","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas refers to a person, Dr. Alvarez, and functions as the subject of 'has led,' so the subjective case 'who' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The archivist digitized the entire photo collection______preserving images that had begun to fade badly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Preserving images that had begun to fade badly' is a participial phrase modifying the whole preceding clause, so it must be set off with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The inspector's report identified a single cause of the collapse______corroded support beams.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the cause was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$She finished her shift at midnight______she drove straight home without stopping.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The password______protects the company's servers must be changed every ninety days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'protects the company's servers' is essential to identifying which password is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct rather than 'which.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The manager and the head chef______responsible for approving the new seasonal menu.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A compound subject joined by 'and' ('the manager and the head chef') is plural, so it takes the plural verb 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Either the plumber or the electricians______available to come by tomorrow morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'electricians,' which is plural, so 'are' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$None of the cake______left by the time the guests arrived at the party.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Cake' here is a noncount (mass) noun referring to a single whole, so 'none' takes the singular verb 'was.' 'Were' is the closest trap because 'none' can take a plural verb when it refers to a count noun ('none of the cookies were left'), but 'cake' here is treated as an uncountable whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$None of the volunteers______willing to work the overnight shift on such short notice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Volunteers' is a plural count noun, so 'none of the volunteers' takes the plural verb 'were.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The nonprofit updated______mission statement to reflect its expanded community programs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","their","his","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Nonprofit' is a singular organization, so it takes the singular possessive pronoun 'its,' not the plural 'their.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The disagreement about the budget was strictly between the treasurer and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","I","myself","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$As the object of the preposition 'between,' the objective case 'me' is required, not the subjective 'I.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The director asked Priya and______to present the quarterly results.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","myself","I","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$As the object of the verb 'asked,' the objective pronoun 'me' is required; 'myself' is a reflexive pronoun and cannot stand in as a simple object here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The new assembly line produces______defective parts than the old one did.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["fewer","less","lesser","little"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Defective parts' is a countable plural noun, so the countable modifier 'fewer' is required rather than 'less,' which modifies noncount nouns.$q$),
  ($q$conv-142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The council must either approve the new zoning law or______it back to committee for revision.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["send","sending","the sending of","to send"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Either...or' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the base verb 'approve' (governed by 'must'), the second must match with the base verb 'send.' 'To send' is the closest trap because it reads naturally, but reinserting 'to' breaks the parallel with the bare infinitive 'approve.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The clinic______over three thousand patients since it opened its doors in 2015.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has treated","treated","treats","will treat"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An action that began in the past and continues up to the present, marked by 'since,' requires the present perfect tense, 'has treated.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The pilot reported that the storm______by the time the plane reached cruising altitude.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already passed","would have passed","has already passed","was already passing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the storm passed before another past event (the plane's reaching altitude), the past perfect 'had already passed' is required to show the earlier action; 'would have passed' is tempting because it also relates two past events, but the conditional perfect wrongly implies the passing depended on some unstated condition.$q$),
  ($q$conv-145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The new lighting system did not______the plants' growth rate in any measurable way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["affect","effect","affects","effected"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Affect' is the verb meaning 'to influence,' which fits the sentence; 'effect' is primarily a noun meaning 'a result.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The second applicant was far more qualified______the first.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["than","then","that","when"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Than' is used to introduce a comparison; 'then' refers to time and does not fit this context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Every department submitted its budget report______the marketing team, which requested an extension.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["except","accept","excepting for","excepted"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Except' means 'excluding,' which fits the meaning here; 'accept' means 'to receive or agree to' and does not make sense in this context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The warranty______covers manufacturing defects, not damage caused by misuse.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["only","the only","only that","that only"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Placing 'only' immediately before the verb 'covers' correctly limits the meaning to manufacturing defects alone, matching the contrast drawn in the second half of the sentence. 'That only' is the closest trap because it still precedes 'covers,' but inserting 'that' turns the sentence into an incomplete relative-clause fragment rather than a complete main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Either the judge or the jury______the final say on the sentencing recommendation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","have","having","had been having"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, the singular 'jury,' so 'has' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Two-thirds of the orchard______damaged by the early frost last spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With fractions, the verb agrees with the noun in the 'of' phrase; 'orchard' is singular, so the singular verb 'was' is correct. 'Were' is the closest trap because fractions take a plural verb when the noun that follows is plural ('two-thirds of the apples were damaged'), but here the noun is the singular 'orchard.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The veteran guide______the hikers safely down the mountain before the storm hit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["led","lead","leaded","has lead"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The past-tense form of the verb 'lead' is spelled 'led'; 'lead' is either the present tense or the name of the metal, neither of which fits this past-tense sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The audience showed______appreciation with a long standing ovation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","their","it's","them"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Audience' is a singular collective noun acting as one unit, so it takes the singular possessive 'its,' not the plural 'their.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The detective finally identified______had tampered with the evidence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","whom","whoever's","which"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun functions as the subject of 'had tampered,' so the subjective case 'who' is required, not the objective 'whom.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The internship involved shadowing senior engineers, documenting lab results, and______client presentations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reviewing","review","to review","the review of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('shadowing,' 'documenting,' and ___), so 'reviewing' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Among the finalists______two former Olympic swimmers from the same training club.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In this inverted sentence, the true subject, 'two former Olympic swimmers,' follows the verb and is plural, so the plural verb 'were' is required even though it comes first. 'Was' is the closest trap because a reader might wrongly match the verb to the nearer word 'finalists' in the opening phrase instead of to the actual subject after the verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$When the results______from the lab, the research team will schedule a press conference.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["arrive","will arrive","would arrive","arriving"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a future time clause introduced by 'when,' Standard English uses the simple present tense, 'arrive,' rather than 'will arrive,' even though the overall meaning is future.$q$),
  ($q$conv-157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The hikers decided to travel ten miles______before setting up camp for the night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["farther","further","farthest","furthest"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Farther' refers to physical distance, which fits this context, while 'further' is generally reserved for figurative or additional extent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The farm reported a significant______of crop losses after the unexpected hailstorm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["number","amount","quantity of","numbering"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Crop losses' is a countable plural noun, so 'number' is the correct modifier; 'amount' is reserved for noncount nouns.$q$),
  ($q$conv-159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Although the volcano had been dormant for nearly two centuries______geologists continued to monitor its seismic activity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Although the volcano had been dormant for nearly two centuries' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Blending seamlessly into the coral______the octopus evaded the diver's camera for several minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Blending seamlessly into the coral' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The library's renovation took far longer than planned______the reading room did not reopen until late autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Merchants along the Silk Road carried a range of valuable goods______silk, spices, and precious stones.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The stock's price rebounded quickly after the announcement______however, analysts remained skeptical of its long-term growth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'However' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The food truck's line stretched around the block all afternoon______but the owners ran out of ingredients by five o'clock.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'But' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The antique clock, ______ had not kept accurate time in decades, was finally repaired by a local craftsman.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the clock, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The satellite's final component______a solar panel engineered to unfold in orbit, its hinges modeled on origami—was tested rigorously before launch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a solar panel engineered to unfold in orbit, its hinges modeled on origami' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$During her solo, the______bow suddenly snapped, forcing her to borrow one from the concertmaster.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["violinist's","violinists'","violinists","violinist"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun 'her' earlier in the sentence shows that only one violinist is being discussed, so the singular possessive 'violinist's' is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The______lockers were inspected after several reports of missing textbooks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["students'","student's","students","students's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since many students' lockers are involved, the plural possessive 'students'' is required, formed by adding only an apostrophe after the final s.$q$),
  ($q$conv-169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The sensor______malfunctioned during testing was replaced before the device shipped.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'malfunctioned during testing' is essential to identifying which sensor is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct rather than 'which.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Climbers______attempting the north face must register with the ranger station in advance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Attempting the north face' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which climbers are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The mural, ______ repainted twice since its unveiling by the same artist who created the original, has become a symbol of the neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been","that has been","who has been","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas describes a thing (the mural), not the artist named later in the sentence, so it needs the thing-pronoun 'which' with a complete verb phrase; 'who has been' is tempting because a person is mentioned nearby, but the clause still modifies 'mural.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Firefighters contained the blaze within two days______the surrounding hillsides remained charred for months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Regulators raised concerns about the exchange's security practices______therefore, several investors withdrew their funds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Therefore' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$She shaped the clay carefully on the wheel______and the instructor praised her steady hands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'And' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The Antarctic station housed three researchers______Dr. Lindqvist, a climatologist; Mr. Oyelaran, a mechanic; and Ms. Voss, a physician.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause, and because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items; the semicolon is the closest trap, since semicolons do correctly separate the list's items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the tsunami warning system detected the tremor within seconds______coastal towns had time to evacuate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Because the tsunami warning system detected the tremor within seconds' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$After decades buried beneath the peat bog______the Viking ship's timbers were finally excavated intact.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After decades buried beneath the peat bog' is an introductory prepositional phrase and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The orchestra's new conductor—trained at a conservatory in Vienna, where she studied under two renowned mentors______surprised the board by programming an entirely contemporary season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'trained at a conservatory in Vienna, where she studied under two renowned mentors' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can close the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside ends.$q$),
  ($q$conv-179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The expedition's lead scientist______a marine biologist specializing in deep-sea ecosystems, briefed the crew before the dive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A marine biologist specializing in deep-sea ecosystems' is a nonessential appositive renaming 'the lead scientist,' so it must be set off with a pair of commas; since the closing comma is already given, the opening comma is needed here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee dedicated the award to the volunteer______they credited with founding the animal shelter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","which","that"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the clause, the pronoun functions as the object of 'credited' ('they credited whom'), so the objective case 'whom' is required; 'who' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were the subject of the clause rather than its object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Professor Alemu, ______ has studied medieval trade guilds for thirty years, published her findings last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","that","which","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas refers to a person, Professor Alemu, and functions as the subject of 'has studied,' so the subjective case 'who' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Pedaling against a fierce headwind______the cyclist fell behind the lead pack during the final stretch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Pedaling against a fierce headwind' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The startup's app crashed repeatedly during the demo______investors still expressed interest in funding the project.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The planetarium's new show reveals a startling fact______most stars visible at night no longer exist in their observed form.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the fact was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The brewery's new recipe sold out within hours______so the owners doubled production for the following week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'So' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The troupe's final rehearsal______a full run-through in costume, complete with the orchestra—convinced the director the show was ready.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a full run-through in costume, complete with the orchestra' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The traffic study revealed severe congestion downtown______consequently, the city fast-tracked the new bus rapid transit line.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Consequently' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Since the blood bank's reserves had dropped to a critical low______the hospital organized an emergency donation drive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Since the blood bank's reserves had dropped to a critical low' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The ingredient______triggered the allergic reaction was removed from the recipe immediately.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'triggered the allergic reaction' is essential to identifying which ingredient is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The suspension bridge, ______ engineered by a team that had never designed for hurricane-force winds before, has never sustained structural damage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which was","that was","who was","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas describes a thing (the bridge), not the team mentioned later in the sentence, so it needs the thing-pronoun 'which' with a complete verb phrase; 'who was' is tempting because a team of people is mentioned nearby, but the clause still modifies 'bridge.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$______proposal for the new wing was approved unanimously by the board.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Chen and Okafor's","Chen's and Okafor's","Chen and Okafor's'","Chens and Okafor's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because Chen and Okafor jointly produced one shared proposal, only the final name takes the apostrophe and s; marking both names as possessive would incorrectly suggest two separate proposals.$q$),
  ($q$conv-192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Drivers______caught speeding in the school zone will receive an automatic fine.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Caught speeding in the school zone' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which drivers are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The curator and her assistant______responsible for cataloging every artifact in the new wing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A compound subject joined by 'and' ('the curator and her assistant') is plural, so it takes the plural verb 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The researchers reviewed______data before submitting the paper for publication.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'researchers' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The panel of judges______unanimous in awarding first prize to the young violinist.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Panel' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'was,' regardless of the plural noun 'judges' in the prepositional phrase that follows it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The praise for the championship win was shared equally between the team captain and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","I","myself","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$As the object of the preposition 'between,' the objective case 'me' is required, not the subjective 'I.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The exhibit was praised not only for its striking visuals but also for______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its clear explanation of complex science","explaining complex science with clarity","how clearly it explained complex science","the complex science it explained clearly"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not only...but also' pairs two objects of 'for'; since the first is the possessive noun phrase 'its striking visuals,' the second must match that form: 'its clear explanation of complex science.' The other choices are grammatical as objects of 'for' but shift to a gerund phrase or a clause, breaking the parallel structure.$q$),
  ($q$conv-198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The diver descended slowly and then______for the shipwreck visible below.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["searched","searches","will search","searching"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('descended'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'searched,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Carving late into the night, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the sculptor finally finished the marble bust.","the marble bust took its final shape under the studio lights.","the studio remained quiet except for the scrape of the chisel.","exhaustion finally caught up with the long project."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Carving late into the night' describes a person performing the action, so the noun immediately after the comma must be that person, 'the sculptor'; the bust, the studio, and the exhaustion cannot carve, even though each choice reads as a complete, plausible sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The board recommended that the policy______revised before the next fiscal quarter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["be","is","was","will be"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Recommended that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form 'be' regardless of the subject's number or the sentence's tense; 'will be' is tempting because the revision is a future event, but the subjunctive 'be' is required after a verb of recommendation, not an ordinary future tense.$q$),
  ($q$conv-201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Everybody at the conference______asked to wear a name badge at all times.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The indefinite pronoun 'everybody' is grammatically singular, so it takes the singular verb 'was.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the train reached the station, the passengers______for nearly three hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had been waiting","waited","would have been waiting","are waiting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the waiting began before and continued up to another past event (the train's arrival), the past perfect progressive 'had been waiting' is required; 'would have been waiting' is tempting because it also combines past time with progressive aspect, but the conditional form wrongly implies the waiting depended on some unstated condition.$q$),
  ($q$conv-203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The grant will go to______submits the most innovative proposal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whoever","whomever","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun functions as the subject of 'submits' within its own clause, so the subjective case 'whoever' is correct even though the entire clause follows the preposition 'to'; 'whomever' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were instead the object of a verb inside the clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Of the two bridges spanning the river, the older one is the______traveled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Of the three trails leading up the ridge, the eastern one is the______difficult.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most","more","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing three or more items, the superlative form 'most' is required rather than the comparative 'more,' which is used for exactly two.$q$),
  ($q$conv-206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A number of residents______attended the town hall to voice their concerns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","has","having","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A number of' is treated as plural, so it takes the plural verb 'have'; this differs from 'the number of,' which takes a singular verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The tools had______on the workbench untouched since the shop's relocation last spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sat","set","sitten","setted"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The intransitive verb 'sit' (to rest in a place) takes the past participle 'sat' after 'had'; 'set' is the past participle of the transitive verb 'set,' which requires a direct object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The coupon______applies to online purchases, not in-store ones.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["only","the only","only that","that only"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Placing 'only' immediately before the verb 'applies' correctly limits the meaning to online purchases alone, matching the contrast drawn in the second half of the sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Her role as project manager involves tracking budgets, scheduling inspections, and______client questions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["answering","answer","to answer","the answering of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('tracking,' 'scheduling,' and ___), so 'answering' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The new recipe contains______sugar than the original version.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["less","fewer","lesser","little"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Sugar' is a noncount (mass) noun, so the noncount modifier 'less' is required rather than 'fewer,' which modifies countable plural nouns.$q$),
  ($q$conv-211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The new tax policy had a significant______on small businesses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["effect","affect","effects","affecting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Effect' is the noun meaning 'a result,' which fits the sentence; 'affect' is primarily a verb meaning 'to influence.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The results of the second exam were better______anyone had predicted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["than","then","that","when"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Than' is used to introduce a comparison; 'then' refers to time and does not fit this context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Someone left______umbrella in the lobby after the meeting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With an indefinite singular antecedent like 'someone' whose gender is unspecified, Standard English uses the pronoun 'their'; 'its' is reserved for things, not people.$q$),
  ($q$conv-214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the critic arrived, the chef______the entire tasting menu.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already prepared","would have prepared","has already prepared","was already preparing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the menu was prepared before another past event (the critic's arrival), the past perfect 'had already prepared' is required; 'would have prepared' is tempting because it also links two past events, but the conditional perfect wrongly implies the preparation depended on some unstated condition.$q$),
  ($q$conv-215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The teacher insisted that each student______their essay before the deadline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["submit","submits","submitted","will submit"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Insisted that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form 'submit' regardless of the subject's number; 'submits' is tempting because it matches the singular 'student,' but subjunctive clauses do not follow ordinary subject-verb agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Hoping to capture the eclipse, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the photographer set up her tripod before dawn.","the tripod stood ready on the hillside before dawn.","dawn broke just as the eclipse began.","careful planning made the early morning shoot possible."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Hoping to capture the eclipse' describes a person, so the noun immediately after the comma must be that person, 'the photographer'; the tripod, the dawn, and the planning cannot hope, even though each choice is a complete, sensible sentence on its own.$q$),
  ($q$conv-217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$There______dozens of species native to this single valley.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject that follows, 'dozens of species,' is plural, so the verb must agree with it: 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The committee could not agree on______to send to the conference.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","whoever","which"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun functions as the object of the infinitive 'to send,' so the objective case 'whom' is required, not the subjective 'who.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new curriculum aims both to challenge advanced students and to______struggling ones.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["support","supporting","the support of","supportive of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Both...and' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the infinitive 'to challenge,' the second must match with the base verb 'support' after 'to.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The two attorneys agreed to discuss the settlement______before presenting it to the judge.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["further","farther","furthest","farthest"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Further' refers to figurative or additional extent, which fits this context, while 'farther' is generally reserved for physical distance.$q$),
  ($q$conv-221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the blacksmith tempered the blade slowly______the metal gained unusual flexibility.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Because the blacksmith tempered the blade slowly' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Measuring the hem twice before cutting______the tailor avoided a costly mistake.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Measuring the hem twice before cutting' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The vineyard's harvest was delayed by a week of rain______workers picked the grapes in a single frantic weekend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The subway line lost signal power downtown______riders waited on the platform for nearly an hour.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon rather than a comma, which would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The greenhouse cultivates three varieties of orchid______the moth orchid, the lady's slipper, and the vanda.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The cartographer discovered the error at last______the coastline had been drawn nearly a mile off.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the error was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The turbine, ______ stands nearly four hundred feet tall, generates enough power for two thousand homes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the turbine, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Chisels______left out in the rain overnight had begun to rust by morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Left out in the rain overnight' is a restrictive (essential) phrase identifying which chisels are meant, so no comma is needed before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bylaw______the board passed by a single vote takes effect next month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'the board passed by a single vote' is essential to identifying which bylaw is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The scholarship is awarded to______the selection committee judges most committed to public service.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whomever","whoever","whom","who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the clause, the pronoun functions as the object of 'judges' ('the committee judges whom'), so the objective case 'whomever' is required even though the whole clause follows the preposition 'to'; 'whoever' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were the subject of 'judges' rather than its object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The party selected a candidate______voters already trusted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","whoever","whomever"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of 'trusted' ('voters trusted whom'), so the objective case 'whom' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The company's new headquarters______a repurposed textile mill dating to the 1890s, complete with its original smokestack—opened to employees six months ahead of schedule.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a repurposed textile mill dating to the 1890s, complete with its original smokestack' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The senator's proposal______a modest increase in the minimum wage, phased in over three years—drew fierce opposition from business groups.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a modest increase in the minimum wage, phased in over three years' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The gem's one imperfection______a faint cloud near its center, visible only under magnification—reduced its appraised value significantly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a faint cloud near its center, visible only under magnification' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The architect's final sketch______a sweeping glass atrium flooded with light from a retractable roof—became the building's most celebrated feature.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a sweeping glass atrium flooded with light from a retractable roof' is a single nonessential aside about the sketch, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open it without being mistaken for a new independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The factory's output rose sharply after the upgrade______however, training for the new machines lagged behind.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'However' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The clinic's wait times grew longer every month______moreover, several nurses resigned in frustration.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Moreover' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Sales slowed on the east coast throughout the winter______meanwhile, the western branch posted record numbers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Meanwhile' is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so it must be preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The cruise ship departed ten minutes late______but it still reached the harbor before the tide turned.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'But' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The students could present their findings in class______or they could submit a written report instead.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Or' is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so a comma must precede it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the stagehands finished setting the props______the actors ran through their final scene.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After the stagehands finished setting the props' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Weighed down by a stack of packages______the courier struggled up the icy steps.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Weighed down by a stack of packages' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Once the auditors finished reviewing the accounts______they flagged three irregular transactions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Once the auditors finished reviewing the accounts' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Despite months of careful planning______the product launch was postponed at the last minute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Despite months of careful planning' is an introductory prepositional phrase and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The printing press broke down twice during the run______the morning edition still reached every newsstand on time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The region's main reservoir dropped to half its usual level______farmers downstream began rationing irrigation water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon rather than a comma, which would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The new phone's reviews were mixed at best______pre-orders still shattered the company's previous sales record.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$A software bug disabled the booking system for six hours______call-center staff processed every reservation by hand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction joining them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The hardware store restocked three essential items before the storm______batteries, bottled water, and plywood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The conference featured three keynote speakers______Dr. Hassan, an immunologist; Ms. Okafor, a biostatistician; and Mr. Weiss, a public policy advisor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list following the complete independent clause; because the items themselves contain commas, semicolons separate the items, but only a colon can introduce the list as a whole.$q$),
  ($q$conv-251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The investigators reached a single conclusion after months of analysis______the leak had originated in the server room.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the conclusion was, following a complete independent clause; the semicolon is the closest trap because it could also join these two independent clauses, but it lacks the colon's specific signal that what follows defines or explains the conclusion just mentioned.$q$),
  ($q$conv-252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The treaty, ______ took nearly a decade to negotiate, finally ended the dispute over fishing rights.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the treaty, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The dam, ______ operating continuously since 1950 under the supervision of a single engineering firm, underwent its first major inspection last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been","that has been","who has been","being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas describes a thing (the dam), not the firm mentioned later in the sentence, so it needs the thing-pronoun 'which' with a complete verb phrase; 'who has been' is tempting because an organization of people is mentioned nearby, but the clause still modifies 'dam.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Only the nurses______had completed the advanced certification were assigned to the trauma unit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","who, ",", who",", who,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'who had completed the advanced certification' is essential (restrictive) to identifying which nurses are meant, so it takes no surrounding commas.$q$),
  ($q$conv-255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The choir______every Tuesday evening in the chapel basement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["practices","practice","have practiced","are practicing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Choir' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'practices.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Nobody on the construction crew______aware of the schedule change until the foreman announced it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The indefinite pronoun 'nobody' is grammatically singular, so it takes the singular verb 'was.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$One of the paintings in the gallery______damaged during shipping.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","are","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the sentence is the singular pronoun 'one,' not the object of the preposition 'paintings,' so the singular verb 'was' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the pilot nor the flight attendants______informed of the delay until boarding had already begun.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In 'neither...nor' constructions, the verb agrees with the nearer subject. Since 'flight attendants' (plural) is nearer, the plural verb 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Either the landlord or the tenants______responsible for the fence repair when the inspector visited last week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'tenants,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A number of volunteers______already signed up for the weekend cleanup.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","has","having","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A number of' is treated as plural, so it takes the plural verb 'have'; this differs from 'the number of,' which takes a singular verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The number of applicants this year______higher than last year's total.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The number of' is treated as singular, so it takes the singular verb 'is'; this differs from 'a number of,' which takes a plural verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Ten minutes______plenty of time to cross the busy intersection safely, according to the crossing guard.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When a span of time is treated as a single unit rather than individual minutes, it takes a singular verb, so 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The passengers gathered______belongings before exiting the train.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'passengers' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The task force submitted______recommendations to the governor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","their","his","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Task force' functions as a singular collective noun acting as one unit, so it takes the singular possessive 'its,' not the plural 'their.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The award was shared equally between the lead engineer and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","I","myself","us"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of the preposition 'between,' so the objective case 'me' is correct, not the subjective 'I.' 'Myself' is the closest trap because reflexive pronouns are often used this way in casual speech, but 'myself' needs an earlier 'I' in the same clause to reflect back to, which is missing here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The director asked the lead actor and______to rehearse the final scene privately.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","I","myself","us"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of 'asked,' so the objective case 'me' is correct, not the subjective 'I.' 'Myself' is the closest trap for the same reason: reflexive pronouns are common in casual speech here, but 'myself' needs an earlier 'I' in the clause to reflect back to.$q$),
  ($q$conv-267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The interns completed the audit by______, without any supervision.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["themselves","theirselves","their self","them"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The plural reflexive pronoun 'themselves' correctly refers back to 'the interns.' 'Them' is the closest trap because it is a real plural pronoun, but the sentence needs the reflexive form to show the interns acted on their own, not a plain object pronoun.$q$),
  ($q$conv-268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the ambulance arrived, the paramedic______the bleeding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already stopped","already stopped","have already stopped","were already stopping"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the bleeding was stopped before another past event (the ambulance's arrival), the past perfect 'had already stopped' is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the lease ends, the tenants______in the apartment for exactly five years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will have been living","will live","lived","are living"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'By the time' with a future event signals the future perfect progressive tense, 'will have been living,' to show an action continuing up to that future point; 'will live' is tempting because it is also future tense, but it lacks the progressive aspect needed to show the five-year duration.$q$),
  ($q$conv-270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Technicians______the pipeline for leaks since the inspection began, and they have found none so far.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have examined","examined","examine","will examine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The action began in the past and continues into the present ('since...have found'), so the present perfect 'have examined' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The physician recommended that the patient______more water each day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["drink","drinks","will drink","drank"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Recommended that' triggers the subjunctive mood, which uses the base form of the verb ('drink') regardless of the subject's number.$q$),
  ($q$conv-272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If the forecast______accurate, the festival organizers would have moved the event indoors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had been","was","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The main clause 'would have moved' signals a past unreal conditional, which requires the past perfect 'had been' in the if-clause. 'Were' is the closest trap because it is the correct subjunctive form for a present contrary-to-fact condition, but this sentence is about a past, completed event, which needs the past perfect instead.$q$),
  ($q$conv-273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Last winter, the crew cleared the trail and______three fallen trees along the way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["removed","removes","will remove","removing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('cleared'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'removed,' to remain consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Squinting against the glare off the snow, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the skier adjusted her goggles before the next run.","her goggles fogged up within minutes of the start.","the next run promised even brighter glare off the snow.","sunlight reflected sharply off the fresh snow."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Squinting against the glare off the snow' describes a person, so the noun immediately after the comma must be that person, 'the skier'; the goggles, the run, and the sunlight cannot squint, even though each choice forms a complete, plausible sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Eager to beat the closing bell, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the trader placed one last order.","one last order was placed by the trader.","the closing bell prompted one last order.","the stock exchange recorded one last order."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Eager to beat the closing bell' describes a person, not the order or the exchange, so the noun immediately following the comma must be 'the trader.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Freshly painted and still smelling of varnish, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the fence looked brand new to the neighbors.","the neighbors admired the fence.","varnish was smelled by the neighbors.","a brand-new look was given to the fence."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Freshly painted and still smelling of varnish' describes the fence, so the noun immediately following the comma must be 'the fence.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Her daily routine includes feeding the chickens, mending fences, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["chopping firewood","to chop firewood","she chops firewood","firewood is chopped"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel gerunds ('feeding,' 'mending,' and ___), so 'chopping firewood' matches the established form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The reviewers described the novel as ambitious, moving, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["beautifully written","written with beauty","beauty in writing","the writing was beautiful"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list requires parallel adjective phrases ('ambitious, moving, and ___'), and 'beautifully written' matches that adjectival form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The grant supports not only scientists studying climate change but also______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["policymakers drafting new regulations","the drafting of new regulations by policymakers","policymakers who draft new regulations","regulations drafted by policymakers"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not only...but also' pairs two objects of 'supports'; since the first is the noun-plus-participle phrase 'scientists studying climate change,' the second must match that exact form: 'policymakers drafting new regulations.' The other choices are grammatical noun phrases but use a gerund, a full relative clause, or a passive participle instead, so they do not match the original construction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new software is designed both to streamline billing and to______customer complaints.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reduce","reducing","the reduction of","reduction in"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Both...and' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the infinitive 'to streamline,' the second must match with the base verb 'reduce' after 'to.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Between the two job offers, the smaller firm's offer was the______generous.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new engine produces more torque than______engine in its class.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["any other","any","all","every"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the engine is itself a member of its class, it must be compared to 'any other engine' to exclude itself from the comparison. 'Any' is the closest trap because it almost completes the idiom correctly, but without 'other' it illogically includes the engine among the things it is being compared to.$q$),
  ($q$conv-283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the telescope was recalibrated______astronomers detected the faint signal again.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After the telescope was recalibrated' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bridge had been closed for repairs______traffic backed up for miles every morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The chef finally revealed the secret to the dish's flavor______a generous spoonful of smoked paprika.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the secret was, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Volunteers______who arrive before sunrise will help set up the aid station.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Who arrive before sunrise' is a restrictive clause identifying which volunteers, so no comma should separate it from the noun it modifies.$q$),
  ($q$conv-287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The violin, ______ had belonged to her grandmother, was insured for an enormous sum.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential, set off by commas, and refers to a thing, the violin, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The satellite lost contact with mission control______however, engineers restored the signal within an hour.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A conjunctive adverb like 'however' joining two independent clauses requires a semicolon before it and a comma after it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Startled by the sudden thunder______the horses bolted across the pasture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Startled by the sudden thunder' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The archive digitized thousands of brittle documents______researchers could finally search them by keyword.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," and, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction must be joined with a semicolon; linking them with only a comma would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The expedition packed only the essentials______a tent, a water filter, and a satellite phone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items that follows the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Beneath the city's oldest cathedral______archaeologists discovered a network of sealed tunnels.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Beneath the city's oldest cathedral' is an introductory phrase and requires a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The lighthouse keeper______a quiet man who, by his own account, rarely left the island—kept meticulous weather logs for forty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'a quiet man who, by his own account, rarely left the island' already contains internal commas, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can open the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The survey revealed one clear trend______younger voters overwhelmingly preferred mail-in ballots.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ",", ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of the trend, following a complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The museum's rarest exhibit______a 400-year-old astrolabe, draws visitors from around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive 'a 400-year-old astrolabe' is nonessential and already closed with a comma, so it must be opened with a matching comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Interest rates fell sharply last quarter______consumer borrowing rose for the first time in years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related, so a semicolon correctly joins them without a coordinating conjunction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The sprinter crossed the finish line______and immediately collapsed from exhaustion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Crossed the finish line' and 'collapsed from exhaustion' share the same subject and form a compound predicate, so no comma is needed before 'and.' The comma is the closest trap because commas do precede 'and' when it joins two independent clauses, but here 'and' only joins two verbs sharing one subject.$q$),
  ($q$conv-298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The app crashed repeatedly______so the developers rushed out an emergency patch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A comma is required before the coordinating conjunction 'so' when it joins two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The CEO's turnaround strategy came down to one word______focus.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon can introduce a single word for emphasis when it follows a complete independent clause; the comma is the closest trap because it could loosely connect 'one word' and 'focus' in casual writing, but formal Standard English calls for the colon's stronger emphasis after a complete clause like this one.$q$),
  ($q$conv-300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Its wings folded tightly against its body______the falcon plummeted toward its prey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Its wings folded tightly against its body' is an introductory absolute phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The manuscript______that the librarian found in a sealed box turned out to be a lost first draft.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'That the librarian found in a sealed box' is a restrictive clause identifying which manuscript, so it takes no comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The sculptor, ______ had never formally studied art, won the competition's top prize.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","that","whom","which"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas refers to a person and functions as the subject of 'had never studied,' so the subject pronoun 'who' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The control group showed no measurable change______therefore, the researchers concluded the treatment was ineffective.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A conjunctive adverb like 'therefore' joining two independent clauses requires a semicolon before it and a comma after it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The tower's spire—reconstructed using the original fifteenth-century blueprints, which had sat forgotten in a monastery archive______finally returned to the skyline after a decade of restoration.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["—",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase 'reconstructed using the original fifteenth-century blueprints, which had sat forgotten in a monastery archive' already contains its own internal comma, so only a matching dash—not a comma, colon, or semicolon—can close the interruption without creating ambiguity about where the aside ends.$q$),
  ($q$conv-305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$To avoid the morning rush______she started leaving for work an hour earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'To avoid the morning rush' is an introductory infinitive phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Before the storm hit, they stocked up on the basics______water, batteries, and canned food.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items that follows the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The prototype, ______ took engineers nearly three years to perfect, finally entered mass production.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential, set off by commas, and refers to a thing, the prototype, so 'which' is correct; 'that' introduces restrictive clauses only.$q$),
  ($q$conv-308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Glacial melt accelerated throughout the decade______coastal cities began investing heavily in sea walls.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction must be joined by a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The post went viral within hours______the company's marketing team had not even finished drafting a response.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be separated by a semicolon rather than joined with only a comma. The comma is the closest trap because a comma plus a coordinating conjunction like 'so' could join these clauses, but a bare comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Although the treaty was signed within weeks of the ceasefire______many of its provisions were not enforced for years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Although the treaty was signed within weeks of the ceasefire' is an introductory dependent clause and must be followed by a single comma. The semicolon is the closest trap because semicolons do separate clauses elsewhere in similar sentences, but a semicolon requires an independent clause on both sides, and this opening clause is dependent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Critics agreed on the mural's central flaw______the perspective collapsed entirely in the lower-left corner.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the flaw was, following a complete independent clause; the semicolon is the closest trap because it could also join these two independent clauses, but it lacks the colon's specific signal that what follows defines or explains the flaw just mentioned.$q$),
  ($q$conv-312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The research team______preparing its final report for the grant committee.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Team' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'is.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the students______expected to submit a lab notebook at the end of the semester.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Each' is singular, regardless of the plural noun in the prepositional phrase that follows it, so the singular verb 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the manager nor the players______happy with the umpire's final call.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a 'neither/nor' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The collection of rare orchids in the botanical garden's east wing______watered by hand every morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun 'collection'; the prepositional phrases that follow do not change its number, so the singular verb 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the train arrives, she______on the platform for nearly two hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will have been waiting","will wait","waited","is waiting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'By the time' with a future event signals the future perfect progressive tense, 'will have been waiting,' to show an action continuing up to that future point; 'will wait' is tempting because it is also future tense, but it lacks the progressive aspect needed to show the two-hour duration.$q$),
  ($q$conv-317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The criteria for approving the new drug______far stricter than those used a decade ago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Criteria' is the plural form of 'criterion,' so it takes the plural verb 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The hikers left______water bottles at the trailhead by mistake.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","his","its","one's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The antecedent 'hikers' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The startup announced that______new app would launch nationwide next month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","its'","their"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive form 'its' (no apostrophe) is required here, not the contraction 'it's,' which means 'it is.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The neighbors said______planning to repaint the fence over the long weekend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","their","there","there's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'They're' is the contraction for 'they are' and is needed before the verb 'planning'; 'their' and 'there' are homophones that do not fit this grammatical role.$q$),
  ($q$conv-321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The detective finally identified______the witness had seen leaving the building.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","whoever","whomever"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whom' is the object of the verb 'had seen' ('the witness had seen whom'), so the object pronoun is required rather than the subject pronoun 'who.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Migrating thousands of miles each winter, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the arctic tern travels farther than almost any other bird.","the journey of the arctic tern spans nearly the length of the globe.","scientists have long studied the arctic tern's migration patterns.","pole-to-pole flight paths make the arctic tern famous among researchers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Migrating thousands of miles each winter' describes a traveler, so the subject of the main clause must be the one migrating, 'the arctic tern'; the journey, the scientists, and the flight paths do not themselves migrate, even though each choice is a grammatically complete sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The exhibit celebrates artists who not only reshaped modern sculpture but also______entire movements in architecture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["inspired","inspiring","have been inspiring","the inspiration of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not only...but also' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the past-tense verb 'reshaped,' the second must match with 'inspired.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new platform is designed both to simplify checkout and to______fraud in real time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flag","flagging","the flagging of","flags"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Both...and' requires parallel structure; since the first element is the infinitive 'to simplify,' the second must match with the base verb 'flag' after 'to.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the two apartments they toured, the one downtown was the______expensive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more","most","much","many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is reserved for three or more.$q$),
  ($q$conv-326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$This processor runs cooler than______chip in its price range.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["any other","any","all","every"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the processor is itself a member of its price range, it must be compared to 'any other chip' to exclude itself from the comparison. 'Any' is the closest trap because it almost completes the idiom correctly, but without 'other' it illogically includes the processor among the things it is being compared to.$q$),
  ($q$conv-327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The______uniforms were donated to the regimental museum after the ceremony.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["soldiers'","soldier's","soldiers's","soldiers"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the uniforms belong to multiple soldiers, the plural possessive 'soldiers'' (apostrophe after the s) is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$No one could identify______umbrella had been left behind in the lecture hall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","who's","whom's","who is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive 'whose' is needed to show ownership of the umbrella; 'who's' is a contraction of 'who is' and does not fit here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$By the time help arrived, the pipe had already______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["burst","bursted","bursting","burst'd"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Burst' is an irregular verb whose past participle is identical to its base form; 'bursted' is a nonstandard form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Scattered across the hillside______dozens of wildflowers no one had planted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is inverted, but the subject is still the plural noun 'wildflowers,' which follows the verb, so the plural verb 'were' is required. 'Was' is the closest trap because a reader might wrongly treat 'hillside' in the opening phrase as the subject instead of the actual subject that follows the verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$My brother and______decided to repaint the garage over the weekend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["I","me","myself","us"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'My brother and I' functions as the subject of the sentence, so the subject pronoun 'I' is required rather than the object pronoun 'me.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The treaty established new borders and______the region's first independent currency.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["created","creates","creating","will create"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence describes past events, so the verb must stay in the past tense, 'created,' to match 'established.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The firm's new policy focuses on reducing waste and______employee morale.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["improving","to improve","improves","improvement of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb 'focuses on' takes the gerund 'reducing,' so the second item in the series must match with the parallel gerund 'improving.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The last person to leave the laboratory that night was______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["she","her","herself","hers"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun follows the linking verb 'was' and renames the subject, so the subject-case pronoun 'she' is required rather than the object-case 'her.' 'Her' is the closest trap because it is the pronoun most people default to after a verb, but a linking verb takes a subject-case complement, not an object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$She is one of the few researchers who______ever successfully cloned this species of coral.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","has","having","is having"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative clause 'who have ever successfully cloned' modifies 'researchers,' a plural noun, so the plural verb 'have' is required. 'Has' is the closest trap because a reader might wrongly match the verb to the singular 'she' instead of to 'researchers,' the actual antecedent of 'who.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The manager asked Priya and______to present the quarterly report.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","myself","I","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of the verb 'asked,' so the object pronoun 'me' is required; 'myself' cannot substitute for 'me' without a prior reference to the speaker in the same clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A number of employees______requested the option to work remotely on Fridays.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","has","having","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A number of' is followed by a plural verb because the emphasis is on the individual employees, unlike 'the number of,' which takes a singular verb. 'Has' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the phrase were 'the number of employees,' which treats the group as one unit rather than as individuals.$q$),
  ($q$conv-338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The baker and the florist______next door to each other for twenty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have worked","has worked","working","works"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The baker and the florist' is a compound subject joined by 'and,' so it requires the plural verb 'have worked.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Either the twins or their cousin______bringing dessert to the reunion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In an 'either/or' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, the singular 'cousin,' so 'is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The charity renovated the______playground behind the old shelter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["children's","childrens'","childs'","children"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Children' is already plural, so its possessive form adds only an apostrophe and s: 'children's'; 'childrens'' incorrectly treats 'children' as if it ended in s.$q$),
  ($q$conv-341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$She should______the form before the deadline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["submit","submits","to submit","submitting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The modal verb 'should' must be followed by the base form of the verb, 'submit,' not an inflected or infinitive form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If I______taller, I would try out for the team.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","am","be"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This hypothetical condition requires the subjunctive mood, which uses 'were' with 'I' rather than the indicative 'was'; 'was' is especially tempting because it is the ordinary past tense of 'be' with 'I,' but the contrary-to-fact 'if' clause calls for the subjunctive form instead.$q$),
  ($q$conv-343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Every morning she wakes early, brews coffee, and______the newspaper.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reads","reading","to read","read"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb must be parallel with and agree with the subject the same way 'wakes' and 'brews' do, so 'reads' is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$After the long hike, the dog immediately______down by the fireplace.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lay","laid","lied","has lain"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Lay' is the simple past tense of 'lie' (to recline); 'laid' is the past tense of the different verb 'lay' (to place something), and 'lied' means to tell a falsehood.$q$),
  ($q$conv-345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the city's old streetlights were replaced with LED fixtures______residents noticed a sharp drop in the monthly electricity bill.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'After the city's old streetlights were replaced with LED fixtures' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Every evening before the audience arrives, the orchestra______its instruments in the quiet rehearsal hall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["tunes","tune","are tuning","have tuned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Orchestra' is a collective noun acting as a single unit, so it takes the singular verb 'tunes'; 'tune' and 'are tuning' are plural or progressive forms that don't agree, and 'have tuned' shifts to a completed action rather than the habitual present described.$q$),
  ($q$conv-347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The trail was washed out after the storm______hikers had to take a longer detour around the ridge.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," and, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone would create a comma splice, and ' and,' is not standard punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The surprise party was planned almost entirely by my sister and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","I","myself","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$As the object of the preposition 'by,' the pronoun must be in the objective case, 'me'; 'I' is subjective, 'myself' has no earlier reference to reflect back to, and 'mine' is possessive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The chef insisted on three qualities in a good broth______patience, balance, and depth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list that explains the 'three qualities' named in the complete independent clause before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Each student in the seminar must submit______essay to the committee by Friday.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["his or her","their","it's","they're"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Each' is singular, so the pronoun referring back to it must also be singular: 'his or her'; 'their' is plural, and 'it's' and 'they're' are contractions that don't function as possessives here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The rain finally stopped______and the children ran outside to play.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; "," ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction 'and' require a comma before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the treaty was finally signed, the two nations______fighting for nearly a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had been","have been","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The fighting was completed before another past event (the signing), so the past perfect 'had been' is required; the other choices are present or simple past forms that don't show that earlier completion.$q$),
  ($q$conv-353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The smartphone app______the startup released last spring quickly gained a million downloads.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","which","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause 'the startup released last spring' is essential to identifying which app is meant and has no surrounding commas, so the restrictive pronoun 'that' is correct; 'which' requires commas for nonessential clauses, and 'who'/'whom' refer to people.$q$),
  ($q$conv-354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The old oak tree finally dropped______last leaves in the November wind.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","its'","their"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive pronoun 'its' takes no apostrophe; 'it's' is a contraction of 'it is,' 'its'' is not a standard word, and 'their' does not agree with the singular 'tree.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Sales dropped sharply in the first quarter______however, a new marketing strategy reversed the trend by summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conjunctive adverb 'however' joins two independent clauses and must be preceded by a semicolon, with a comma following it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The coach expected the team not only to practice diligently but also______their mistakes honestly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to acknowledge","acknowledging","acknowledges","acknowledged"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correlative pair 'not only...but also' requires parallel grammatical forms; since the first part uses the infinitive 'to practice,' the second must match with 'to acknowledge.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Our itinerary includes visits to museums______gardens, and historic cathedrals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; "," ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Items in a series of three or more must be separated by commas.$q$),
  ($q$conv-358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$I wish the weather report______more reliable before we planned the picnic around it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","be"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After 'wish,' a contrary-to-fact statement requires the subjunctive mood, which uses 'were' regardless of the subject's number; 'was' is especially tempting because it is the ordinary past tense that would fit outside a wish statement, but 'wish' specifically calls for the subjunctive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The museum's newest exhibit, a sprawling collection of ancient coins______fascinated visitors from around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A sprawling collection of ancient coins' is a nonessential appositive renaming the exhibit, so it must be closed with a second comma matching the one that opened it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Having replaced the worn brake pads just that morning, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the mechanic test-drove the car around the block.","the car was test-driven around the block.","the block was where the car was test-driven.","it was around the block that the car was test-driven."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase 'Having replaced the worn brake pads' describes a person performing that action, so the subject immediately following must be 'the mechanic'; the other choices place the car or an unrelated word right after the modifier, creating a dangling modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Even though the ballet company had rehearsed the finale dozens of times______the lead dancer still missed her cue on opening night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Even though the ballet company had rehearsed the finale dozens of times' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so a comma is needed to join it to the independent clause that follows.$q$),
  ($q$conv-362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$After the ceremony, the graduates gathered in the______lounge to celebrate with their families.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["graduates'","graduate's","graduates's","graduates"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lounge belongs to multiple graduates, so the plural noun 'graduates,' which already ends in -s, takes only an apostrophe: 'graduates''; 'graduate's' wrongly treats the noun as singular, and 'graduates's' adds a nonstandard extra s.$q$),
  ($q$conv-363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The comet, ______ astronomers had tracked for nearly a year, finally became visible to the naked eye.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","that","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause is nonessential, set off by commas, and refers to a thing (the comet), so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses, and 'who'/'whom' refer to people.$q$),
  ($q$conv-364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Her daily routine includes watering the vegetable beds, pulling weeds, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pruning the roses","to prune the roses","she prunes the roses","the roses are pruned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The list of activities must be grammatically parallel; since the earlier items use gerunds ('watering,' 'pulling'), the final item must match with the gerund 'pruning.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Despite the heavy rainfall earlier in the week______the river delta remained surprisingly dry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Despite the heavy rainfall earlier in the week' is an introductory phrase and must be separated from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The recruiter couldn't recall______she had interviewed for the position earlier that morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","whomever","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whom' is the object of the verb 'had interviewed' ('she had interviewed whom'), so the objective case is required; 'who' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the pronoun were the subject of 'had interviewed,' which it is not.$q$),
  ($q$conv-367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The pianist improvised for nearly ten minutes______the crowd never once stopped swaying to the rhythm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," but, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon correctly joins them; a comma alone would create a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If you have any questions about the proposal, please contact Maria or______directly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["me","myself","I","mine"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$As the object of 'contact,' the objective pronoun 'me' is required; 'myself' is a reflexive pronoun with no earlier reference to reflect, 'I' is subjective, and 'mine' is possessive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The senator closed her speech with a single line the crowd would remember for years______'Progress is never owed; it is earned.'$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the quotation that follows a complete independent clause; the comma is the closest trap because commas often introduce quotations in dialogue, but Standard English convention calls for a colon when the quotation serves as a formal, emphatic conclusion to an independent clause like this one.$q$),
  ($q$conv-370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$This pair of running shoes is______than the one I bought last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lighter","more light","more lighter","lightest"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a one-syllable adjective like 'light,' the comparative is formed by adding '-er': 'lighter'; 'more light' and 'more lighter' are redundant or nonstandard, and 'lightest' is a superlative used for comparing three or more things.$q$),
  ($q$conv-371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The observatory's roof retracted on schedule for the evening viewing______clouds rolled in and obscured the entire night sky within minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone creates a splice, and ' so,' is not standard punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Behind the old courthouse today, there______a narrow alley lined with gas lamps.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In this inverted sentence, the verb must agree with the delayed subject 'alley,' which is singular, so 'is' is correct; 'are' is plural, and 'were'/'have been' shift to past tense, which doesn't match the present-tense context set by 'today.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Thank you______Professor Alvarez, for taking the time to meet with me yesterday afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Professor Alvarez' is a direct address and must be set off from the rest of the sentence with a comma on each side; the comma after it is already given, so the blank needs a matching comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Dripping with rainwater and shivering from the cold, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["the hiker finally spotted a weathered cabin through the trees.","a weathered cabin stood just visible through the trees.","the trees finally thinned enough to reveal a cabin.","it was a weathered cabin that the hiker finally spotted through the trees."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase describes a person who is dripping and shivering, so the subject immediately following the comma must be 'the hiker'; a cabin or a stand of trees cannot shiver, and restructuring the sentence with 'it was...that' still places the illogical subject 'it' right after the modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The solution turned a vivid blue______when the technician added the copper sulfate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When a subordinate clause like 'when the technician added the copper sulfate' follows the main clause, no comma is needed before the subordinating conjunction; adding one, or using a semicolon or colon, incorrectly breaks the sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The flight was delayed for nearly three hours______because the storm had grounded every departure since dawn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subordinate clause introduced by 'because' at the end of the sentence is directly connected to the main clause, so no punctuation is needed before it; a comma, semicolon, or colon would incorrectly separate them.$q$),
  ($q$conv-377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Every morning, Elena stretches for ten minutes and then______three miles before breakfast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["runs","ran","running","to run"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb must stay in the present tense to match 'stretches,' describing Elena's habitual routine; 'ran' shifts to past tense, and 'running'/'to run' are not finite verb forms that can pair with 'and then.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The artifact______according to the lead archaeologist, dates back nearly three thousand years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'According to the lead archaeologist' is a parenthetical interrupter and must be set off by commas on both sides; the comma after it is already given, so the blank requires a matching comma. The semicolon is the closest trap because semicolons can separate related independent clauses, but this interrupting phrase is not an independent clause and needs commas, not a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Everyone who attended the reunion______surprised by how much the old schoolhouse had changed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","have been","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Everyone' is a singular indefinite pronoun and takes a singular verb, 'was'; 'were,' 'have been,' and 'are' are plural or present-tense forms that don't agree.$q$),
  ($q$conv-380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The goalkeeper dove for the ball______but it slipped just past her fingertips into the net.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; "," ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction 'but' require a comma before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$She is one of the finalists who______invited to present their research at the national conference.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative pronoun 'who' refers to 'finalists' (plural), the antecedent in 'one of the finalists who,' so the verb must be plural: 'were.' 'Was' is the closest trap because a reader might wrongly match the verb to the singular 'she' instead of to 'finalists,' the actual antecedent of 'who.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The region's unemployment rate climbed for three straight months______nevertheless, job fairs across the city reported record attendance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conjunctive adverb 'nevertheless' links two independent clauses and must be preceded by a semicolon, with a comma following it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$My two younger brothers______the vegetable garden every Saturday morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["water","waters","is watering","has watered"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The plural subject 'brothers' requires the plural verb 'water'; 'waters,' 'is watering,' and 'has watered' are singular or otherwise mismatched in form.$q$),
  ($q$conv-384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The negotiators finally reached an agreement after midnight______exhausted but relieved after sixteen straight hours of talks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trailing phrase 'exhausted but relieved after sixteen straight hours of talks' is a nonessential description of the negotiators and must be set off with a comma. The semicolon is the closest trap because semicolons can follow an independent clause, but this trailing phrase is not itself an independent clause and needs a comma instead.$q$),
  ($q$conv-385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If the electrician had checked the circuit breaker sooner, the outage______prevented entirely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["could have been","could be","could have","can be"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This past hypothetical situation requires the modal perfect 'could have been' to show an unrealized possibility in the past; 'could be' and 'can be' are present forms, and 'could have' is missing the necessary past participle 'been.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Waking before dawn to catch the first light______the photographer hiked nearly a mile to the overlook.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory participial phrase must be separated from the main clause with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Training for a marathon demands far more discipline than______a single race.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["running","to run","the running of","runners who run"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The comparison must use parallel grammatical forms; since 'training' is a gerund functioning as the subject's counterpart, the second half of the comparison must also be the bare gerund 'running,' not the infinitive 'to run,' the noun phrase 'the running of,' or 'runners who run,' which illogically compares discipline to people rather than to an activity.$q$),
  ($q$conv-388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Maria Lopez______a self-taught engineer from a small coastal town, patented her first irrigation device at nineteen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A self-taught engineer from a small coastal town' is a nonessential appositive describing Maria Lopez and must be set off with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The detective finally identified______fingerprints had been left on the broken window latch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","who's","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whose' is the possessive form needed to show that the fingerprints belong to someone; 'who's' is a contraction of 'who is,' and 'who'/'whom' are not possessive.$q$),
  ($q$conv-390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$While the neighborhood association debated the proposed noise ordinance for nearly three hours______several residents outside grew increasingly impatient.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",". "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'While the neighborhood association debated the proposed noise ordinance for nearly three hours' is a dependent clause; ending it with a period would create a sentence fragment, so a comma is needed to connect it to the independent clause that follows.$q$),
  ($q$conv-391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$By the ninth inning, the rain______falling so hard that the umpires finally called the game.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had begun","had began","have begun","begins"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The past participle of 'begin' is 'begun,' so the correct past perfect form is 'had begun'; 'had began' incorrectly uses the simple past form as a participle, and 'have begun'/'begins' don't match the past-tense context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The wildfire had been contained for nearly a week______a sudden wind shift caused it to jump the containment line overnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon is required; a comma alone would create a splice, and ' so,' is nonstandard punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The chef's selection of seasonal vegetable dishes______praised by nearly every food critic in the city.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","have been","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the sentence is 'selection,' not the intervening plural noun 'dishes,' so the singular verb 'has been' is required; 'have been,' 'were,' and 'are' incorrectly agree with the nearby plural noun instead of the true subject.$q$),
  ($q$conv-394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Before starting the repair, she laid out everything she would need______a wrench, pliers, and electrical tape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list that follows the complete independent clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If gravity on the moon's surface______as strong as Earth's, astronauts could never have jumped so high.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","is","had been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the moon's weaker gravity is a constant, ongoing fact, the hypothetical requires the present subjunctive 'were,' not the past-perfect 'had been,' which would incorrectly suggest gravity itself once changed; 'was' and 'is' are not the correct subjunctive form for this contrary-to-fact statement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Bats navigate dense forests with remarkable precision using echolocation______many other nocturnal mammals rely primarily on scent instead.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," but, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and contrast with each other with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon correctly joins them; a comma alone would create a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the three finalists, Priya submitted the______essay, according to every judge on the panel.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["strongest","stronger","more strong","most strong"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When comparing three or more things, the superlative form is required: 'strongest'; 'stronger' compares only two things, and 'more strong'/'most strong' are nonstandard versions of the comparative and superlative.$q$),
  ($q$conv-398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The biologist told the reporters______'We still don't fully understand how the species survives the winter.'$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When a quotation follows a reporting verb like 'told,' a comma introduces it; a colon is reserved for introducing a quotation after a complete independent clause that doesn't already contain a verb of speaking directly connected to the quote.$q$),
  ($q$conv-399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$This year, she resolved to exercise more, to eat healthier, and______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to read more books","reading more books","more books to read","books were read more"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The series must stay parallel with the earlier infinitives 'to exercise' and 'to eat,' so the final item must also be an infinitive: 'to read more books.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The painting's colors seemed dull under the gallery's fluorescent lights______they came alive in the afternoon sun streaming through the skylight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," but, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related with no coordinating conjunction between them, so a semicolon correctly joins them; a comma alone would create a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A number of volunteers from the community college______up early to help rebuild the playground.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["showed","shows","has shown","is showing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'A number of' functions as a plural quantifier meaning 'several' and takes a plural verb, 'showed.' 'Has shown' is the closest trap because it would be correct if the phrase were 'the number of volunteers,' which treats the group as a single unit rather than as individuals.$q$),
  ($q$conv-402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The band played three encores______yet the crowd still begged for one more song.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; "," ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction 'yet' require a comma before it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Her physical therapist recommended both strengthening her core and______her shoulders to prevent further injury.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stretching","to stretch","she stretches","the stretch of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correlative pair 'both...and' requires parallel forms; since the first part uses the gerund 'strengthening,' the second must match with 'stretching.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The auction catalog listed a Victorian writing desk, valued at several thousand dollars______a pair of hand-carved chairs, each over a century old; and a porcelain tea set from the same estate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," and ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because each item in this list already contains an internal comma, semicolons are needed to separate the three main items clearly. The comma is the closest trap because commas normally separate list items, but using only commas here would blur where one item ends and the next begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The biographer, ______ spent six years researching the reclusive author's life, finally published the long-awaited book.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","whom","which","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Within the nonessential clause, the pronoun functions as the subject of the verb 'spent,' so the subjective 'who' is required; 'whom' is the closest trap because it is the correct choice when the pronoun is the object of a verb or preposition within its clause, which is not the case here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The curtain rose precisely on time______the understudy, not the usual lead, stepped into the spotlight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", "," so, ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both clauses are independent with no coordinating conjunction linking them, so a semicolon is required. The comma is the closest trap because a comma plus 'so' could join these clauses correctly, but a bare comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the rain stopped______ the children ran outside to play in the puddles.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory dependent clause ('After the rain stopped') must be followed by a comma before the main clause begins.$q$),
  ($q$conv-408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum opens at nine in the morning______ it closes at five in the afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction need a semicolon. A comma alone would create a comma splice, and no punctuation would create a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The recipe calls for four ingredients______ flour, eggs, butter, and sugar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon correctly follows the complete independent clause to introduce the list that specifies the four ingredients.$q$),
  ($q$conv-410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Maria had hoped to attend the concert______ but her flight was delayed by a snowstorm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A comma is needed before the coordinating conjunction 'but' when it joins two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$My neighbor, a retired pilot______ grows prize-winning tomatoes in her backyard.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["","; ",", ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The appositive 'a retired pilot' is nonessential, so it must be closed with a comma to match the comma that opened it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Dr. Okafor's lecture focused on a single question______ how do city lights affect migrating birds?$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon after the independent clause introduces the specific question the lecture addressed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The storm knocked out power across the county______ however, crews restored service within a few hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ","",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A semicolon should precede a conjunctive adverb like 'however' when it connects two independent clauses; a comma there would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The hikers carried everything they would need for the overnight trip______ tents, sleeping bags, stoves, and enough food for two days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ","",": "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause is complete, and what follows lists the items, so a colon is the proper punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The author's first novel, which was published in 1998______ became a surprise bestseller.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", ",""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The nonrestrictive clause 'which was published in 1998' began after a comma and must end with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Some historians argue that the library at Alexandria was destroyed in a single fire______ others contend that it declined slowly over several centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two complete, closely related ideas are joined by a semicolon. A comma alone would be a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Native to the high deserts of Chile______ the plant stores water in its thick, waxy leaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory participial phrase modifies 'the plant' and should be followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The new policy has one clear goal______ to reduce the time residents spend waiting for permits.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ","",": "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A colon introduces the explanation of the single goal that the first clause announces.$q$),
  ($q$conv-419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences______ physics and chemistry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ","",": ",", but"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The words after the blank specify which two sciences, so a colon after the complete clause is appropriate.$q$),
  ($q$conv-420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The conductor raised her baton______ the orchestra fell silent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": ","; ",""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no conjunction, so a semicolon is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bakery on Fifth Street sells three kinds of bread______ sourdough, rye, and a seeded multigrain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon follows a complete clause and introduces the list that names the three breads.$q$),
  ($q$conv-422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Although the experiment had failed twice______ the team decided to try one more time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ","",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subordinate clause beginning with 'Although' must be separated from the main clause by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Ms. Alvarez, who coaches the debate team______ was named teacher of the year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": ",""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonessential relative clause is set off by a pair of commas; a closing comma is needed before the verb 'was'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The river flooded the lower fields______ the farmers lost nearly their entire spring planting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ","",": while",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined without a conjunction require a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The city's tallest building was completed in 1931______ at the time, it was the tallest in the world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": ",""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'At the time, it was the tallest in the world' is an independent clause, so a semicolon (not a comma) must separate the two clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Researchers who study sleep have identified a surprising factor in teenagers' fatigue______ school start times.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",", ",": ",""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The independent clause promises a specific factor, and the fragment 'school start times' names it, so a colon is appropriate.$q$),
  ($q$conv-427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The film, praised by critics for its restrained performances______ received no major awards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase 'praised by critics for its restrained performances' is a nonessential modifier opened by a comma and must be closed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Whether the bridge can be saved______ depends on the results of next month's engineering survey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The noun clause 'Whether the bridge can be saved' is the subject of 'depends', and a subject must not be separated from its verb by punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee's final report recommended three changes______ extending library hours, adding bus routes, and funding after-school programs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ","—and",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon follows the independent clause and introduces the three recommended changes.$q$),
  ($q$conv-430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The telescope's mirror, polished to within a fraction of the wavelength of light______ allowed astronomers to image distant galaxies in unprecedented detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["","; ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The participial phrase interrupts the sentence between the subject and its verb, so it must be closed with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Not every proposal was approved______ the board rejected the two most expensive ones outright.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[":",", ",", and","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second independent clause elaborates on the first; a semicolon joins them correctly, whereas a comma alone would be a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Beethoven's late quartets were considered bafflingly strange by many of his contemporaries______ today they are regarded as among the greatest works in the repertoire.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ",", ","","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two contrasting independent clauses need a semicolon (or a comma plus a conjunction).$q$),
  ($q$conv-433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The ship's captain, hoping to avoid the worst of the weather______ altered course twice during the night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The participial phrase is nonessential and sits between the subject and verb, so it is closed with a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$My brother plays the cello______ my sister plays the flute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction require a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Please bring the following to the exam______ two pencils, a calculator, and a photo ID.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ","",", ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A colon follows the complete clause and introduces the list of items.$q$),
  ($q$conv-436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The study's results were inconclusive______ the sample was too small to reveal any meaningful pattern.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","",": ","; but"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second clause explains why the results were inconclusive, so a colon (or semicolon) fits; a comma would create a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Anna loves hiking in the mountains______ and her brother prefers kayaking on the lake.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A comma is needed before the coordinating conjunction 'and' joining two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The mayor's announcement caught many residents off guard______ it contradicted her earlier promise to freeze property taxes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": which","","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second independent clause is closely related to the first and is joined with a semicolon; a comma alone is a splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Because the soil in the valley is unusually rich in volcanic minerals______ vineyards there produce grapes with a distinctive flavor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ","",", ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The dependent clause introduced by 'Because' must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The collection of rare stamps______ displayed in a glass case near the entrance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","is","are"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject 'collection' is singular, so the singular verb 'is' is required.$q$),
  ($q$conv-441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the students______ responsible for submitting a final project.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","were","have been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Each' is singular and takes a singular verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The twins asked ______ parents to drive them to the game.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","there","they're","its"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive pronoun 'their' agrees with the plural 'twins'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Yesterday, the team ______ its final practice before the tournament.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["held","holds","will hold","has holding"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Yesterday' calls for the simple past tense, 'held'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Neither the director nor the actors______ satisfied with the first rehearsal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","is","was","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$With 'neither…nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'actors', which is plural.$q$),
  ($q$conv-445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The books on the top shelf, which ______ mostly poetry collections, belonged to her grandmother.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative pronoun 'which' refers to the plural 'books', so the plural verb 'were' is required, and the past tense matches 'belonged'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Walking along the shore at dawn, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the dolphins' appearance was noticed just beyond the breakers.","the hikers noticed a pod of dolphins just beyond the breakers.","a pod of dolphins appeared just beyond the breakers for the hikers.","dolphins were noticed by the hikers just beyond the breakers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory participial phrase 'Walking along the shore at dawn' must modify the noun that follows, and only the hikers could be walking.$q$),
  ($q$conv-447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The scientist, along with her two assistants, ______ the data before submitting the paper.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["review","were reviewing","have reviewed","reviewed"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular 'scientist'; 'along with her two assistants' is a parenthetical, so the singular-compatible past tense 'reviewed' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The reporter asked the candidate ______ she believed would best serve the district.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","which","whose","who"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun is the subject of 'would best serve'; 'she believed' is parenthetical. A subject takes 'who', not 'whom'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Because Priya had practiced every day, she ______ the piece flawlessly at the recital.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is performing","will perform","performed","performs"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The surrounding past-perfect context ('had practiced') and 'at the recital' establish a past event, so the past tense 'performed' fits.$q$),
  ($q$conv-450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The new library offers more programs than ______ in the neighboring town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["those","that of","it","the one"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The comparison is between the library's programs and the neighboring town's library, so the singular referent 'the one' (the library) is appropriate.$q$),
  ($q$conv-451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Having finished the exam early, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Jonas's answers were double-checked in the extra time.","an extra review of the answers was made by Jonas.","the extra time was used by Jonas to double-check his answers.","Jonas used the extra time to double-check his answers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The participial phrase 'Having finished the exam early' must modify Jonas, the person who finished.$q$),
  ($q$conv-452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The committee ______ divided in their opinions about the proposal, with half favoring it and half opposed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","are","was"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Even when members disagree, 'committee' as a unit takes a singular verb; 'was' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Increasing the sample size reduces random error, and ______ the results more reliable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["makes","to make","make","making"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The verb 'makes' is parallel to 'reduces' and agrees with the gerund subject 'increasing'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The designer's aim was to create a space that felt open, inviting, and ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that people of all ages could feel welcome.","it welcomed people of all ages.","welcoming to people of all ages.","to welcome people of all ages."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The series 'open, inviting, and ___' requires parallel adjectives, so 'welcoming to people of all ages' keeps the list parallel.$q$),
  ($q$conv-455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Of the two proposals, the second is ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the most cost-effectively.","the more cost-effective.","the most cost-effective.","more cost-effective than it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$When comparing two things, the comparative ('more') is used, not the superlative.$q$),
  ($q$conv-456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The volcano's eruption in 1980 ______ the surrounding forest and altered the landscape for decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["devastating","devastated","devastates","had devastating"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A past-tense verb is needed to match 'eruption in 1980' and the parallel verb 'altered'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Their neighbor ______ new dog barks every morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["'s"," is","s'","s"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive 'neighbor's' shows ownership by the singular noun 'neighbor'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Not only the manager but also the interns ______ attending the workshop.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With 'not only…but also', the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'interns', so the plural 'are' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The rate at which glaciers retreat, like the rates of many other natural processes, ______ over time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["varies","are varying","vary","have varied"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular 'rate'; the intervening phrases do not change its number.$q$),
  ($q$conv-460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Last summer, we ______ to the coast, where we spent a week hiking and swimming.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will travel","have traveled","travel","traveled"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Last summer' establishes a completed past action, so the simple past 'traveled' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The report, which examined three decades of rainfall data, ______ that droughts have become more frequent in the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["concluding","to conclude","concludes","conclude"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The singular subject 'report' takes the singular present verb 'concludes'; the 'which' clause is a nonessential modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Several of the paintings ______ been restored.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","is","was","have"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The plural subject 'Several' requires the plural auxiliary 'have'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$If the proposal ______ approved next week, construction could begin by June.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","was","will be","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A hypothetical or unlikely condition uses the subjunctive 'were'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The mountain range stretches for miles, ______ peaks are covered with snow even in midsummer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","whose","its","who's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'Whose' is the possessive relative pronoun for 'range'; 'who's' means 'who is'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-465$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Maya practices piano more diligently than ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["her brother's.","her brother.","her brother does.","her brother is."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The comparison requires a complete parallel clause ('than her brother does'), which keeps the elements parallel and clear.$q$),
  ($q$conv-466$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$One of the most important factors in a plant's survival ______ the availability of water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","are","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is 'One' (singular), not 'factors'.$q$),
  ($q$conv-467$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Honeybees communicate through dance______and scouts signal the direction and distance of flowers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": "," ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("and") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-468$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The toolkit contains three essentials______a wrench, a level, and a tape measure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-469$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The committee announced that ______ would delay the vote until next month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it","its","them","they"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Committee" is a singular collective noun, so the singular subject pronoun "it" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-470$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The botanist collected three kinds of moss______sphagnum, cord, and haircap.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ",", "," ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-471$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A cluster of tiny islands off the northern coast ______ a protected marine reserve.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are forming","form","forms","have formed"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the singular "cluster," so the singular verb "forms" agrees with it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-472$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Few people visit the island in winter______the ferry runs only twice a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", "," ","; ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both "Few people visit the island in winter" and "the ferry runs only twice a week" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-473$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The ideas presented in the senator's three speeches ______ widely debated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "ideas" is plural, so the plural verb "were" is required; "speeches" is inside a prepositional phrase and does not control the verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-474$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Of the two routes, the coastal road is the ______ scenic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most","much","very","more"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative "more" is used instead of the superlative "most."$q$),
  ($q$conv-475$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Next year the city ______ a new transit line that will connect the airport to downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has opened","had opened","opened","will open"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Next year" indicates a future event, so "will open" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-476$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Visitors to the park may do one of two things______hike the ridge trail or paddle across the lake.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": "," ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-477$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The museum extended ______ hours during the festival.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its'","its","it's","their's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Its" is the possessive pronoun and has no apostrophe; "it's" means "it is."$q$),
  ($q$conv-478$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The museum's ancient scrolls are extremely fragile______curators display only reproductions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ","; and "," "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Both "The museum's ancient scrolls are extremely fragile" and "curators display only reproductions" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-479$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Rainfall in the valley has doubled since 1990______and farmers have switched to new crops.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("and") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-480$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Rainfall in the valley has doubled since 1990______farmers have switched to new crops.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ","; and ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both "Rainfall in the valley has doubled since 1990" and "farmers have switched to new crops" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-481$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$When the storm hit the coast last September, residents ______ to higher ground before the flooding began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["already move","had already moved","have already moved","will already move"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The past perfect "had already moved" shows an action completed before another past event (the flooding began).$q$),
  ($q$conv-482$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Maya practiced the violin every morning before school______so her audition piece was nearly flawless.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("so") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-483$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The inventor patented her design in 1931______but it was not manufactured until a decade later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; "," ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("but") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-484$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bridge had been closed for repairs all week______commuters were forced to find alternate routes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; and ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both "The bridge had been closed for repairs all week" and "commuters were forced to find alternate routes" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-485$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The results of the clinical trial ______ that the treatment is effective.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has suggested","suggest","is suggesting","suggests"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Results" is plural, so it takes the plural verb "suggest."$q$),
  ($q$conv-486$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Octopuses have three hearts______two of them pump blood to the gills.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; "," ",", ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both "Octopuses have three hearts" and "two of them pump blood to the gills" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-487$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The town's oldest oak, which has stood for nearly 300 years______was struck by lightning last week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase between the subject and the verb is nonessential, so it must be set off by a pair of commas. The opening comma is already there; a closing comma is needed after the phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-488$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The inventor patented her design in 1931______it was not manufactured until a decade later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", "," ","; ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both "The inventor patented her design in 1931" and "it was not manufactured until a decade later" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-489$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The stack of unpaid invoices on the manager's desk ______ taller every week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["grows","are growing","have grown","grow"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular "stack," not the plural "invoices" in the prepositional phrase, so the singular verb "grows" is needed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-490$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Tidal turbines produce power predictably______but wind farms depend on the weather.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("but") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-491$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The quality of the reproductions in the museum's catalog ______ remarkably high.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","are","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular "quality," so "is" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-492$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Dr. Lee enjoys hiking, painting, and ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to play the cello","she plays the cello","the cello","playing the cello"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The series "hiking, painting, and ..." requires a parallel gerund phrase: "playing the cello."$q$),
  ($q$conv-493$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The novel______ final chapter surprised critics.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["s'","'s","s","es"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The possessive of the singular noun "novel" is formed with an apostrophe and s: "novel's."$q$),
  ($q$conv-494$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The coach selected the player ______ she believed had the strongest work ethic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","whom","whose","which"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Who" is the subject of "had the strongest work ethic," so the subjective form "who" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-495$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe requires just a few ingredients______flour, butter, sugar, and a pinch of salt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": "," ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-496$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Neither the captain nor the sailors ______ prepared for the sudden storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","has been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With "neither...nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "sailors," which is plural: "were."$q$),
  ($q$conv-497$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$My neighbor, an avid gardener______shares tomatoes with everyone on the street.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase between the subject and the verb is nonessential, so it must be set off by a pair of commas. The opening comma is already there; a closing comma is needed after the phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-498$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Before the expedition left the harbor______the crew checked every piece of equipment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ","; ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Before the expedition left the harbor" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-499$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$As the tide receded______hundreds of crabs appeared on the sand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; ",": "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"As the tide receded" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-500$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$All of the ______ lockers were repainted over the summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["students","students'","students's","student's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lockers belong to multiple students, so the plural possessive "students'" is needed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-501$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The two sisters______ bedroom was painted bright green.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["'s","'"," ","s'"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Plural nouns ending in s form the possessive by adding only an apostrophe: "sisters'."$q$),
  ($q$conv-502$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The new train is faster ______ the old one.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["than","then","as","that"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Comparisons use "than."$q$),
  ($q$conv-503$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The scientist to ______ the award was given has worked on the project for decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","which","whom","who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun is the object of the preposition "to," so the objective form "whom" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-504$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The old lighthouse no longer guides ships______it remains a popular destination for tourists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; and ",", ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both "The old lighthouse no longer guides ships" and "it remains a popular destination for tourists" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-505$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The author finished the manuscript in 2015, but it ______ until 2019.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was not published","will not be published","is not published","has not been published"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The time reference "in 2015" and "until 2019" places both events in the past, so the simple past passive "was not published" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-506$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The study found that students who slept longer scored higher not only on memory tests but also ______ attention tasks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["through","with","for","on"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correlative "not only on... but also on" requires parallel prepositions.$q$),
  ($q$conv-507$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Among the museum's most valuable treasures ______ a rare first edition of the dictionary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","are","is","were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In this inverted sentence the subject is the singular "edition," so the singular verb "is" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-508$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Since the observatory opened in 1964, astronomers there ______ more than two hundred comets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["discovered","had discovered","discover","have discovered"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Since 1964" calls for the present perfect, "have discovered," for an action continuing up to the present.$q$),
  ($q$conv-509$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The cellist, who joined the orchestra in 2018______has already performed as a soloist.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; "," ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase between the subject and the verb is nonessential, so it must be set off by a pair of commas. The opening comma is already there; a closing comma is needed after the phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-510$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$______ likely that the bridge will reopen in March.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Its'","Its","Is","It's"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence needs the contraction of "it is": "It's."$q$),
  ($q$conv-511$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Unless the council approves additional funding______the program will end in June.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Unless the council approves additional funding" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-512$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee considered two options______raising ticket prices or reducing the number of shows.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ","; "," ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-513$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The festival begins on Friday evening______volunteers must arrive by noon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; and ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both "The festival begins on Friday evening" and "volunteers must arrive by noon" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-514$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The price of fresh vegetables, along with fuel and housing costs, ______ risen sharply this year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","were","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Along with fuel and housing costs" is parenthetical; the subject is the singular "price," so "has" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-515$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The researchers who conducted the survey ______ to publish their findings next month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is planning","has planned","plan","plans"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject "researchers" is plural, so the plural verb "plan" is correct; "who conducted the survey" is a modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-516$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The company changed ______ return policy after customers complained.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","their","it's","its"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Company" is singular, so the singular possessive "its" is needed; "it's" means "it is."$q$),
  ($q$conv-517$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The author spent a decade researching the novel______its historical detail is remarkable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; and ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both "The author spent a decade researching the novel" and "its historical detail is remarkable" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-518$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The architect, who designed the stadium______will lead the tour this afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase between the subject and the verb is nonessential, so it must be set off by a pair of commas. The opening comma is already there; a closing comma is needed after the phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-519$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the volunteers sorted the donations______they loaded the trucks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", "," ",": ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"After the volunteers sorted the donations" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-520$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Octopuses have three hearts______and two of them pump blood to the gills.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A coordinating conjunction ("and") joining two independent clauses must be preceded by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-521$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By the time the rescue team arrived, the hikers ______ for nearly two days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were waiting","wait","had been waiting","have been waiting"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The past perfect progressive "had been waiting" shows an ongoing action that began before the rescue team arrived.$q$),
  ($q$conv-522$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the volunteers ______ assigned a different task.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","are","was"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Each" is singular, so the verb must be singular: "was."$q$),
  ($q$conv-523$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Lena painted the mural over three summers______neighbors gathered each evening to watch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; and ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both "Lena painted the mural over three summers" and "neighbors gathered each evening to watch" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$),
  ($q$conv-524$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The researchers shared ______ findings at the annual conference.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","there","they're","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Their" is the plural possessive pronoun that matches "researchers."$q$),
  ($q$conv-525$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Although the forecast predicted sunshine______the picnic was moved indoors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": "," ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Although the forecast predicted sunshine" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-526$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$While researchers debated the findings______the journal postponed publication.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"While researchers debated the findings" is an introductory dependent clause, so it must be followed by a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-527$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The program teaches students to analyze data, write clear reports, and ______ their findings to a general audience.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["presentation","presenting","to presenting","present"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The items in the series must be parallel: "to analyze, write, and present."$q$),
  ($q$conv-528$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Every spring the herd migrates north, and by June it ______ the high plateau.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reached","reaches","had reached","would have reached"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence describes a recurring present-tense pattern ("migrates"), so the present tense "reaches" is consistent.$q$),
  ($q$conv-529$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The course covers four periods of history______ancient, medieval, early modern, and contemporary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The words before the blank form a complete sentence that introduces a list or elaboration, so a colon is the correct punctuation. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation would be incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-530$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum opens at nine______ however, the special exhibit does not open until noon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", "," ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses joined without a coordinating conjunction need a semicolon (a comma alone would create a comma splice).$q$),
  ($q$conv-531$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Maya packed a tent, a stove______ and a map for the camping trip.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Items in a series are separated by commas.$q$),
  ($q$conv-532$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The recipe requires three ingredients______ flour, butter, and sugar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon follows a complete sentence to introduce a list that explains it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-533$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The train was delayed______ so the passengers waited in the station café.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A comma precedes the coordinating conjunction 'so' joining two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-534$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the storm passed______ the neighbors began clearing fallen branches from the street.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A comma follows an introductory subordinate clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-535$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Dr. Okafor______ who studies migratory birds, will speak at the library tonight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ",", ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonessential phrase 'who studies migratory birds' is set off by commas on both sides, so a comma is needed here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-536$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The library's new wing includes a reading room______ a media lab, and a quiet study area.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": ","; "," "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Commas separate the items in a series.$q$),
  ($q$conv-537$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The lake froze early this year______ skaters were out by the first week of December.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": so "," so, ","; so ",", so "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses joined by 'so' require a comma before the conjunction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-539$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the road was closed______ the bus took a longer route.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma follows an introductory dependent clause.$q$),
