  ($q$conv-603$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Walking along the beach, ______ found a small, perfectly round stone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the afternoon","a small stone was found by Anya","Anya","the stone"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory participial phrase must modify the noun right after it: 'Anya' was walking.$q$),
  ($q$conv-604$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The CEO, along with her assistants,______ attending the conference.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","have been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is 'CEO'; the phrase 'along with her assistants' does not make it plural.$q$),
  ($q$conv-605$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The number of volunteers______ grown steadily each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","were","are","have"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'The number of' is singular and takes 'has.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-606$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The mayor praised the volunteers ______ had organized the cleanup.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","which","whom","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Who' is the subject of the clause 'had organized the cleanup.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-607$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The mayor, ______ the voters had elected twice, announced her retirement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","whose","whom","which"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Whom' is the object of 'had elected' in the clause 'the voters had elected [her].'$q$),
  ($q$conv-608$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The company has moved ______ headquarters to Chicago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","there","its","it's"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Its' is the possessive form for the singular 'company.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-609$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Neither of the two proposals______ adequate for the city's needs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","have been","were"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Neither' is singular and takes 'is.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-610$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The reports by the committee, which spent two years studying the issue,______ that more funding is needed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["suggests","suggest","is suggesting","has suggested"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plural subject 'reports' takes the plural verb 'suggest.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-611$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Having reviewed the evidence, ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["a unanimous decision was reached by the committee","it was decided unanimously by them","the decision seemed unanimous","the committee reached a unanimous decision"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The participial phrase 'Having reviewed the evidence' must modify the committee, the one who reviewed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-612$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The professor's lecture was as informative as ______ of the guest speaker.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["them","it were","that","those"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'That' refers back to the singular 'lecture' for a logical comparison.$q$),
  ($q$conv-613$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$If the data ______ more reliable, the team would have trusted the results.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were being","has been","had been","was"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A past counterfactual condition requires the past perfect 'had been.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-614$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The ideas that the philosopher developed in her later years______ quite different from her early work.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The plural subject 'ideas' takes 'were,' matching the past tense.$q$),
  ($q$conv-615$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Not only the manager but also the employees______ invited to the ceremony.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","were","is","was"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$With 'not only...but also,' the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'employees.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-616$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The astronomer demonstrated that light ______ at a constant speed in a vacuum.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["travels","traveled","has traveled","had traveled"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A general scientific truth takes the present tense 'travels.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-617$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Of the two designs, the second is ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["better","more better","the best","best"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Comparing two items requires the comparative 'better.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-618$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The anthology includes poems by Hughes, essays by Baldwin, and ______ by Morrison.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["writing of short stories","short stories","stories short","a short story's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The parallel structure 'poems..., essays..., short stories' needs a plural noun.$q$),
  ($q$conv-619$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$She not only wrote the report ______ presented it to the board.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["and also","but","but also","as well"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correlative pair is 'not only...but also.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-620$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$There ______ three reasons for the delay.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","was being","is","has"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The plural subject 'reasons' (after 'There') takes 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-621$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The children ______ playing in the park since noon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","have been","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plural subject needs 'have been' with 'since noon.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-622$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$He ______ the answer before anyone else did.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["knows","known","know","knew"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The simple past 'knew' matches the past context.$q$),
  ($q$conv-623$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The athletes trained harder than ______ had the previous year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","them","theirs","they"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject pronoun 'they' completes the comparison 'than they had.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-624$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Every one of the paintings______ been restored.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","were","have","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Every one' is singular and takes 'has.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-625$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The teacher gave the award to ______ she thought had worked hardest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whichever","who","whomever","whoever"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Whoever' is the subject of 'had worked hardest.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-626$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's members ______ their instruments before the concert.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has tuned","tuned","tunes","tuning"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plural subject 'members' with a past-tense context takes 'tuned.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-627$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Despite their differences, the two scientists ______ respect for each other's work.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sharing","shared","shares","has shared"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A plural subject in a past-tense narrative takes 'shared.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-628$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The recipes in this book, many of which ______ passed down for generations, are simple.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","have been","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'Many' (of the recipes) is plural and takes 'have been.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-629$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The proposed changes to the zoning laws, though controversial among residents,______ likely to pass.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","are","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plural subject 'changes' takes 'are.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-630$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The two sisters opened ______ own bakery last spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","its","their","there"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The plural possessive 'their' modifies 'own bakery.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-631$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The company's profits ______ higher this year than last.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was being","are","is","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plural subject 'profits' takes 'are' in a present-tense comparison.$q$),
  ($q$conv-632$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$My neighbor's garden ______ more vegetables than ours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["produces","are producing","produce","have produced"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The singular subject 'garden' takes 'produces.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-633$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Last summer, we ______ to the coast every weekend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will drive","drove","drive","have driven"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'Last summer' signals the simple past, 'drove.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-634$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The cat chased ______ tail around the room.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it's","its'","its","their"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Its' is the possessive; 'it's' means 'it is.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-635$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The students' projects, ______ were due on Monday, were displayed in the hall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","whose","whom","which"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$'Which' refers to things (projects) in a nonessential clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-636$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Rarely ______ such a talented young pianist.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["we heard","did we heard","have we heard","we have heard"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the negative adverb 'Rarely,' the auxiliary comes before the subject: 'have we heard.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-637$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The principal, as well as the teachers,______ supporting the new schedule.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","are","is","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is 'principal'; 'as well as the teachers' does not change its number.$q$),
  ($q$conv-638$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Anyone who wants to join the club should submit ______ application by Friday.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["your","its","his or her","our"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The singular 'anyone' takes a singular possessive; 'his or her' is the formal choice for agreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-639$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new exhibit features sculptures, paintings, and ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["photographing","photographs of which","to photograph","photographs"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The series needs parallel nouns: sculptures, paintings, photographs.$q$),
  ($q$conv-640$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The lecture was so engaging ______ nobody looked at a phone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["than","that","what","which"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'So...that' forms the standard result construction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-641$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The coach asked ______ players to arrive early.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["all the of","all of","all of the","the all of"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'All of the' is the standard idiomatic phrase before a plural noun with an article.$q$),
  ($q$conv-642$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The study, along with several earlier ones, ______ that sleep affects memory.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have suggested","suggest","are suggesting","suggests"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The singular subject 'study' takes 'suggests'; the interrupting phrase does not change that.$q$),
  ($q$conv-643$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$By next June, the city ______ the new library for two years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has operated","had operated","will have operated","operated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The future perfect 'will have operated' fits a future time reference with a duration.$q$),
  ($q$conv-644$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The explorers, ______ supplies had run low, turned back before reaching the summit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","whom","which","who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whose' shows possession of the supplies by the explorers.$q$),
  ($q$conv-645$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the pilot nor the flight attendants ______ the announcement before the turbulence began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["heard","was hearing","hears","has heard"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The past tense 'heard' fits the sequence, and the verb form does not vary by number here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-646$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The article's main argument is that cities should invest in public transit, ______ reduces both traffic and emissions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["these","what","which","who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Which' refers to the investment in public transit described earlier in the clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-647$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The conductor raised her baton______ and the orchestra fell silent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma precedes 'and' joining two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-648$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Wanting to improve his score______ Jamal began studying each evening.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A comma follows the introductory participial phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-649$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The island has one road______ it circles the coast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": "," ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces the explanation of the road's route.$q$),
  ($q$conv-650$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The senator's proposal______ which would raise the minimum wage, passed by a narrow margin.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The nonessential clause beginning with 'which' needs a comma on each side.$q$),
  ($q$conv-651$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The physicist received two honors that year______ the Nobel Prize and the Copley Medal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces the specific honors after a complete clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-652$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The path was narrow and steep______ nevertheless, the hikers pressed on.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A semicolon precedes the conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-653$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The editor, who had read the manuscript twice______ asked the author for major revisions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The comma closes the nonessential clause 'who had read the manuscript twice.'$q$),
  ($q$conv-654$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The students who studied abroad______ reported greater confidence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; "," ",", ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The clause 'who studied abroad' identifies which students and is essential, so no comma is used.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Marine ecologist Talia Ferrante had expected warming waters to drive the kelp forests off the coast of Baja California into steady decline ______ her decade of surveys revealed that some stands, shaded by persistent fog banks, were actually expanding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["decline, instead,","decline instead,","decline, instead;","decline; instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both "Marine ecologist... into steady decline" and "her decade of surveys revealed..." are independent clauses. "Instead" is a conjunctive adverb, so a semicolon must come before it and a comma after it. The other choices create a comma splice or attach "instead" to the wrong clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The archive's most fragile holdings—letters, ledgers, and a handful of hand-drawn maps from the ______ are kept in a climate-controlled vault that only two conservators may enter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1840s,","1840s;","1840s—","1840s:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The list of holdings is a nonessential element that interrupts the subject ("holdings") and its verb ("are kept"). Because it opens with a dash, it must close with a matching dash. A comma would mismatch the opening punctuation, and a semicolon or colon cannot separate a subject from its verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineers who designed the first generation of earthquake-resistant towers in ______ relied on base isolators, flexible bearings that let a building's foundation shift without transmitting the full force of the shaking upward.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Santiago,","Santiago","Santiago;","Santiago—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The long subject "The engineers who designed the first generation of earthquake-resistant towers in Santiago" must not be separated from its verb "relied." No punctuation belongs in the blank, even though the subject is long.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In her study of medieval trade networks, historian Odile Marchetti argues that the decline of the Champagne fairs had less to do with war than with ______ the rise of permanent banking houses in Italian cities made the fairs' traveling money changers obsolete.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["finance:","finance;","finance,","finance, and"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and what follows explains specifically how finance was responsible. A colon correctly introduces such an explanation. A semicolon would not signal that the second clause elaborates on "finance," a comma creates a comma splice, and "and" presents the explanation as a separate, unrelated point.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novelist ______ debut, a sprawling family saga set in 1920s Lagos, was rejected by eleven publishers, later called the book's rejection "the most useful education I ever received."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Chidinma Okorie whose","Chidinma Okorie; whose","Chidinma Okorie—whose","Chidinma Okorie, whose"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause "whose debut... was rejected by eleven publishers" is nonessential information about the novelist and is closed by a comma before "later called." It must therefore open with a comma as well. No punctuation would leave the clause unbalanced, a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause, and a dash does not match the closing comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some astronomers suspect that the faint, reddish object designated 2019-QX4 is not a single body at ______ two icy fragments orbiting so closely that their light blurs together in even the most powerful telescopes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["all, but","all; but","all but","all: but"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Is not a single body at all but two icy fragments" is one clause using the construction "not X but Y." Because "but" here joins two noun phrases rather than two independent clauses, no punctuation should separate them.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To reconstruct the diets of Neolithic farmers, archaeologists analyzed three kinds of evidence: charred grains from storage ______ residues absorbed into the walls of cooking pots, some still bearing fingerprints of the potters; and isotopes preserved in human bone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pits, sheds, and hearths,","pits, sheds, and hearths;","pits; sheds; and hearths,","pits, sheds, and hearths:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The list after the colon has three items, and the items themselves contain commas ("pits, sheds, and hearths" and "pots, some still bearing..."). To keep the items distinct, they must be separated by semicolons, as the semicolon before "and isotopes" confirms. So the first item must end with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The glassmakers of Murano guarded their techniques so jealously that, for centuries, artisans were forbidden to leave the ______ who did were sometimes pursued across Europe by agents of the Venetian state.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["island; those","island, those","island those","island, and, those"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Artisans were forbidden to leave the island" and "those who did were sometimes pursued..." are independent clauses. A semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and, those" incorrectly puts a comma after the conjunction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have long known that octopuses can change color in a fraction of a second. What puzzled them was how an animal with only one type of light receptor in its ______ apparently colorblind, could match the hues of its surroundings so precisely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["eyes and thus","eyes; and thus","eyes: and thus","eyes, and thus"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"And thus apparently colorblind" is a nonessential phrase describing the animal; it is closed by the comma after "colorblind" and must be opened by a comma as well. Without that opening comma, the phrase is unbalanced; a semicolon or colon cannot introduce this modifying phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The 1911 survey of the Okefenokee Swamp recorded more than two hundred bird ______ the 1990s, fewer than half of those species could be found there during any given breeding season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["species, by","species by","species; by","species, by:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contains two independent clauses: "The 1911 survey... recorded more than two hundred bird species" and "by the 1990s, fewer than half... could be found there." A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation makes "by the 1990s" appear to modify the survey.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Fermented in clay amphorae buried up to their necks in the ______ traditional Georgian wines develop a deep amber color and a tannic bite that surprises drinkers used to lighter white wines.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["earth;","earth,","earth","earth:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence begins with a long introductory participial phrase ("Fermented in clay amphorae buried... in the earth") that modifies "traditional Georgian wines." A comma should separate this introductory phrase from the main clause. A semicolon or colon requires an independent clause before it, and no punctuation produces a confusing run-together.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Of the forty-two instruments in the Smithsonian's collection made by the Cremonese luthier Nicolò ______ the 1688 viola is considered the best preserved, its varnish still bearing the original amber tint.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Amati,","Amati;","Amati","Amati:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Of the forty-two instruments... made by... Nicolò Amati" is an introductory prepositional phrase that must be set off from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before it, and no punctuation would blur where the introductory phrase ends.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the bridge's designers tested a scale model in a wind tunnel, they noticed a troubling ______ gusts above a certain speed, the deck began to twist in a slow, rhythmic motion that grew larger with each cycle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pattern; in","pattern, in","pattern in","pattern: in"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause specifies what the troubling pattern was. A colon is used to introduce such an explanation. A semicolon doesn't signal the explanatory relationship, a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Poet and translator Ilse ______ rendered the poems of the Swedish modernists into English for nearly four decades, insisted that a translation should "sound like it was written yesterday, in the language of the reader."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Brandt who","Brandt; who","Brandt, who","Brandt—who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Who rendered the poems... for nearly four decades" is a nonessential clause about Brandt, and it closes with a comma before "insisted." It must open with a matching comma. A dash would not match the closing comma, a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause, and omitting punctuation leaves the clause unbalanced.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most social insects, which build nests and defend a fixed ______ army ants are nomadic, moving their entire colony—sometimes more than a million individuals—to a new bivouac site almost every night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["territory;","territory,","territory","territory—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "Unlike most social insects, which build nests and defend a fixed territory" is introductory and must be followed by a comma before the main clause ("army ants are nomadic"). A semicolon or dash is not used to end an introductory phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study's authors were careful to note a limitation: because all of the participants were recruited from a single ______ results may not apply to people who grew up in rural areas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["city, the","city; the","city: the","city. The"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because all of the participants were recruited from a single city" is a dependent clause introducing the main clause "the results may not apply...." A comma correctly separates them. A semicolon, colon, or period would leave the dependent clause standing alone as if it were a complete sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Hoping to understand why some languages have dozens of words for kinship relations while others have only a ______ linguists compared vocabularies from more than four hundred societies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["few;","few","few:","few,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Hoping to understand why... while others have only a few" is a long introductory participial phrase modifying "linguists." It should end with a comma. A semicolon or colon requires a complete clause before it, and no punctuation runs the phrase into the subject.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's 1952 recording of the partitas is prized by collectors for a strange ______ in the third movement, a passing truck can be heard rumbling beneath the music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reason, in","reason; in","reason: in","reason in"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The violinist's 1952 recording... is prized by collectors for a strange reason" is independent, and the following clause explains the reason. A colon introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, a semicolon fails to signal the explanation, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Engineers at the plant considered two options for cooling the ______ water drawn from the nearby river or a closed loop of recirculated coolant—before settling on a hybrid of both.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reactor, fresh","reactor—fresh","reactor: fresh","reactor; fresh"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase listing the two options is nonessential and closes with a dash after "coolant." It must therefore open with a matching dash. A comma or colon would not match the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot introduce a phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mathematician spent years trying to prove the ______ she discovered, to her dismay, that a colleague had published a counterexample the previous spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["conjecture; then","conjecture, then","conjecture then","conjecture, then;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here: "The mathematician spent years trying to prove the conjecture" and "then she discovered...." "Then" is an adverb, not a coordinating conjunction, so a comma alone would create a comma splice. A semicolon correctly joins the clauses.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Bioluminescent fungi, which glow a pale green in the dark forests of Brazil and ______ are thought to attract insects that carry their spores to new logs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Japan;","Japan","Japan—","Japan,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The relative clause "which glow a pale green... in the dark forests of Brazil and Japan" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "fungi." It must close with a comma before the verb "are thought." A semicolon or dash would not match, and no punctuation leaves the clause unbalanced.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Greek astronomer Aristarchus proposed that Earth revolves around the Sun nearly two millennia before Copernicus did. His idea gained few ______ the prevailing view, endorsed by Aristotle, held that a moving Earth would fling objects off its surface.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["followers; however,","followers, however,","followers, however;","followers however"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"His idea gained few followers, however" is a complete clause in which "however" contrasts with the previous sentence; it is followed by a second independent clause that explains why. The semicolon must come after "however" to join the two clauses. Placing it before "however" would wrongly attach the contrast to the explanation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Among the most celebrated works of the Harlem Renaissance is the 1926 collection by the poet ______ The Weary Blues, which drew on the rhythms of jazz and the blues to depict everyday Black life in the urban North.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Langston Hughes","Langston Hughes,","Langston Hughes;","Langston Hughes, and"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The Weary Blues" renames the noun phrase "the 1926 collection by the poet Langston Hughes," and it is followed by the nonessential clause "which drew on...." Because the title is an appositive that adds information about a collection already identified (the poet's 1926 collection), it is set off by a comma. A semicolon cannot introduce an appositive, no punctuation would fuse the poet's name with the book title, and "and" would wrongly turn the title into a second, separate work.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's director ______ spent three winters filming in the Arctic, says that the hardest part of the project was not the cold but the months of total darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Marit Solberg, who","Marit Solberg who","Marit Solberg; who","Marit Solberg—who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Who spent three winters filming in the Arctic" is a nonessential clause identifying more about the director, already named. It closes with a comma before "says," so it must open with a comma. A dash would not match the closing comma, and a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers found that bumblebees trained to associate a reward with a particular shape could later identify that shape by touch ______ suggests that bees form something like a mental image that combines information from different senses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["alone; a result that","alone. A result that","alone, a result, that","alone, a result that"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "a result that suggests..." is a noun phrase summing up the preceding clause, so it attaches with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave "a result that suggests..." as a fragment, and the comma after "result" wrongly separates the noun from its restrictive clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To keep the reconstruction faithful to the original, the restorers used only materials available in the fourteenth ______ lime mortar, hand-split oak beams, and clay tiles fired in a wood kiln.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["century;","century, such as:","century:","century"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and a list of the specific materials follows. A colon introduces the list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, "such as:" incorrectly places a colon after "such as," and no punctuation runs the list into the sentence.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Even after the patent expired, few manufacturers attempted to copy the ______ precision machining it required was beyond the capabilities of most factories at the time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["device, the","device; the","device the","device, and, the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Few manufacturers attempted to copy the device" and "the precision machining it required was beyond..." are both independent clauses. A semicolon joins them correctly. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," with a comma after the conjunction is incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mural's central ______ woman holding a lantern above a flooded street—was inspired by a photograph the artist found in her grandmother's attic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["figure—a","figure, a","figure: a","figure a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive "a woman holding a lantern above a flooded street" is closed with a dash before "was inspired," so it must open with a matching dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash, and no punctuation leaves the dash unpaired.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many of the town's earliest records were lost in the flood of ______ the minutes of every council meeting held between 1890 and 1902 survived because a clerk had taken them home to copy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1903, remarkably, however,","1903; remarkably however","1903, remarkably; however,","1903; remarkably, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first independent clause ends at "1903," and a second independent clause begins. A semicolon must join them. "Remarkably" and "however" are both interrupting adverbs at the start of the second clause, so each is followed by a comma. Only the first choice combines the semicolon with correctly punctuated adverbs.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Although the composer wrote more than three hundred works over his long career, he is remembered today almost entirely for a single ______ a brief, haunting setting of a medieval lullaby for unaccompanied choir.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["piece;","piece, that is","piece:","piece. That is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The main clause is complete, and what follows identifies the single piece. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon must be followed by an independent clause, and "that is" would need to be followed by a comma (and the period choice creates a fragment).$q$),
  ($q$conv-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In the 1970s, the chemist Mario Molina and his colleague F. Sherwood Rowland warned that chlorofluorocarbons, or ______ common in aerosol sprays and refrigerants, could destroy the ozone layer that shields Earth from ultraviolet radiation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["CFCs then","CFCs, then","CFCs; then","CFCs—then"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Then common in aerosol sprays and refrigerants" is a nonessential phrase describing chlorofluorocarbons; it closes with a comma before "could destroy." The phrase must therefore open with a comma. A dash would not match, and a semicolon cannot introduce a phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novel opens with a list of ______ a red bicycle, a drawer full of keys to locks that no longer exist, and a letter that was never mailed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["objects:","objects;","objects, including:","objects that are:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause "The novel opens with a list of objects" is followed by the items of the list. A colon correctly introduces them. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, and a colon should not follow a word like "including" or a verb like "are" that already leads grammatically into the list.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tunnel's ______ by a team of nineteenth-century surveyors working with little more than chains, compasses, and candlelight—remained accurate to within a few inches over its entire two-mile length.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["alignment, calculated","alignment: calculated","alignment calculated","alignment—calculated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "calculated by a team... and candlelight" is a nonessential interruption between the subject "alignment" and the verb "remained." It closes with a dash, so it must open with a dash. A comma would not match the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The survey asked respondents to rank five ______ affordability, commute time, school quality, access to parks, and proximity to family.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["priorities;","priorities, which were:","priorities:","priorities which were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The survey asked respondents to rank five priorities" is an independent clause, and the list that follows identifies the priorities. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and "which were:" puts a colon after a verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Seismologists have identified a subtle signal that sometimes precedes large ______ slow, almost imperceptible slipping along a fault that can last for weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["earthquakes;","earthquakes:","earthquakes, and","earthquakes"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause before the blank is followed by a description of the signal. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon would require an independent clause after it, "and" would wrongly present the slipping as something the signal precedes, and no punctuation runs the explanation into "earthquakes."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The city's first public ______ in 1848 in a converted warehouse near the harbor—held fewer than two thousand volumes, most of them donated by local merchants.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["library—opened","library, opened","library; opened","library opened"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "opened in 1848 in a converted warehouse near the harbor" interrupts the subject and verb and is closed with a dash. It must open with a dash. A comma would mismatch the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot set off a phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Ultramarine, a pigment made from ground lapis lazuli, was once more valuable than gold; Renaissance contracts sometimes specified exactly how much of it a painter was required to ______ in the robes of the Virgin Mary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["use; usually","use: usually,","use. Usually","use, usually"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Usually in the robes of the Virgin Mary" is a modifying phrase that adds information to the clause ending "required to use." A comma attaches it. A semicolon or period would make the phrase a fragment, and the colon option adds an unnecessary comma after "usually."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The professor's lecture notes, which were later published as a ______ the basis for the first undergraduate course in ecology offered at the university.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["textbook became","textbook; became","textbook, became","textbook—became"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which were later published as a textbook" is nonessential and begins with a comma after "notes." It must end with a comma before the main verb "became." No punctuation, a semicolon, or a dash would fail to close the clause properly.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When its channel to the North Sea silted up in the fifteenth century, the port of Bruges was cut off from the ______ city's fortunes declined steadily as trade moved to Antwerp.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sea, the","sea, and the","sea and, the","sea the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses ("the port... was cut off from the sea" and "the city's fortunes declined steadily") must be joined properly. A comma followed by the coordinating conjunction "and" is correct. A comma alone is a comma splice, the comma after "and" is misplaced, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many species of ______ the tiny Kirk's dik-dik of eastern Africa, mate for life and defend small territories as a pair.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["antelope, including","antelope including","antelope; including","antelope: including"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Including the tiny Kirk's dik-dik of eastern Africa" is a nonessential phrase, closed by the comma before "mate." It must be opened by a comma. A semicolon or colon cannot introduce this phrase, and no punctuation leaves it unbalanced.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The volunteers were divided into three groups: those who received the vaccine at the start of the ______ who received it six weeks later; and those who received a placebo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["study, those","study: those","study those","study; those"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The list after the colon contains items with internal punctuation, and the second and third items are separated with a semicolon ("later; and those"). For consistency, the first and second items must also be separated with a semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Refined during the Second World War to detect approaching ______ radar was soon adapted by meteorologists, who realized that its signals also bounced off raindrops.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["aircraft;","aircraft","aircraft,","aircraft—"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory participial phrase "Refined during the Second World War to detect approaching aircraft" must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or dash cannot end an introductory phrase, and no punctuation would make "aircraft radar" read as one phrase.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The architect's guiding principle was ______ "A building should look as though it grew from the ground it stands on."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["simple;","simple:","simple","simple, that"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The architect's guiding principle was simple" is an independent clause, and the quotation that follows states the principle. A colon introduces such an explanatory quotation. A semicolon would not signal the relationship, no punctuation runs the clause into the quotation, and "that" would create a grammatically awkward construction with the capitalized quotation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Neither the original blueprints nor the contractor's ______ any mention of the hidden room that workers discovered behind the library's fireplace in 2014.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["notes contain","notes, contain","notes; contain","notes—contain"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Neither the original blueprints nor the contractor's notes" is the compound subject, and "contain" is its verb. No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineers knew the dam could withstand a hundred-year ______ uncertain was whether it could survive the kind of once-in-a-millennium storm that climate models now predict will become more common.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flood, what remained","flood what remained","flood: what, remained","flood; what remained"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first clause is independent, and "what remained uncertain was whether..." is also an independent clause (its subject is the noun clause "what remained uncertain"). A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the last option inserts a comma inside the noun clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$A single teaspoon of healthy topsoil can contain more microorganisms than there are people on ______ bacteria, fungi, protozoa, and nematodes, all interacting in ways scientists are only beginning to understand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Earth;","Earth, these are","Earth—","Earth"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The material after the blank is a list explaining what the microorganisms are. A dash can introduce such an explanatory list at the end of a sentence. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, "these are" after a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation runs the list into "Earth."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The poet Gwendolyn Brooks, whose 1949 collection Annie Allen made her the first Black author to win a Pulitzer ______ much of her life in the Bronzeville neighborhood of Chicago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Prize spent","Prize, spent","Prize; spent","Prize—spent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The relative clause "whose 1949 collection... to win a Pulitzer Prize" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "Brooks." It must close with a comma before the main verb "spent."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The excavation uncovered thousands of fragments of ______ which were painted with scenes from daily life, including fishing, weaving, and the brewing of beer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pottery, many of","pottery many of","pottery; many of","pottery. Many of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Many of which were painted..." is a relative clause providing nonessential information about the pottery fragments, so it is set off by a comma. A semicolon or period would leave "many of which..." as a fragment, and no punctuation fails to separate the nonessential clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The algorithm performed well on the test ______ when researchers applied it to images from hospitals in other countries, its accuracy fell sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["data, however,","data however;","data, however","data; however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The algorithm performed well on the test data" and "when researchers applied it... its accuracy fell sharply" are independent clauses. The conjunctive adverb "however" introduces the second, so a semicolon goes before it and a comma after. The other choices create comma splices or put the semicolon in the wrong place.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Most of the expedition's ______ the cameras, the satellite phone, and the solar chargers—failed within a week in the humidity of the cloud forest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["electronics,","electronics:","electronics—","electronics"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The list "the cameras, the satellite phone, and the solar chargers" interrupts the subject and its verb "failed." Because the list contains commas and is closed by a dash, it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The glacier has retreated nearly two miles since ______ a rate of loss that has accelerated sharply in the past two decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1900;","1900,","1900, and","1900."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"A rate of loss that has accelerated sharply" is a noun phrase that sums up the preceding clause; it is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would turn it into a fragment, and "and" would leave the noun phrase without a verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute during ______ night, however, it can slow to fewer than 50 beats per minute as the bird enters a hibernation-like state called torpor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flight; at","flight, at","flight at","flight: at"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The hummingbird's heart can beat... during flight" and "at night, however, it can slow..." are two independent clauses. They must be joined with a semicolon (the "however" is set off within the second clause). A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The town's annual festival, first held in ______ fewer than two hundred visitors; today, it draws more than forty thousand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1958 attracted","1958; attracted","1958—attracted","1958, attracted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"First held in 1958" is a nonessential phrase between the subject "festival" and its verb "attracted," and it opens with a comma. It must close with a comma. A dash would not match, a semicolon cannot separate a subject and verb, and no punctuation leaves the phrase unbalanced.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Only one of the three proposed sites met all of the ______ it was at least fifty miles from the nearest city, sat on stable bedrock, and had access to a reliable water supply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["criteria,","criteria","criteria:","criteria, and"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second clause explains how the site met all of the criteria. A colon introduces such an explanation after an independent clause. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to convey that the second clause specifies the criteria.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bridge, completed in 1937 after four years of ______ the longest suspension span in the world for nearly three decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["construction remained","construction, remained","construction; remained","construction: remained"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Completed in 1937 after four years of construction" is a nonessential phrase that opens with a comma after "bridge." It must close with a comma before the verb "remained." A semicolon or colon cannot separate the subject and verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Few people today realize that the color ______ was originally the name of a fruit and only later came to describe a hue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["orange","orange,","orange—","\"orange,\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The color orange" uses "orange" as a restrictive appositive that identifies which color is meant; restrictive appositives are not set off with punctuation. Adding a comma or dash would separate the subject from its verb "was."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The organizers expected roughly five hundred attendees at the ______ than three thousand people arrived, forcing the event to move outdoors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["conference, instead, more","conference instead, more","conference; instead more,","conference; instead, more"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here. "Instead" is a conjunctive adverb that begins the second clause, so a semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it. The other choices create a comma splice, a run-on, or misplace the comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The traditional dish requires three ______ that have soaked overnight, slow-roasted tomatoes, and a broth simmered for at least six hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["components; chickpeas","components, chickpeas","components: chickpeas","components chickpeas"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The traditional dish requires three components" is an independent clause introducing a list, so a colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, a comma would make "chickpeas" read like an appositive for only one item, and no punctuation runs the list into the clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Scientists studying the ______ a 3.4-billion-year-old rock formation in Western Australia—have found microscopic structures they believe are the fossilized remains of some of Earth's earliest life.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Strelley Pool chert,","Strelley Pool chert—","Strelley Pool chert:","Strelley Pool chert"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The appositive "a 3.4-billion-year-old rock formation in Western Australia" is closed by a dash before "have found." It must be opened by a dash as well. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash, and no punctuation leaves the dash unpaired.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript had been copied by hand dozens of times before the printing press was ______ its surviving versions differ from one another in hundreds of small ways.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["invented,","invented;","invented","invented:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because the manuscript had been copied... before the printing press was invented" is a dependent clause beginning the sentence. It must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or colon cannot follow a dependent clause, and no punctuation creates a confusing run-together.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor worked in an unusual ______ salvaged from shipwrecks along the Outer Banks of North Carolina.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["medium; timber","medium, timber, that was","medium timber","medium: timber"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause "The sculptor worked in an unusual medium" is complete, and what follows identifies the medium. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, the third option adds unnecessary words and commas, and no punctuation runs the explanation into the noun.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The team's analysis of tree rings, ice cores, and lake ______ together provide a record of rainfall stretching back more than two thousand years—suggests that the region's current drought is the most severe in at least twelve centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sediments, which","sediments; which","sediments—which","sediments which"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which together provide a record of rainfall..." is closed by a dash before the verb "suggests," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma would not match the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new law did not ban the pesticide ______ it required farmers to keep detailed records of when and where they applied it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["outright, rather,","outright; rather,","outright rather","outright; rather"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The new law did not ban the pesticide outright" and "it required farmers to keep detailed records..." are independent clauses. "Rather" is a conjunctive adverb introducing the second clause, so it is preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The photograph shows the inventor standing beside her ______ prototype she built in her garage in 1923 using bicycle parts and a sewing-machine motor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["creation, a","creation; a","creation. A","creation: a,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A prototype she built in her garage..." is an appositive noun phrase renaming "creation." A comma sets it off. A semicolon or period would leave the appositive as a fragment, and the colon choice adds a stray comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The committee's final report recommended three changes to the city's water ______ replacing lead service lines in older neighborhoods, which were built before 1950; adding corrosion inhibitors at the treatment plant; and testing every school's drinking fountains twice a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["system;","system,","system","system:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause "The committee's final report recommended three changes to the city's water system" introduces a list, so a colon is required. The list items are separated by semicolons because they contain internal commas; a semicolon before the first item would not introduce the list.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Renata Vasquez-Lowe spent six years documenting the courtship displays of the ______ small songbird whose males build elaborate bowers decorated with blue objects, from flower petals to bottle caps.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["satin bowerbird a","satin bowerbird; a","satin bowerbird, a","satin bowerbird. A"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"A small songbird whose males build elaborate bowers..." is an appositive renaming "the satin bowerbird," so it is set off with a comma. No punctuation runs the appositive into the noun, and a semicolon or period would leave the appositive as a fragment.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The software update was supposed to fix the ______ it introduced a new problem that caused the app to crash whenever users tried to upload a photo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bug, unfortunately,","bug; unfortunately,","bug unfortunately,","bug; unfortunately"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses are joined: "The software update was supposed to fix the bug" and "it introduced a new problem...." "Unfortunately" is an introductory adverb in the second clause. A semicolon before it and a comma after it is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historians disagree about the purpose of the stone ______ some argue that it served as an astronomical calendar, while others believe it marked a boundary between rival kingdoms.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["circle:","circle,","circle","circle, and"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause explains the disagreement. A colon is appropriate. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to show that the second clause details the disagreement.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Anyone who has tried to photograph the night sky knows that the camera often records far more than the eye can ______ stars too faint to see, the smudge of a distant galaxy, the pale green glow of the upper atmosphere.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["see;","see, such as:","see, for example;","see:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and what follows is a list of examples. A colon introduces such a list. A semicolon must precede an independent clause, a colon should not follow "such as," and "for example;" misuses the semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new telescope's mirror, which is made of 18 hexagonal segments coated in a thin layer of ______ unfold in space like the petals of a flower.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["gold was designed to","gold; was designed to","gold, was designed to","gold—was designed to"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which is made of 18 hexagonal segments... gold" opens with a comma after "mirror" and must close with a comma before the main verb "was designed."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the composer's notebooks were finally made available to ______ discovered that several of her most famous melodies had first appeared decades earlier as children's songs she had written for her nieces.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["scholars they","scholars, they","scholars; they","scholars: they"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The dependent clause "When the composer's notebooks were finally made available to scholars" must be followed by a comma before the main clause "they discovered...." A semicolon or colon would leave the dependent clause as a fragment, and no punctuation blurs the boundary.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The flat, treeless ______ is home to a surprising diversity of life, from burrowing owls to the swift fox, a species once nearly eliminated from the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["plain","plain,","plain—","plain;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The flat, treeless plain" is the subject of the sentence, and "is" is its verb. No punctuation should separate them.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In a series of ______ researchers asked participants to estimate how long a short video clip lasted, sometimes after playing them fast music and sometimes after playing slow music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["experiments;","experiments:","experiments","experiments,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"In a series of experiments" is an introductory prepositional phrase that can be followed by a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before them, and with no punctuation "experiments researchers" would be confusing.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The coastal town relies on a single industry for most of its ______ it is unclear how the community will adapt as fish stocks continue to decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["jobs,","jobs","jobs, and","jobs and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined. A comma plus the coordinating conjunction "and" is correct. A comma alone is a comma splice, no punctuation is a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mountain pass is open for only about four months each ______ the rest of the year, snowdrifts more than thirty feet deep make it impassable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["year, for","year; for","year for","year: for,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The mountain pass is open for only about four months each year" and "for the rest of the year, snowdrifts... make it impassable" are independent clauses, so they are joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the last option adds a comma that separates "for" from its object.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ceramicist's glazes owe their unusual depth to an ingredient she declines to ______ she will say only that it comes from a riverbank near the village where her grandmother was born.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["name; instead,","name, instead,","name instead,","name, instead;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first clause ends at "name," and a second independent clause begins with the conjunctive adverb "instead." A semicolon must precede "instead," and a comma follows it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The report's authors acknowledged one important ______ data on rural clinics came from surveys completed by clinic directors, who may have overstated the services their facilities provide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["weakness, the","weakness the","weakness, and the","weakness: the"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The report's authors acknowledged one important weakness" is independent, and the next clause identifies the weakness. A colon introduces the explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to show that the second clause names the weakness.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violin maker inspects every piece of spruce for ______ grain lines, an absence of knots, and a bright, ringing tone when the wood is tapped.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["three qualities; tight","three qualities, tight","three qualities: tight","three qualities tight"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause "The violin maker inspects every piece of spruce for three qualities" is complete and introduces a list. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and a comma or no punctuation runs the list into the clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The theater's founder, who had trained as an architect before turning to stage ______ the building's unusual horseshoe-shaped auditorium herself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["design planned","design, planned","design; planned","design—planned"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonessential clause "who had trained as an architect before turning to stage design" is opened with a comma and must close with a comma before the main verb "planned."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Because the comet's orbit carries it close to the Sun only once every 76 ______ astronomers who miss one appearance may have to wait the rest of their careers for the next.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["years,","years;","years:","years"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because the comet's orbit carries it close to the Sun only once every 76 years" is a dependent clause at the start of the sentence, so it ends with a comma. A semicolon or colon would make it a fragment, and no punctuation runs it into the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tidal ______ by the gravitational pull of both the Moon and the Sun—are strongest during new and full moons, when the two bodies align.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bulges, created","bulges: created","bulges created","bulges—created"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "created by the gravitational pull of both the Moon and the Sun" interrupts the subject and verb and is closed with a dash. It must open with a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study followed more than 8,000 participants for twenty ______ who walked at least thirty minutes a day had a significantly lower risk of heart disease than those who were largely sedentary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["years, those","years those","years; those","years: those,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two independent clauses ("The study followed... for twenty years" and "those who walked... had a significantly lower risk") are joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the last choice inserts a comma between the subject "those" and its modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novelist is known for her intricate ______ chapter of her latest book, for example, is told from the point of view of a different object in the same house.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["structures, each","structures; each","structures each","structures, and, each"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The novelist is known for her intricate structures" and "each chapter... is told from the point of view of a different object" are independent clauses, joined correctly with a semicolon. (A colon would also be possible, but it is not offered.) A comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The crater, which scientists estimate is about 66 million years ______ thought to have been formed by the same impact that led to the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["old, is","old is","old; is","old—is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Which scientists estimate is about 66 million years old" is a nonessential clause opened by a comma after "crater"; it must close with a comma before the verb "is thought."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The museum has acquired a rare first edition of the poet's debut ______ in 1855 and printed at the author's own expense, the book sold so poorly that most copies were eventually given away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["collection, published","collection published","collection; published,","collection. Published"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence that begins with "Published in 1855" has its own subject ("the book") and verb ("sold"), and the introductory phrase modifies "the book." A period separating the two sentences is correct. A comma would create a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the last option adds a misplaced comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Engineers designed the stadium's roof to retract in under ten ______ fans can enjoy open skies on a clear evening and be sheltered within minutes if a storm rolls in.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["minutes so,","minutes; so","minutes, so","minutes, so,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"So" is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so it should be preceded by a comma. A comma after "so" or a semicolon before it is incorrect.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists working at the site found a cache of ______ arrowheads, a carved bone flute, and dozens of beads made from the shells of freshwater mussels.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["artifacts; obsidian","artifacts: obsidian","artifacts, obsidian","artifacts, such as: obsidian"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Archaeologists working at the site found a cache of artifacts" is complete and is followed by a list of the artifacts. A colon introduces the list. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, a comma makes "obsidian arrowheads" look like an appositive for only part of the list, and a colon should not follow "such as."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' conclusion was ______ birds raised in noisy urban environments sang at a higher pitch than birds of the same species raised in quiet forests.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["clear:","clear;","clear,","clear"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The researchers' conclusion was clear" is an independent clause, and the following clause states the conclusion. A colon introduces it. A semicolon does not signal the explanation, a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novel's ______ an aging lighthouse keeper on a remote Norwegian island—narrates the story in a series of unsent letters to his estranged daughter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["protagonist,","protagonist:","protagonist","protagonist—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "an aging lighthouse keeper on a remote Norwegian island" sits between the subject and its verb "narrates" and closes with a dash. It must open with a dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Though she never studied ______ the self-taught botanist identified more than two hundred plant species in the hills around her farm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["formally;","formally:","formally,","formally"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Though she never studied formally" is a dependent introductory clause, which should end with a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon would leave the dependent clause as a fragment, and no punctuation obscures the boundary.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bakery sells three kinds of ______ a dense dark rye, a tangy sourdough, and a sweet brioche glazed with honey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bread;","bread:","bread, including","bread, such as:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause is followed by a list of all three kinds of bread. A colon introduces the complete list. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, "including" implies the list is incomplete even though the sentence names exactly three kinds, and a colon should not follow "such as."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The geologists' survey of the ______ along with satellite measurements of how much the ground has risen—suggests that magma is accumulating beneath the volcano faster than previously thought.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["crater rim—","crater rim,","crater rim;","crater rim"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "along with satellite measurements of how much the ground has risen" interrupts the subject "survey" and its verb "suggests." It closes with a dash and must open with a dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary focuses on a single ______ an elderly fisherman in Ghana who has noticed his catch shrinking year after year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["subject;","subject. An","subject, an,","subject:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause is followed by a phrase identifying the subject. A colon introduces such a phrase. A semicolon must be followed by an independent clause, a period would make the phrase a fragment, and the last option adds an incorrect comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Scientists once assumed that the deep ocean was too cold and dark to support much ______ hydrothermal vents discovered in 1977 were found to host thriving communities of tube worms, clams, and bacteria.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["life, however,","life however,","life; however,","life; however"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined by the conjunctive adverb "however," so a semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Of all the challenges facing the ______ greatest was the lack of reliable maps of the river's upper reaches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["expedition the","expedition, the","expedition; the","expedition: the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Of all the challenges facing the expedition" is an introductory phrase, and "the greatest was the lack of reliable maps..." is the main clause. A comma separates them. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before the mark.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some critics dismissed the film as ______ others praised its refusal to offer viewers an easy resolution.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bleak, while","bleak; while","bleak while,","bleak. While"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"While others praised its refusal..." is a dependent clause attached to the main clause; a comma before "while" is correct here because "while" introduces a contrasting clause. A semicolon or period would leave the dependent clause as a fragment.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers measured three variables in each ______ temperature, the concentration of dissolved oxygen, and the number of insect larvae per square meter of streambed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stream; water","stream, water","stream water","stream: water"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause introduces a list of the three variables, so a colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and a comma or no punctuation makes "water temperature" run into the preceding noun.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many listeners assume that the song's lyrics are ______ fact, the songwriter has said they were inspired by a short story she read as a teenager.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["autobiographical, in","autobiographical in","autobiographical; in","autobiographical: in,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two independent clauses are joined by a semicolon; "in fact" begins the second clause and is followed by a comma. A comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In a 2021 paper, Kenyan ______ Wanjiru Kamau described a method for detecting counterfeit malaria drugs using an inexpensive handheld spectrometer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["chemist,","chemist","chemist—","chemist:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Kenyan chemist" is a descriptive title placed directly before the name "Wanjiru Kamau." Such a title is restrictive and is not separated from the name by punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The surveyors reported ______ the eastern wall of the fortress had shifted nearly four inches since the previous inspection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","that,",", that","that:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"That the eastern wall... had shifted" is a noun clause serving as the direct object of "reported." No punctuation should separate a verb from its object or separate "that" from the clause it introduces.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The vaccine proved highly effective in clinical ______ it required storage at extremely low temperatures, which made distributing it to remote areas difficult.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["trials, nevertheless,","trials nevertheless","trials; nevertheless","trials; nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined, and "nevertheless" is a conjunctive adverb at the start of the second. A semicolon must come before it and a comma after it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bakery's sales doubled during the first year after it moved to a busier ______ the owners were able to hire four new employees and open a second location.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["street, consequently,","street consequently","street; consequently,","street, consequently;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Consequently" is a conjunctive adverb linking two independent clauses. A semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it; the other options produce comma splices or misplace the semicolon.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The sculpture appears to be carved from a single block of ______ it is assembled from more than forty separate pieces joined so precisely that the seams are invisible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["marble, in reality,","marble; in reality,","marble in reality,","marble; in reality"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence joins two independent clauses with the transitional phrase "in reality." A semicolon must separate the clauses, and "in reality" is followed by a comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tortoise had been listed as extinct for more than a century when a single female was discovered on a remote volcano in ______ researchers are now searching for a mate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["2019;","2019,","2019","2019, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence has two independent clauses ("The tortoise had been listed... in 2019" and "researchers are now searching for a mate"). They must be joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violinist who performed the solo at last night's ______ only nineteen years old, began studying music at the age of three.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["concert","concert;","concert—","concert,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Only nineteen years old" is a nonessential phrase describing the violinist; it is closed by a comma before the verb "began." It must be opened by a comma as well. A dash would not match, and a semicolon cannot separate the subject from its verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The town meeting ran past ______ residents had lined up to speak about the proposed highway, and the council refused to adjourn until every one of them had been heard.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["midnight, more than eighty","midnight more than eighty","midnight: more than eighty","midnight; and more than eighty"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the next two clauses explain why the meeting ran so late. A colon introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "; and" is an incorrect combination here.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers tested the paint samples for three heavy ______ cadmium, and mercury—all of which were once common in artists' pigments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["metals, lead,","metals—lead,","metals: lead,","metals; lead,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The list "lead, cadmium, and mercury" is closed by a dash before "all of which," so it must be opened by a dash. A colon or comma would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ancient city of Petra, carved directly into rose-colored sandstone ______ hidden from most of the world until a Swiss explorer reached it in 1812.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cliffs, remained","cliffs remained","cliffs; remained","cliffs—remained"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Carved directly into rose-colored sandstone cliffs" is a nonessential phrase that opens with a comma after "Petra"; it must close with a comma before the verb "remained."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study found no link between screen time and sleep ______ it did find that teenagers who used phones in bed fell asleep, on average, twenty minutes later than those who did not.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["quality, it","quality it","quality: it,","quality; it"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined. A semicolon is correct. A comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers at the observatory have spent years searching for the source of a mysterious radio ______ a burst of energy that repeats roughly every sixteen days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["signal;","signal. A","signal,","signal that is,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"A burst of energy that repeats roughly every sixteen days" is an appositive noun phrase renaming "signal," so it is set off with a comma. A semicolon or period would make the phrase a fragment.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The library's oldest ______ a fourteenth-century book of hours with gold-leaf illustrations—is displayed only one day each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["volume,","volume—","volume:","volume"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The appositive is closed by a dash before "is displayed," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The shipwreck lies in water so cold and so low in oxygen that the wood-boring organisms that destroy most wrecks cannot survive ______ the ship's hull, masts, and even its painted figurehead remain almost perfectly preserved.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["there; as a result,","there, as a result,","there as a result,","there; as a result"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined by the transitional phrase "as a result." A semicolon goes before the phrase and a comma after it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, sailors told stories of rogue ______ sudden walls of water more than twice the height of the surrounding swells—but scientists dismissed the accounts as exaggerations until one was recorded by an oil platform's sensors in 1995.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["waves,","waves:","waves;","waves—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "sudden walls of water... surrounding swells" is an appositive closed by a dash before "but." It must open with a matching dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineer explained that the turbine's blades are angled to capture wind from any ______ the hub rotates automatically to face whichever way the wind is blowing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["direction,","direction","direction:","direction, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The turbine's blades are angled to capture wind from any direction" is followed by a clause that explains how. A colon introduces the explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Although the two species look nearly ______ can be distinguished by their songs: one sings a rising trill, the other a series of short, falling notes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["identical; they","identical, they","identical. They","identical they"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Although the two species look nearly identical" is a dependent clause and must be joined to the main clause "they can be distinguished..." with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave the dependent clause as a fragment.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new ferry can carry up to four hundred ______ than twice as many as the boat it replaced.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["passengers, more","passengers; more","passengers. More","passengers: more,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"More than twice as many as the boat it replaced" is a phrase that modifies the preceding clause, so it attaches with a comma. A semicolon or period would turn it into a fragment.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The historian's account of the ______ on letters written by soldiers on both sides—offers a strikingly personal view of the conflict.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["war, based","war; based","war based","war—based"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "based on letters written by soldiers on both sides" is closed by a dash before the verb "offers," so it must open with a dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the volcano erupted in 1815, it ejected so much ash into the atmosphere that the following year became known in Europe and North America as the "Year Without a ______ failed as snow fell in June and frost struck in August.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Summer,\" crops","Summer\" crops","Summer\": crops","Summer,\" and, crops"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause explains why 1816 earned its name. A colon (placed outside the closing quotation mark) introduces this explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's narrator never appears on ______ voice, low and unhurried, guides viewers through three centuries of the island's history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["screen, her","screen; her","screen her","screen, and her,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The documentary's narrator never appears on screen" and "her voice... guides viewers" are both independent clauses, so a semicolon is needed. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the last option inserts a comma between the subject and its modifier.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Commissioned by a wealthy merchant in ______ the tapestry depicts a hunting scene in which every animal, from the stag to the smallest hare, is shown in astonishing detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1520,","1520;","1520:","1520"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Commissioned by a wealthy merchant in 1520" is an introductory participial phrase modifying "the tapestry." It ends with a comma before the main clause.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The pianist's hands are ______ she can span an interval of thirteen keys, nearly two full octaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["unusually large,","unusually large","unusually, large:","unusually large:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The pianist's hands are unusually large" is an independent clause, and the second clause illustrates just how large. A colon introduces the illustration. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma after "unusually" separates an adverb from the adjective it modifies.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers concluded that the ______ in the Atacama Desert, which receives almost no rainfall—survive by absorbing moisture from fog that rolls in from the Pacific.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lichens, which grow","lichens which grow","lichens—which grow","lichens; which grow"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which grow in the Atacama Desert, which receives almost no rainfall" is closed by a dash before the verb "survive." It must be opened by a dash. A comma would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The old mill's waterwheel still ______ it no longer powers the grindstones, it now generates electricity for the nearby visitor center.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["turns, although","turns; although","turns although","turns: although,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The old mill's waterwheel still turns" is an independent clause. The rest of the sentence ("although it no longer powers the grindstones, it now generates electricity...") is a second independent clause that begins with a dependent clause. A semicolon is needed between the two independent clauses; a comma would create a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have identified the oldest known ______ a 2.4-billion-year-old filament found in volcanic rock in South Africa.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["fungus-like fossil:","fungus-like fossil;","fungus-like fossil. A","fungus-like fossil, it is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause is followed by a noun phrase identifying the fossil. A colon introduces it. A semicolon or period would leave the noun phrase as a fragment, and "it is" after a comma creates a comma splice.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many early photographers coated their glass plates with ______ a sticky solution of gun cotton dissolved in ether—which had to be exposed and developed before it dried.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["collodion,","collodion:","collodion","collodion—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "a sticky solution of gun cotton dissolved in ether" is closed by a dash before "which," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The committee members agreed on the goal but not on the ______ some wanted to raise the fee immediately, while others preferred to phase it in over three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["method,","method","method:","method, so"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the following clause explains the disagreement about method. A colon introduces such an explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "so" signals a cause-and-effect relationship that doesn't exist.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Glass frogs, so named because the skin on their bellies is ______ can make themselves nearly invisible while sleeping by storing most of their red blood cells in their livers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["translucent","translucent,","translucent;","translucent—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "so named because the skin on their bellies is translucent" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "frogs." It must close with a comma before the verb "can make."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike its neighbors, the small nation has no standing ______ it relies on a treaty with a larger ally for its defense.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["army; instead,","army, instead,","army instead,","army; instead"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined with the conjunctive adverb "instead." A semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The authors of the report, a team of economists from three ______ that the tax credit lifted roughly 400,000 children out of poverty in its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["universities estimate","universities; estimate","universities—estimate","universities, estimate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "a team of economists from three universities" begins with a comma after "report" and must close with a comma before the verb "estimate."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To make the traditional ______ cooks wrap the seasoned meat in banana leaves and bury it in a pit lined with hot stones for most of a day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dish;","dish:","dish,","dish"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"To make the traditional dish" is an introductory infinitive phrase. It is followed by a comma before the main clause "cooks wrap...."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's plan has two main ______ to reduce traffic in the city center and to raise money for public transit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["goals;","goals:","goals, these are","goals, they are:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause introduces the goals, which follow in a list. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and "these are" after a comma creates a comma splice; the last option also places a colon after a verb.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The beetle's shell reflects only circularly polarized ______ property that has inspired engineers working on new kinds of camera filters and display screens.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["light, a","light; a","light. A","light a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A property that has inspired engineers..." is a noun phrase that sums up the preceding clause. It is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would make it a fragment, and no punctuation runs it into "light."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The author's memoir describes her childhood in a farming village in northern ______ and her later career as a physician in Toronto.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Portugal; her emigration to Canada at sixteen;","Portugal: her emigration to Canada at sixteen,","Portugal, her emigration to Canada at sixteen;","Portugal, her emigration to Canada at sixteen,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence lists three things the memoir describes: her childhood, her emigration, and her career. Because none of the items contains internal commas, commas are used to separate them. Semicolons are unnecessary, and a colon would wrongly introduce the second item as an explanation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The fossilized footprints show that the dinosaur walked on its hind legs ______ the shallow impressions of its front feet appear only where it seems to have slipped in the mud.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most of the time,","most of the time","most of the time;","most, of the time;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The fossilized footprints show... most of the time" and "the shallow impressions... appear only where..." are independent clauses. A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The term "______ came into wide use in the 1970s to describe the practice of planting a mix of trees, shrubs, and crops on the same plot of land.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["agroforestry,\"","agroforestry\"","agroforestry\",","agroforestry\":"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The term 'agroforestry'" is the subject, and "came" is its verb. No punctuation should separate them, so the closing quotation mark is followed directly by the verb. The word inside the quotation marks is a restrictive appositive for "term."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many scholars now believe that the epic was not the work of a single ______ was assembled over several centuries from the songs of many different storytellers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["poet but","poet, but,","poet; but","poet. But"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The epic was not the work of a single poet but was assembled..." is a single clause with a compound predicate built on "not... but." No punctuation is needed before "but," and a comma after "but" or a semicolon or period before it would break the construction.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ship's captain kept two ______ official one, which recorded the weather and the ship's position each day, and a private journal filled with sketches of the birds and fish he observed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["logs; an","logs, an,","logs an","logs: an"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause "The ship's captain kept two logs" introduces a description of the two logs. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce such a list, and the other choices misplace or omit punctuation.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historians have long debated whether the treaty ended the war or merely paused ______ question may finally be answered by a cache of diplomatic letters discovered last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it, the","it the","it; the","it: the,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Historians have long debated whether... merely paused it" and "the question may finally be answered..." are independent clauses, so a semicolon joins them.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The chef sources her ingredients from within fifty miles of the restaurant, a rule she breaks for only one ______ she imports from a small family farm in Sri Lanka.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["item; cinnamon, which","item: cinnamon, which","item, cinnamon which","item: cinnamon which,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The clause "a rule she breaks for only one item" is complete, and "cinnamon" identifies the item, so a colon introduces it. The relative clause "which she imports..." is nonessential and is set off with a comma after "cinnamon."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The collection of letters, diaries, and photographs that the historian assembled over three decades of research in archives across four continents ______ now housed at the university library.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun "collection"; "letters, diaries, and photographs" and the long relative clause that follows are part of a modifier. A singular, present-tense verb ("is") is needed to agree with "collection" and with "now."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Among the most surprising findings of the decade-long survey of the Amazon basin ______ that several bird species thought to depend on undisturbed forest were thriving in patches of regrowth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were the discovery","were the discoveries,","have been the discovery","was the discovery"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject, "the discovery," comes after the verb. Because "discovery" is singular, the verb must be "was." The plural verbs agree with "findings," which is the object of the preposition "among," not the subject.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Having spent nearly a decade translating the epic from Old Norse, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the news that a rival translation would be published the same month dismayed the scholar.","a rival translation, the scholar learned, would be published the same month.","the scholar was dismayed to learn that a rival translation would be published the same month.","it was dismaying for the scholar to learn that a rival translation would be published the same month."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "Having spent nearly a decade translating the epic" must be followed immediately by the person who did the translating. Only the first choice places "the scholar" right after the phrase. The others make it seem that the news, the rival translation, or "it" spent a decade translating.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the lead researcher nor her two assistants ______ able to explain why the samples stored in the second freezer had degraded so much faster than the others.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject. The nearer subject is "her two assistants," which is plural, so the verb must be "were." The past tense also matches "had degraded."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the first European ships reached the island in 1722, its inhabitants ______ hundreds of enormous stone statues, many of which stood on platforms along the coast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already carved","already carve","have already carved","will have already carved"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The carving happened before another past event (the ships' arrival in 1722). The past perfect, "had already carved," expresses an action completed before a past moment. The present and present perfect are inconsistent with 1722, and the future perfect is illogical.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The city's two largest employers, a hospital network and a regional airline, have both announced plans to expand, and economists expect ______ combined hiring to cut the local unemployment rate by almost a full percentage point.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","they're","there","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to the two employers, a plural antecedent, and shows possession of "combined hiring." The plural possessive "their" is correct. "Its" is singular, "they're" means "they are," and "there" indicates a place.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The number of species known to science ______ grown dramatically over the past century, but biologists estimate that most species on Earth have still never been formally described.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","has","were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The number of species" is singular because the subject is "number" ("of species" is a prepositional phrase). The singular verb "has" is correct. Note that "a number of" would take a plural verb, but "the number of" takes a singular one.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Originally designed to measure the brightness of distant stars, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["climate scientists later used the instrument to track changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice.","the instrument was later used by climate scientists to track changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice.","changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice were later tracked with the instrument by climate scientists.","the reflectivity of Arctic ice was later tracked by climate scientists using the instrument."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The modifier "Originally designed to measure the brightness of distant stars" describes the instrument, so "the instrument" must come immediately after the comma. The other choices make it sound as though climate scientists, changes, or reflectivity were designed to measure stars.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each spring, a team of volunteers from the nearby university ______ the river's banks for invasive plants and records the location of every patch they remove.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["surveys","survey","have surveyed","are surveying"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun "team" (the phrase "of volunteers from the nearby university" is a modifier). The verb must be singular and present tense to match the other verb in the sentence, "records." "Surveys" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The painter's early work drew heavily on the folk traditions of her native Oaxaca, but by the 1960s she ______ to experiment with abstraction, layering bands of color that only faintly suggested landscapes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has begun","begins","will begin","had begun"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence describes the past ("by the 1960s"), and "by" signals an action completed before a past reference point. "Had begun" is correct; "has begun" and "begins" are inconsistent with the past time frame, and "will begin" is illogical.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Several of the documents that the archivist recovered from the flooded basement ______ written in a shorthand that no one at the library could read.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "Several," which is plural; the phrase "of the documents that the archivist recovered..." is a modifier. The plural, past-tense "were" agrees with the subject and matches the past-tense "recovered."$q$),
  ($q$conv-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$One of the oldest surviving maps of the Pacific, which show islands that European navigators had not yet reached, ______ to have been drawn with the help of a Tahitian navigator named Tupaia.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are believed","is believed","were believed","believe"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "One," which is singular; "of the oldest surviving maps of the Pacific" is a modifier, and the relative clause is set off by commas. The verb must be singular, so "is believed" is correct.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In 1919, the astronomer's calculations, which built on observations collected by dozens of earlier ______ that the Milky Way was far larger than most of her contemporaries believed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["astronomers, suggested","astronomers, suggests","astronomers' suggested","astronomer's suggested"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject "calculations" needs a past-tense verb (the sentence is set in 1919), and the nonessential relative clause must be closed with a comma before that verb. "Astronomers, suggested" is correct. "Suggests" is in the wrong tense, and the possessive forms are incorrect because nothing is being possessed.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The tradition of carving masks for the winter festival, passed down through generations of families in the mountain villages, ______ nearly vanished in the twentieth century before a revival in the 1980s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","were","are","had"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "tradition," which is singular. "Had nearly vanished" agrees with it and correctly places the near-disappearance before the 1980s revival. The plural verbs agree with "generations" or "families," which are inside modifying phrases.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The researchers asked participants to rate how confident they were in each answer. Surprisingly, those who scored lowest on the test tended to rate ______ confidence the highest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","there","their","its"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun refers to "those who scored lowest," a plural group, and it indicates possession of "confidence." "Their" is the plural possessive pronoun.$q$),
  ($q$conv-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Walking through the reconstructed village, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the methods residents used to spin wool, cure fish, and repair boats can be seen.","visitors can see how residents spun wool, cured fish, and repaired boats more than a thousand years ago.","a thousand-year-old way of life, including spinning wool, curing fish, and repairing boats, is visible.","it is possible to see how residents spun wool, cured fish, and repaired boats."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The opening phrase "Walking through the reconstructed village" must describe the subject that immediately follows it. Only "visitors" can walk through the village.$q$)
